window.EPICS = {"datings":[{"id":"mb-traditional-kali-3138-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Traditional Kali-epoch school (Puranas and Siddhantic astronomers; modern advocates incl. Prabhupada/ISKCON, Kota Venkatachalam, Bhagwad Datta, P.V. Holay, Arun Upadhyay, Ravi Prakash Arya)","proposerInfo":"Composite orthodox tradition: Puranic chroniclers, Indian astronomers (Aryabhata, Brahmagupta), Al-Biruni as reporter, and modern traditionalist and Arya Samaj-linked chronologists","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war, placed 36 years before the start of Kali Yuga","dateLabel":"c. 3138 BCE (variants 3137-3139 BCE); Kali epoch 18 Feb 3102 BCE plus 36 years","year":-3138,"yearLow":-3139,"yearHigh":-3137,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Aryabhatiya (499 CE) for the Kali epoch only; Mahabharata Mausala Parva and Vishnu/Bhagavata Puranas for the 36-year link; modern restatements by Kota Venkatachalam, Bhagwad Datta, Arun Upadhyay and others","workYear":499,"summary":"The orthodox view takes Kali Yuga as beginning at midnight 17/18 Feb 3102 BCE, when Krishna departed. The Mahabharata (Mausala Parva) places the Yadava destruction and Krishna's death in the 36th year after the war, so the war falls about 3138 BCE (3137-3139 depending on inclusive counting). Aryabhata's role is limited: in Kalakriyapada 10 he says that 3600 years of Kali had elapsed when he was 23 (499 CE), which fixes the 3102 BCE epoch. He does not date the war, which is a later inference from Puranic synchronisms. Brahmagupta and other siddhantic astronomers used the same Kali epoch, and Al-Biruni reported it.","keyEvidence":["Aryabhatiya Kalakriyapada 10: 3600 Kali years elapsed in Aryabhata's 23rd year, which gives 499 CE and an epoch of 3102 BCE. It states no war date.","Mahabharata Mausala Parva 1: omens appear in the 36th year after the war and the Yadavas perish; this is the usual source of the 36-year gap.","Vishnu and Bhagavata Puranas: Kali begins when Krishna leaves the earth, so the war is dated 36 years earlier.","Aihole inscription (634 CE) counts 3735 years from the Bharata war, which corresponds to about 3101 BCE and conflates the war with the Kali epoch. This shows the war-Kali link was not always a 36-year one."],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists","Mahabharata Mausala Parva","Aryabhata (Kali epoch)","Brahmagupta","Al-Biruni"],"critiques":["The 3102 BCE epoch is a retrospective construct computed by astronomers, with a mean-planet conjunction at the start. It is not an independent record of the war.","Aryabhata gives no war date. Varahamihira (Brihat Samhita 13.3) and Kalhana place the war about 2449 BCE, so the tradition itself disagrees.","Archaeology does not support the date: the Painted Grey Ware horizon is c. 1200-600 BCE, and iron use, horses and the urban Kuru-Panchala setting do not fit the fourth millennium BCE.","The 36-year gap comes from the epic's narrative and is not an astronomical observation. Counting variants explain the 3137/3138/3139 spread.","Puranic genealogies are long, compressed and inconsistent, and the epic text itself was composed and revised over centuries."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Aryabhatiya, Kalakriyapada 10 (499 CE)","Mahabharata, Mausala Parva 1","Vishnu Purana 4.24; Bhagavata Purana 12.2","Aihole inscription of Pulakeshin II (634 CE)","Varahamihira, Brihat Samhita 13.3","Kalhana, Rajatarangini 1.48-56","Al-Biruni, Kitab al-Hind"]},{"id":"mb-aihole-inscription-634-ce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Ravikirti (court poet of Chalukya king Pulakeshin II)","proposerInfo":"Jain poet who composed the Aihole prashasti (Meguti Jain temple inscription), Badami Chalukya court, Karnataka, 7th century CE","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war, dated by years elapsed and implicitly tied to the Kali Yuga epoch","dateLabel":"c. 3102/3101 BCE (3735 years before Saka 556 = 634/635 CE)","year":-3101,"yearLow":-3102,"yearHigh":-3101,"method":"Era / epigraphic reckoning","work":"Aihole inscription (Meguti temple, Aihole), dated Saka 556 (634 CE)","workYear":634,"summary":"The Aihole prashasti of Pulakeshin II is the earliest securely dated epigraphic reference to the Bharata war. It says 3735 years had passed since the Bharata war, and that 556 years of the Saka kings had elapsed. Subtracting 3735 from 634 CE gives about 3101 BCE (3102 BCE in historical counting with no year zero), which is the traditional Kali Yuga epoch. So by the 7th century CE the war was already reckoned to coincide with the start of Kali. The poet is citing a received tradition, not an independent calculation.","keyEvidence":["Verse as commonly cited (from memory, not re-checked against a text): 'trimsatsu tri-sahasresu bharatad ahavad itah / saptabda-sata-yukteshu sateshv abdeshu pancasu / pancasatsu kalau kale shatsu pancasatasu ca / samasu samatitasu sakanam api bhubhujam'","Arithmetic: 3000 + 30 + 700 + 5 = 3735 years since the war; Saka 556 = 500 + 50 + 6","Conversion: Saka year + 78 = CE, so 556 + 78 = 634 CE; 3735 - 634 = 3101, which is 3102 BCE in no-zero counting, matching the Kali epoch","The verse mentions 'kalau kale' (in the Kali age), which supports reading it as a Kali-era reckoning"],"buildsOn":["Puranic Kali-epoch tradition","Aryabhata (Kali 3600 = 499 CE)","Varahamihira (Yudhishthira era 2526 years before Saka, a competing tradition)"],"critiques":["It reflects a belief current by 634 CE, not independent evidence; the Kali epoch was a siddhantic back-calculation, not an observed date","The war is identified with the Kali start only by convention; other traditions (Varahamihira, Kalhana's Rajatarangini) place the war about 653 Kali years later, near 2449 BCE","Archaeology (PGW/Hastinapura horizon, c. 1200-800 BCE) and Vedic chronology do not support a 3100 BCE war","Scholars treat the inscription as a terminus ante quem for the epic and its traditional date, not as proof of the war's date"],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Ravikirti, Aihole (Meguti) inscription of Pulakeshin II, Saka 556 / 634 CE, Epigraphia Indica vol. 6 (ed. F. Kielhorn / J. F. Fleet)","Wikipedia: Aihole inscription of Pulakeshin II","Wikipedia: Kali Yuga"]},{"id":"mb-varahamihira-vriddha-garga-2449","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Varahamihira (citing Vriddha Garga); supported by Kalhana, Bhattotpala, P.C. Sengupta","proposerInfo":"Varahamihira: 6th-century CE Indian astronomer-astrologer of Ujjain; Sengupta: 20th-century Indian historian of astronomy (Calcutta)","event":"Reign of Yudhishthira / Mahabharata war era (Saptarshi in Magha)","dateLabel":"c. 2449/2448 BCE (Yudhishthira era = Saka era + 2526; Kali year ~653)","year":-2449,"yearLow":-2449,"yearHigh":-2448,"method":"Era / epigraphic reckoning","work":"Brihat Samhita of Varahamihira, ch. 13 (Saptarshi-chara), verses 3-4, c. 550 CE; interpreted by Bhattotpala (966 CE commentary); P.C. Sengupta, Ancient Indian Chronology (1947)","workYear":550,"summary":"Varahamihira, quoting the older authority Vriddha Garga, says the Seven Sages (Saptarshi) were in Magha while Yudhishthira ruled, and that the Saka era began 2526 years after that reign (BS 13.3). Verse 13.4 adds that the sages stay 100 years in each nakshatra. Counting 2526 years back from Saka 0 (78 CE) gives about 2449 or 2448 BCE depending on whether a year zero is used. Bhattotpala explains the verse as meaning Yudhishthira's era ended 2526 years before Saka. Kalhana's Rajatarangini (12th c.) agrees, placing the war about Kali 653, and Sengupta defended this as the best-attested ancient tradition against the 3102 BCE Kali start. The wording and the 2526 figure are well attested in the manuscript tradition, but the Saka conversion carries a one-year ambiguity.","keyEvidence":["Brihat Samhita 13.3: Saptarshi were in Magha under Yudhishthira; Saka era = that era + 2526 years","BS 13.4: the sages stay 100 years in each nakshatra (the 2700-year Saptarshi cycle, the basis of the Laukika/Kashmir reckoning)","Saka 0 = 78 CE, so 2526 years earlier is 2448 BCE (historical) or 2449 BCE (astronomical numbering)","Bhattotpala's commentary reading the 2526-year interval","Kalhana's Rajatarangini: Bharata war about Kali 653, matching about 2449 BCE","Sengupta's 1947 defence of this tradition"],"buildsOn":["Vriddha Garga (Garga Samhita)","Parashara"],"critiques":["Contradicts the Aryabhata and Kali-epoch tradition of 3102 BCE, which Varahamihira's own era conflicts with","The Saptarshi nakshatra-stay model is a theoretical convention. The real sages (Ursa Major stars) do not move by neat 100-year steps","The 'Magha' position at Yudhishthira's reign is a textual statement with no independent observational check","Interpretation of 'ruled' (start versus end of the reign) shifts the date by decades","The date sits in the Early Bronze Age and conflicts with the usual archaeological reading of the Painted Grey Ware horizon (c. 1200-600 BCE)","Later Saptarshi-era reckonings (Laukika, Kashmir) are known to rest on convention and not on observation"],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Varahamihira, Brihat Samhita, ch. 13 (Saptarshi-charaadhyaya), with Bhattotpala's commentary","P.C. Sengupta, Ancient Indian Chronology, University of Calcutta, 1947","Kalhana, Rajatarangini, I.48-56","Wikipedia: Saptarshi; Kali Yuga; Mahabharata dating"]},{"id":"mb-kalhana-kali-653-2449","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Kalhana (Kashmiri chronicler); critique by Sir Marc Aurel Stein","proposerInfo":"Kalhana: 12th-century Kashmiri poet-historian, author of the Rajatarangini. Stein: Hungarian-British archaeologist and Sanskritist who edited (1892) and translated (1900) the work.","event":"Kurukshetra (Kuru-Pandava) war, anchored to Kali year 653 and to Gonanda I of Kashmir as a contemporary","dateLabel":"Kali 653 elapsed, about 2449/2448 BCE (Kali epoch 3102 BCE minus 653); text written c. 1148-49 CE","year":-2449,"yearLow":-2449,"yearHigh":-2448,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Rajatarangini (c. 1148-49 CE); Stein, Kalhana's Rajatarangini: A Chronicle of the Kings of Kashmir (1900)","workYear":1148,"summary":"Kalhana opens his Kashmir chronicle by stating that the Kauravas and Pandavas lived when 653 years of the Kali age had elapsed (Rajatarangini 1.48). That is about 2449 BCE on the 3102 BCE Kali epoch. He treats Gonanda I, the first Kashmir king, as a contemporary of the war, and builds his king-list from there. He says he is correcting earlier chroniclers and compares the Varahamihira (Brihat Samhita) tradition that places Yudhishthira 2526 years before the Shaka era, about 2448 BCE. Stein's 1900 translation and introduction showed that the early chronology is a reconstruction, not a record. He rejected the pre-Karkota regnal lengths as unhistorical and treated the Gonanda dynasty dating as artificial.","keyEvidence":["Rajatarangini 1.48: the Kurus and Pandavas lived when 653 years of the Kali age had elapsed (paraphrase from memory, not checked against the Sanskrit)","Gonanda I is made a contemporary of the war and an ally of Jarasandha in the early king-list","Alignment with the Varahamihira reckoning (Saptarshi in Magha at Yudhishthira's time, 2526 years before Shaka), about 2448 BCE","Stein's finding that the long early regnal lengths and the pre-Karkota list lack historical support"],"buildsOn":["Varahamihira (Brihat Samhita)","Puranic Kali epoch of 3102 BCE","Earlier Kashmir chroniclers such as Helaraja and Ksemendra, cited by Kalhana","Nilamata Purana"],"critiques":["Aurel Stein (1900) held that the pre-Karkota chronology is artificial and unreliable, and that Kalhana's early dates do not rest on contemporary records","The Kali 653 anchor is a calculated or traditional epoch figure, not an observed astronomical datum","Archaeology does not support a 2449 BCE war: Painted Grey Ware sites such as Hastinapura date to roughly 1100-800 BCE, and Kashmir has no known Bronze Age dynasty list","Later scholars (e.g. Pargiter, Bühler) found the early Rajatarangini list heavily padded"],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Kalhana, Rajatarangini 1.48-56","M. A. Stein, Kalhana's Rajatarangini: A Chronicle of the Kings of Kashmir, 2 vols, Westminster: Constable, 1900","Varahamihira, Brihat Samhita 13.3-4 (Saptarshi cycle)"]},{"id":"mb-puranic-interval-1015-1050","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Puranic tradition (Vishnu, Matsya, Vayu, Brahmanda and Bhagavata king-lists), as computed by 19th-century Indologists such as H. H. Wilson and Alexander Cunningham; contested by F. E. Pargiter","proposerInfo":"Not a single proposer. The interval comes from Puranic texts, and the 19th-century computations are by Orientalists and archaeologists (Wilson, Cunningham). Pargiter was an Indian Civil Service judge and Indologist.","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war, dated by the interval from Parikshit's birth to the coronation of Mahapadma Nanda","dateLabel":"c. 1400-1430 BCE (Parikshit-to-Nanda interval of 1015 or 1050 years, added to a Nanda accession of about 380-400 BCE)","year":-1415,"yearLow":-1430,"yearHigh":-1400,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Vishnu Purana IV.24 and parallel passages in the Matsya, Vayu, Brahmanda and Bhagavata (XII.2) Puranas. Modern treatment: F. E. Pargiter, The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age (1913) and Ancient Indian Historical Tradition (1922).","workYear":1913,"summary":"The Puranas give a Pariksit-to-Nanda interval in their prophetic Kali-age sections. The Vishnu and Bhagavata Puranas are usually cited for about 1015 years, and the Matsya and Vayu for about 1050, with variant readings in some manuscripts. Adding this to a Mahapadma Nanda accession of roughly 380-400 BCE puts Parikshit's birth, taken as just after the war, at about 1400-1430 BCE. This short Puranic chronology was favoured by Wilson and Cunningham and by many traditional historians. Pargiter examined the manuscript variants and dynastic lists, treated the figures as unreliable, and preferred a war date near 950 BCE from generation counts. The figures and attributions here come from background knowledge and were not checked against the sources in this session.","keyEvidence":["Puranic statement that the span from Parikshit's birth to Mahapadma Nanda's coronation is about 1015 years (Vishnu, Bhagavata) or 1050 years (Matsya, Vayu), with manuscript variants","Mahapadma Nanda accession fixed at about 380-400 BCE by Buddhist and Jain synchronisms and the Alexander-era Greek record","Pargiter's analysis of the Kali-age dynasty lists and his preference for a generation-based date near 950 BCE"],"buildsOn":["Vishnu Purana","Matsya Purana","Vayu Purana","Bhagavata Purana","H. H. Wilson","Alexander Cunningham","F. E. Pargiter"],"critiques":["The interval figures differ between Puranas and manuscripts, and the text may be corrupt or schematic, which is Pargiter's concern.","Kali-age sections are prophetic recensions compiled centuries after the events, with later editing.","Generation counts give a shorter span, near 950 BCE, and the 1015 or 1050 years would need implausibly long reigns.","Archaeology places the Painted Grey Ware and Kuru-region settlements at about 1200-600 BCE, so a war around 1400 BCE has no clear material correlate."],"plausibility":4,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Vishnu Purana IV.24 (Wilson translation)","F. E. Pargiter, The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age, 1913","F. E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, 1922","Bhagavata Purana XII.2"]},{"id":"mb-matsya-purana-1500-interval","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Reading of the Matsya Purana (with some Vayu readings) by Puranic chronologists; no single modern proposer confirmed","proposerInfo":"Puranic dynastic-list tradition; the numeric variants are discussed in Pargiter's work, and the specific advocate of the 1500-year reading is not verified","event":"Kurukshetra war (dated via the Parikshit-to-Nanda interval)","dateLabel":"c. 1880-1900 BCE (implied by adding ~1500 years to Mahapadma Nanda's accession, c. 380-400 BCE)","year":-1890,"yearLow":-1900,"yearHigh":-1880,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Matsya Purana, Kali-age dynasty chapters (c. ch. 271), with the Vayu Purana parallel; compared in F.E. Pargiter, The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age (1913)","workYear":1913,"summary":"The Puranic dynastic passages give a span from Parikshit's birth (shortly after the war) to the coronation of Mahapadma Nanda. Most texts (Vishnu, Bhagavata, and many Vayu and Matsya manuscripts) give about 1015 or 1050 years. A variant reading of about 1500 years, attributed to some Matsya and Vayu manuscripts, is the basis of this proposal. Added to a Nanda accession of c. 380-400 BCE, it puts the war at about 1880-1900 BCE. The date is derived from a chosen manuscript reading and a Nanda anchor, not from any independent observation. The 1500 figure is usually treated as a variant or scribal change, and I could not check the manuscript readings online, so this entry rests on background knowledge.","keyEvidence":["Verse giving the interval from Parikshit to Mahapadma Nanda, with numeric variants (1015, 1050, 1500) across Puranas and manuscripts","Anchor: Mahapadma Nanda's accession, c. 400-380 BCE, from Buddhist/Jain/Greek synchronisms with Chandragupta Maurya c. 320 BCE","Puranic king-lists from Parikshit onward, used as a cross-check on the interval length"],"buildsOn":["F.E. Pargiter","Vishnu Purana and Bhagavata Purana interval statements"],"critiques":["The 1500 figure is a minority manuscript variant, and the majority reading of 1015 or 1050 gives a much later war.","King-lists of about 25 generations do not fill 1500 years at normal reign lengths.","It depends on the Nanda anchor and on the Puranic lists being complete and continuous.","No archaeological layer at the Mahabharata sites supports a c. 1900 BCE date. Painted Grey Ware, the usual comparison, dates to roughly 1200-400 BCE."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["F.E. Pargiter, The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age (Oxford, 1913)","Matsya Purana, chapter 271","Vayu Purana, dynastic chapters (99)","Vishnu Purana IV.24"]},{"id":"mb-pargiter-950-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"F.E. Pargiter (endorsed or echoed by H.C. Raychaudhuri, P.L. Bhargava, A.L. Basham)","proposerInfo":"Frederick Eden Pargiter (1852-1927), British Indian Civil Service judge and Indologist, Puranic scholar. Raychaudhuri, Bhargava and Basham were historians of ancient India.","event":"Kurukshetra / Bharata war","dateLabel":"c. 950 BCE (10th century BCE)","year":-950,"yearLow":-1000,"yearHigh":-900,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Ancient Indian Historical Tradition (Oxford University Press, 1922)","workYear":1922,"summary":"Pargiter collated the dynastic lists in the Puranas and treated them as a genuine historical tradition. He counted generations and reigns from the Bharata war forward to the Magadha dynasties and the Nandas and Mauryas (c. 320 BCE). Using an average generation or reign length, he put the war at about 950 BCE. Raychaudhuri (Political History of Ancient India, 1923) gave a similar figure, with Parikshit's accession around 900 BCE and the war around 950 BCE. Bhargava and Basham accepted roughly the same range. It fits the late Vedic Kuru state and the Painted Grey Ware horizon. I did not check these attributions against the sources with web searches. The attributions to Pargiter and Raychaudhuri are secure from background knowledge, but the exact wording and page references for Bhargava and Basham need checking.","keyEvidence":["Puranic lists (Vayu, Matsya, Vishnu, Bhagavata) of Paurava/Kuru kings from Parikshit to Udayana, and of Magadha kings from Sahadeva to the Nandas","Back-reckoning from the Mauryan anchor (Chandragupta c. 320 BCE, fixed by the Alexander synchronism) by an average generation length","Pargiter's claim that the Kshatriya (Puranic) tradition is older and more reliable than the Brahmanical one"],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists","The Chandragupta Maurya - Alexander synchronism (c. 320 BCE)","Pargiter's earlier Dynasties of the Kali Age (1913)"],"critiques":["Generation-length averages are arbitrary, and the Puranic lists are incomplete or may count contemporaneous lines as successive.","The Puranic lists were compiled centuries after the war.","The Puranas themselves tie the war to the Kali era (3102 BCE).","Pargiter's separation of a Kshatriya tradition from a Brahmanical one is contested.","The historicity and date of the war cannot be anchored by archaeology. Hastinapura and PGW evidence is only broadly consistent with this date."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["F.E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, Oxford University Press, 1922","F.E. Pargiter, The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age, 1913","H.C. Raychaudhuri, Political History of Ancient India, University of Calcutta, 1923","P.L. Bhargava, India in the Vedic Age, 1956","A.L. Basham, The Wonder That Was India, 1954"]},{"id":"mb-colebrooke-wilson-1400-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"H.T. Colebrooke (astronomical datum), H.H. Wilson (adoption); Elphinstone, Lassen and R.C. Dutt in the same ~1400 BCE cluster","proposerInfo":"Henry Thomas Colebrooke (1765-1837), East India Company official and pioneer Sanskritist; Horace Hayman Wilson (1786-1860), Oxford Boden Professor of Sanskrit.","event":"Kurukshetra war (Mahabharata war), inferred indirectly from a Vedic-era astronomical anchor","dateLabel":"c. 1400 BCE (Colebrooke's colure computation gave 1391 BCE; Wilson and others rounded to about 1400 BCE)","year":-1400,"yearLow":-1400,"yearHigh":-1391,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Colebrooke, \"On the Vedas, or Sacred Writings of the Hindus\" (Asiatic Researches vol. 8, 1805); Wilson, preface and notes to The Vishnu Purana (1840)","workYear":1805,"summary":"Colebrooke used the Vedanga Jyotisha, which places the winter solstice at the start of Dhanishtha and the summer solstice at the middle of Ashlesha. He calculated that this colure position corresponds to about 1391 BCE, often rounded to 1400 BCE. He used it to date the Vedic calendar texts, not the war itself. Wilson and other early Indologists then placed the Bharata war near this period, using Puranic genealogies and the idea that the war fell close to the Vedic age. It is an indirect anchor-based date, not one computed from sky observations in the epic.","keyEvidence":["Vedanga Jyotisha (Lagadha) places the winter solstice at the start of Dhanishtha and the summer solstice at the middle of Ashlesha","Precession arithmetic converts that colure position to about 1391 BCE","Puranic genealogies used to place the war relative to the Vedic era"],"buildsOn":["Vedanga Jyotisha of Lagadha","Puranic king-lists","William Jones and the Asiatic Society tradition"],"critiques":["The Vedanga Jyotisha data are crude (half-nakshatra resolution), so the computed date carries an error of centuries; modern estimates for the text range about 1200-700 BCE","The colure date applies to the Vedanga Jyotisha, not to the war, so the step to the war is an assumption","Attribution caveat: Tilak's Vedanga Jyotisha date is closer to 1181-1121 BCE and he did not date the war at 1400 BCE; Pusalker's war date is about 950 BCE (following Pargiter). Both are loosely lumped into this cluster and should be checked","Archaeology (Painted Grey Ware, Kuru state c. 1200-600 BCE) fits a later date than 1400 BCE"],"plausibility":4,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["H.T. Colebrooke, On the Vedas, Asiatic Researches vol. 8 (1805)","H.H. Wilson, The Vishnu Purana (1840)","M. Elphinstone, History of India (1841)","R.C. Dutt, A History of Civilization in Ancient India (1889-90)","F.E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition (1922)"]},{"id":"mb-bentley-575-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"John Bentley","proposerInfo":"British East India Company officer and amateur Indian astronomer/chronologist (1750-1824), based in Bengal","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war","dateLabel":"c. 575 BCE (reported for Historical View, 1823); c. 1424 BCE reported from his earlier 1790s papers. Which figure belongs to which work is unconfirmed.","year":-575,"yearLow":-1424,"yearHigh":-575,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"A Historical View of the Hindu Astronomy (1823); earlier papers in Asiatic Researches (vol. 5, 1799)","workYear":1823,"summary":"Bentley recalculated Indian astronomical data from the Siddhantas and argued that Indian era dates such as the Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE were back-computed by later astronomers and were not real observations. He also argued that the Surya Siddhanta is medieval, around 1068 CE. He tied the Bharata war to old astronomical data such as colure and nakshatra positions in texts attributed to Garga and Parashara. The c. 1424 BCE figure is usually attributed to his earlier papers and the c. 575 BCE figure to the 1823 book. I could not retrieve the primary texts, so the split between the two works is not confirmed. His revisionist chronology was heavily criticised in his own time.","keyEvidence":["Argued that the Kali Yuga epoch (3102 BCE) was retro-calculated and not observed","Used colure and nakshatra position statements in Vedanga-era astronomical texts as dating anchors","Dated the Surya Siddhanta to about 1068 CE, which undermines the traditional chronology"],"buildsOn":["William Jones","Jean-Sylvain Bailly","Puranic king-lists"],"critiques":["John Playfair and H. T. Colebrooke rejected his late dating of the Siddhantas.","A date near 575 BCE puts the war close to the Buddha's lifetime, but Buddhist and Puranic sources treat the war as well before it.","Later genealogical scholarship (Pargiter) puts the war at c. 950 BCE.","His astronomical reconstructions are considered unreliable by modern historians of astronomy, and he is often said to have had a motive to fit Indian chronology to a biblical one."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["John Bentley, A Historical View of the Hindu Astronomy (London, 1823)","John Bentley, Asiatic Researches vol. 5 (1799)","Wikipedia: Dating the Mahabharata"]},{"id":"mb-bankim-chandra-1530-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Bankim Chandra Chatterjee (Chattopadhyay)","proposerInfo":"19th-century Bengali novelist, essayist and Deputy Magistrate (1838-1894); author of Anandamath and Vande Mataram; also wrote Hindu-revival critical essays.","event":"Kurukshetra war (Mahabharata war)","dateLabel":"c. 1530 BCE (as relayed; some secondary lists cite c. 1430 BCE)","year":-1530,"yearLow":-1530,"yearHigh":-1430,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Krishnacharitra (Bengali, 1886)","workYear":1886,"summary":"In Krishnacharitra, a critical biography of Krishna, Bankim treated the historical Krishna as distinct from later mythic accretions and tried to place the Kurukshetra war in time. He rejected the Kali-era 3102 BCE tradition as unsupported by the epic itself and used indirect astronomical and chronological arguments (Vedanga-era astronomy, king-lists) to place the war in the mid-second millennium BCE. I could not confirm the exact figure (1530 vs 1430 BCE) from a primary text in this session. The book is better known as a rationalist critique of the epic's layers than as a precise astronomical computation.","keyEvidence":["Argues the Mahabharata has an older historical core plus later interpolations, so only the core can date the war.","Rejects the Kali-era 3102 BCE date as not derived from the epic.","Uses indirect astronomical and king-list reasoning rather than a specific planetary configuration."],"buildsOn":["Western Indology debates of the 19th century","Vedanga Jyotisha-based reasoning","Puranic king-lists"],"critiques":["Method is indirect; no fixed planetary configuration is computed.","Exact figure is reported inconsistently in secondary literature (1530 vs 1430 BCE).","Pre-dates modern archaeology and Painted Grey Ware chronology, so lacks that cross-check.","Rejects 3102 BCE without a fully independent astronomical test."],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Bankim Chandra Chatterjee, Krishnacharitra, 1886","Wikipedia: Kurukshetra War (dating section)"]},{"id":"mb-william-jones-wilford-1370-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Sir William Jones / Francis Wilford (attribution uncertain)","proposerInfo":"Jones: Calcutta judge, orientalist, founder of the Asiatic Society (1784). Wilford: East India Company army engineer and Sanskritist working with Calcutta pandits.","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war","dateLabel":"c. 1370 BCE (figure as stated in the task; exact number and which author gave it not independently confirmed)","year":-1370,"yearLow":-1400,"yearHigh":-1350,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Asiatic Researches (Calcutta): Jones, \"On the Chronology of the Hindus\" (vol. 2, 1790), with Wilford's later chronological essays (1790s-1810)","workYear":1790,"summary":"The first European chronology of India anchored Indian history by identifying Sandracottus with Chandragupta Maurya (c. 320 BCE). Jones and Wilford then counted back through Puranic dynasty and Magadha king-lists, with help from Calcutta pandits. This put the Bharata war in the second millennium BCE, roughly the 14th century BCE. I could not verify which of the two authors gave the c. 1370 BCE figure, or the exact number, so treat the attribution as uncertain. Wilford's work was later found to rest partly on forged Sanskrit material.","keyEvidence":["Sandracottus = Chandragupta synchronism used as the fixed anchor","Puranic dynasty and Magadha king-lists counted back from that anchor"],"buildsOn":["Greek accounts of Sandracottus","Puranic dynasty lists","Calcutta pandits' tradition"],"critiques":["Wilford admitted around 1805-1810 that his pandit informant had forged or interpolated Sanskrit texts, which undermines his work.","Puranic king-lists include overlapping dynasties, so counting them end to end inflates the total.","There is no astronomical anchor for the date.","Later scholars disagreed, e.g. Bentley (1825) argued for a much later date."],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Asiatic Researches vol. 2 (1790), W. Jones, On the Chronology of the Hindus","Wilford, essays in Asiatic Researches vols. 3-10 (1792-1808)","Wilford forgery affair, c. 1805-1810 (Asiatic Researches vol. 10)"]},{"id":"mb-cunningham-1426-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Alexander Cunningham","proposerInfo":"British Army engineer, first Director-General of the Archaeological Survey of India, numismatist and epigraphist (1814-1893)","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war","dateLabel":"c. 1426 BCE (attributed; not verified against the text)","year":-1426,"yearLow":-1426,"yearHigh":-1426,"method":"Era / epigraphic reckoning","work":"The Book of Indian Eras, with Tables for Calculating Indian Dates (1883)","workYear":1883,"summary":"Cunningham's Book of Indian Eras is a real 1883 reference work of calendars, epochs and conversion tables. It discusses the Kali, Saptarshi (Laukika) and related reckonings. The figure of c. 1426 BCE for the Bharata war is attributed to him in secondary lists of proposed dates. I could not open the book to confirm the number or his derivation. His approach was probably to reconcile the traditional Kali epoch (3102 BCE) with the Puranic king-lists and the Saptarshi cycle, then to treat the war as a historical event of the late second millennium BCE. This entry is low confidence until the page is checked.","keyEvidence":["The book's subject matter is Indian eras (Kali, Saptarshi, Vikrama, Saka, Gupta and others), so any war date in it would come from era arithmetic and Puranic chronology, not from planetary positions.","No specific planetary positions or verses are known to be cited for the 1426 BCE figure. I could not confirm which source passage he used."],"buildsOn":["Puranic dynastic lists","Kali Yuga epoch tradition","Saptarshi (Laukika) cycle reckoning"],"critiques":["The exact figure and the derivation are not verified; secondary lists may misattribute it.","Nineteenth-century era and king-list methods depend on unreliable Puranic regnal lengths.","Later astronomical, genealogical and archaeological work (Painted Grey Ware horizon, Hastinapura excavations) has led to different dates, so the figure should be read as a historical-school estimate."],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Cunningham, A. (1883). The Book of Indian Eras, with Tables for Calculating Indian Dates. Calcutta: Thacker, Spink & Co. (archive.org has scans; page not checked)"]},{"id":"mb-dikshit-1424-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Shankar Balakrishna Dikshit (S.B. Dikshit)","proposerInfo":"Marathi Indian astronomer and historian of astronomy (1853-1898); author of Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra, the first comprehensive history of Indian astronomy.","event":"Kurukshetra war (Bharata war), as attributed to Dikshit's work; the exact figure is unverified","dateLabel":"c. 1424 BCE (recalled figure, unverified; other recalled values of ~1400-1900 BCE are not confirmed)","year":-1424,"yearLow":-1900,"yearHigh":-1400,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra (Marathi, 1896; English translation by R.V. Vaidya, Govt. of India, 1969-81)","workYear":1896,"summary":"Dikshit's history of Indian astronomy treated the traditional 3102 BCE Kali epoch as a later computational convention, not an observed date. He anchored early Indian chronology on the solstice position in the Vedanga Jyotisha (roughly 1400 BCE by his reckoning) and on Vedic and epic astronomical references. This puts the Bharata war in the second millennium BCE, around the mid-15th century BCE in the figure commonly attributed to him. I could not check the exact number against the text, so 1424 BCE is a recalled attribution and the other figures in the task prompt are unconfirmed. K.D. Abhyankar also worked on Vedic and epic astronomy, but I could not confirm which Mahabharata date he proposed or how it relates to Dikshit's.","keyEvidence":["Argument that the 3102 BCE Kali epoch was back-calculated by later siddhantic astronomers rather than observed.","Vedanga Jyotisha solstice position, which Dikshit used as a chronological anchor (about 1400 BCE).","Epic astronomical references read against that anchor (specific verses and planetary positions not verified)."],"buildsOn":["Vedanga Jyotisha solstice data","Bal Gangadhar Tilak (Orion, 1893) on Vedic astronomical dating","Siddhantic and Puranic tradition (as the target of his critique)"],"critiques":["The exact figure of 1424 BCE is not verified against the text of Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra.","Dating the Vedanga Jyotisha by its solstice position is itself disputed, because the text is ambiguous and may be later.","Modern archaeology and Vedic chronology generally place the Kuru polity and Painted Grey Ware horizon around 1200-800 BCE, so a mid-15th-century BCE war sits earlier than most scholarly estimates.","Later astronomical daters such as Oak and Vartak reject his basis and propose different dates."],"plausibility":4,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["S.B. Dikshit, Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra (1896); English translation by R.V. Vaidya, Govt. of India, 1969-81","K.D. Abhyankar, papers on Vedic and epic astronomy (specific titles not verified)"]},{"id":"mb-sengupta-1298-1198-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Prabodh Chandra Sengupta (P.C. Sengupta)","proposerInfo":"Indian historian of astronomy and chronology, Calcutta University; wrote on Indian astronomy and the Surya Siddhanta","event":"Kurukshetra war (claimed alternative date, unverified)","dateLabel":"c. 1298 BCE or 1198 BCE (alternative attributed to Sengupta; not confirmed). His documented date is 2449 BCE.","year":-1298,"yearLow":-1298,"yearHigh":-1198,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Ancient Indian Chronology (University of Calcutta, 1947)","workYear":1947,"summary":"P.C. Sengupta's Ancient Indian Chronology (1947) is well known for dating the Mahabharata war to 2449 BCE. He used the Mahabharata's eclipse references and the Saptarishi tradition (Vriddha Garga, Varahamihira). I could not find any source in which he proposes 1298 BCE or 1198 BCE, and I could not confirm the claim. It may be a garbled or secondary attribution. Treat this entry as unverified and do not rely on it.","keyEvidence":["Sengupta's documented 2449 BCE argument rests on the Mahabharata's recorded eclipses and on the Vriddha Garga and Varahamihira statement that the Saptarishi were in Magha at Yudhishthira's time.","No evidence located for the 1298 or 1198 BCE variant."],"buildsOn":["Vriddha Garga","Varahamihira (Brihat Samhita)","Kali-era reckoning"],"critiques":["The 1298/1198 BCE attribution is not found in any source I could check. No web search was run, so this rests on background knowledge only.","If the dates exist, they would conflict with Sengupta's own 2449 BCE date.","Dates of 1200-1300 BCE are nearer the range archaeologists favour (c. 1000-900 BCE), but that does not support attributing them to Sengupta."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Not found","references":["P.C. Sengupta, Ancient Indian Chronology, University of Calcutta, 1947 (the book itself was not examined for this entry)"]},{"id":"mb-sita-nath-pradhan-1400s","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Sita Nath Pradhan","proposerInfo":"Indian historian at the University of Calcutta (ancient Indian history); worked in the Pargiter tradition of Puranic chronology.","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war, with Parikshit's accession","dateLabel":"c. 1424 BCE (commonly cited figure; scheme falls in the 1400s BCE)","year":-1424,"yearLow":-1500,"yearHigh":-1400,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Chronology of Ancient India (from the Rise of Parikshit to the Rise of Mahapadma Nanda), University of Calcutta","workYear":1927,"summary":"Pradhan reconstructed early Indian chronology from the Puranic dynastic lists. He worked back from historically fixed anchors (Chandragupta Maurya, the Nandas, and the Buddha and Mahavira) through the Puranic interval between Parikshit and Mahapadma Nanda. The Puranas give that interval as roughly 1000 to 1050 years, and he accepted it with limited adjustment. Adding it to the Nanda anchor puts the war and Parikshit's accession in the 1400s BCE, and 1424 BCE is the figure usually quoted. This is a historian's genealogical method that treats the Puranic lists as broadly reliable. It is not an astronomical dating. I could not check the book directly, so the exact figure rests on background knowledge.","keyEvidence":["Puranic statement that about 1015 years (variants 1050, 1115) separate Parikshit from Mahapadma Nanda","Anchoring the Nanda and Maurya dynasties to the historical chronology","Regnal lengths of the Paurava, Aikshvaka and Magadha lists"],"buildsOn":["F. E. Pargiter","Puranic king-lists (Vishnu, Matsya, Bhagavata, Vayu)"],"critiques":["The Puranic interval has variant readings, so the result moves by centuries with the version chosen.","The lists may include contemporaneous dynasties and be inflated, which is why Pargiter reached about 950 BCE.","The date is not tied to any astronomical data in the epic.","Recent archaeology links the Painted Grey Ware horizon, which is often associated with the epic, to roughly 1200 to 600 BCE, so 1400s BCE is probably a little early."],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["S. N. Pradhan, Chronology of Ancient India, University of Calcutta, 1927","F. E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, 1922"]},{"id":"mb-daftari-1000s-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Kesheo Lakshman (K.L.) Daftari","proposerInfo":"Indian scholar and member of the Calendar Reform Committee (which prepared the Indian national calendar); Wikipedia lists him among Mahabharata war dating proposers.","event":"Kurukshetra war (Mahabharata war)","dateLabel":"1197 BCE (Wikipedia's Kurukshetra War article); falls in the late 2nd millennium BCE, not the 1000s BCE","year":-1197,"yearLow":-1197,"yearHigh":-1197,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Not identified. I could not confirm the title of his book or paper.","workYear":null,"summary":"Wikipedia's list of proposed dates for the Kurukshetra War credits Kesheo Lakshman Daftari with 1197 BCE. The task's phrase \"around 1000s BCE\" is therefore imprecise. 1197 BCE is in the 1100s BCE, and it is close to Dieter Koch's 1198 BCE. I could not retrieve Daftari's own work, so I could not check his method or the verses he used. I have not confirmed that his method was astronomical.","keyEvidence":["Wikipedia's Kurukshetra War article lists Daftari at 1197 BCE and describes him as a member of the Calendar Reform Committee.","The same list gives Dieter Koch 1198 BCE (planetarium software and super-conjunctions), V. S. Dubey 950 BCE, B. N. Achar 3067 BCE and B. B. Lal 836 BCE.","I did not find his method or the verses he relied on in any source."],"buildsOn":[],"critiques":["The 1197 BCE date comes from a single secondary source (Wikipedia). I could not check it against his own writing.","Mainstream archaeology generally places the Painted Grey Ware and Kuru-region material later, in the early first millennium BCE. A 12th-century BCE war is earlier than most historians would accept.","The agreement with Koch's 1198 BCE may be coincidence, because the methods are probably different."],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Partly verified","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurukshetra_War"]},{"id":"mb-majumdar-1000-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"R.C. Majumdar (as general editor of the Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan history series; the war chronology rests on Pargiter, Raychaudhuri and A.D. Pusalker)","proposerInfo":"Eminent Indian historian (1888-1980), general editor of The History and Culture of the Indian People, former Vice-Chancellor of Dacca University.","event":"Bharata (Kurukshetra) war","dateLabel":"c. 1000 BCE (approximate; the Vedic Age volume's own chapter gives c. 950 BCE, so the round figure is a loose summary)","year":-1000,"yearLow":-1000,"yearHigh":-900,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"The History and Culture of the Indian People, Vol. I: The Vedic Age (ed. R.C. Majumdar, Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan)","workYear":1951,"summary":"The mainstream 20th-century academic position, which the Bhavan series under Majumdar's editorship adopted, puts the Bharata war in roughly the 10th century BCE. It is not an astronomical date. It counts back through Puranic dynastic lists from historically anchored kings (Nandas and Mauryas, 4th century BCE) using averaged generation lengths. Pargiter's calculation gave c. 950 BCE and Raychaudhuri placed Parikshit near 900 BCE. This school treats the epic as having a historical core of a Kuru-Panchala conflict and regards the traditional 3102 BCE date and astronomical reconstructions as unreliable. I did not check the exact wording or page of Majumdar's own figure online, so the round 1000 BCE is approximate.","keyEvidence":["Puranic king-lists from Parikshit down to Mahapadma Nanda, with the Puranic statement of roughly 1015 or 1050 years between them","Averaged generation lengths applied to those lists","Late Vedic literature places the Kuru king Parikshit after the war","Painted Grey Ware archaeology at Hastinapura and elsewhere, c. 1100-800 BCE, is often cited as loosely consistent"],"buildsOn":["F.E. Pargiter","H.C. Raychaudhuri","A.D. Pusalker"],"critiques":["Puranic lists are late, incomplete and inconsistent, and generation lengths are assumed.","The result depends on the anchor to Nanda and Mauryan chronology.","Skeptics doubt that a single historical war can be dated at all.","Traditionalists and astronomical daters reject it as far too late."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["R.C. Majumdar (ed.), The History and Culture of the Indian People, Vol. I: The Vedic Age, Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan","F.E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition (1922)","H.C. Raychaudhuri, Political History of Ancient India"]},{"id":"mb-vaidya-3101-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"C. V. Vaidya (Chintaman Vinayak Vaidya)","proposerInfo":"Marathi historian and Sanskritist (1861-1938), Bombay; retired judicial officer, author of works on the epics and medieval Hindu India.","event":"Kurukshetra (Bharata) war, tied to the start of the Kali era","dateLabel":"c. 3101 BCE (traditional Kali-era epoch of 3102/3101 BCE, which Vaidya took as the war date)","year":-3101,"yearLow":-3102,"yearHigh":-3101,"method":"Era / epigraphic reckoning","work":"The Mahabharata: A Criticism (1904); Epic India, or India as described in the Mahabharata and the Ramayana (1907)","workYear":1904,"summary":"Vaidya, writing in the early 1900s, defended the orthodox Indian tradition that the Bharata war was fought at the Dvapara-Kali junction, c. 3101 BCE, against Western scholars who placed it near 1400-1000 BCE. He treated the Kali era and Puranic chronology as authentic early reckoning, and argued the epic's core was composed soon after the war and expanded later. He is an early 20th-century voice of the traditionalist case that later writers such as Nilesh Oak revived with astronomy. I could not retrieve the books online in this session, so the details of his internal argument come from background knowledge and are not verified.","keyEvidence":["Kali-era epoch (18 Feb 3102 BCE) taken as the war date, following Aryabhata's tradition","Puranic statement that Kali began after Krishna's departure, shortly after the war","Argument that the epic's society and geography are very ancient and its core text was composed near the war"],"buildsOn":["Aryabhata and the Indian siddhantic Kali-epoch tradition","Puranic genealogies and yuga chronology"],"critiques":["The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is a back-computed astronomical epoch, first attested in Aryabhata (499 CE) and in inscriptions from Aihole (634 CE), not a contemporary record.","Archaeology shows no matching urban or iron-using Kuru culture in 3100 BCE; Painted Grey Ware sites at Hastinapura and elsewhere date to about 1200-600 BCE.","Other Indian traditions give conflicting war dates, such as Varahamihira's 2449 BCE, so the tradition is not unanimous.","Western historians (Pargiter and others) derive about 950 BCE from the king-lists, and the Vedic and epic evidence supports a first-millennium BCE core."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["C. V. Vaidya, The Mahabharata: A Criticism (Bombay, 1904)","C. V. Vaidya, Epic India, or India as described in the Mahabharata and the Ramayana (Bombay, 1907)","C. V. Vaidya, Riddle of the Ramayana (1906)"]},{"id":"mb-achar-3067-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Narahari G. Achar","proposerInfo":"Physicist (University of Memphis) who also writes on Vedic and Indian astronomy history; not a professional historian or Sanskritist.","event":"Start of the Kurukshetra war (Mahabharata war)","dateLabel":"22 November 3067 BCE (war begins; as recalled, unverified against the original paper)","year":-3067,"yearLow":-3067,"yearHigh":-3067,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Papers in the Indian Journal of History of Science and conference volumes, c. 1998-2003, including \"On the astronomical basis of the date of the Mahabharata war\"; exact titles and volumes not verified.","workYear":2000,"summary":"Achar, a physicist, takes astronomical omens and sky descriptions from the Mahabharata (mainly Udyoga and Bhishma Parvas) and tests them with Voyager and Planetarium software. The Bhishma Parva omen, in which the Moon is eclipsed or conjoined with Krittika/Rohini while Saturn is near Rohini, is central to his argument. He reads the war as starting on 22 Nov 3067 BCE. He takes Bhishma's death at the winter solstice (Uttarayana) as a check on the date. He follows the earlier work of K. Srinivasa Raghavan, who also reached about 3067 BCE, and he treats the sequence of eclipses as a main discriminator. He rejects the traditional 3102 BCE date and the Aryabhata-style 3137/3138 BCE date.","keyEvidence":["Bhishma Parva omens: eclipses within 13 days (lunar then solar), the Moon near Krittika, Saturn near Rohini","Planetarium and Voyager software used to search for matching sky configurations","Bhishma dies at Uttarayana, giving a solstice check on the war date","Agreement with Raghavan's earlier calculation of about 3067 BCE"],"buildsOn":["K. Srinivasa Raghavan","P. V. Vartak (as a contrast)","Aryabhata and Varahamihira traditions (rejected)"],"critiques":["The Mahabharata sky descriptions are poetic and inconsistent, and different readers pick different verses; this makes the method selective.","The text is a composite, and the omen verses may be later interpolations, so they may not record real observations.","Nilesh Oak and others argue that a different set of verses gives a different date (Oak: 5561 BCE).","Archaeology gives no support to a 3000 BCE Kuru kingdom with a developed state; PGW and Hastinapura evidence points much later.","A date from a single physicist's software run has had limited peer review among Sanskritists and historians."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Achar, N. 'On the astronomical basis of the date of the Mahabharata war', Indian Journal of History of Science (c. 2000; not checked)","Raghavan, K. S. 'Date of the Bharata War' (1969; not checked)","Oak, N. 'When Did the Mahabharata War Happen?' (2011; for the critique)"]},{"id":"mb-saroj-bala-isserve-3067-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra (I-SERVE)","proposerInfo":"Saroj Bala is a retired Indian Revenue Service officer and I-SERVE (Institute of Scientific Research on Vedas) researcher who also dates the Ramayana; Kulbhushan Mishra is a co-researcher and author with I-SERVE.","event":"Start of the Kurukshetra (Mahabharata) war","dateLabel":"22 November 3067 BCE (as stated by the proposers; the day the war began)","year":-3067,"yearLow":-3067,"yearHigh":-3067,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Dating the Epic of India series (Mahabharata part), I-SERVE, with Planetarium Gold software; later articles and talks by Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra","workYear":2010,"summary":"I-SERVE researchers Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra took a large set of astronomical references scattered through the Mahabharata and fed them to planetarium software (Planetarium Gold or similar) to find a single year in which they all hold together. They arrive at 22 November 3067 BCE for the start of the war. Their reasoning is that the epic's sky descriptions, which include eclipses, planetary positions, Saptarishi and nakshatra references, only match a narrow window of years, and that 3067 BCE gives the best fit. The date is later than Nilesh Oak's 5561 BCE and Achar's 3067 BCE, which sits close to it. It is also well before the traditional 3138/3102 BCE dates that other scholars use. They treat the epic as history and the sky data as observations made at the time.","keyEvidence":["Eclipse pair described before the war (a lunar eclipse followed by a solar eclipse about 13 days apart), matched against the software's eclipse list","Planetary positions given in the Bhishma Parva omens, including Mars, Saturn and Jupiter near particular nakshatras","Saptarishi, Arundhati and Vasishtha omen verses","Reference to the Pole star and seasonal markers such as Kartika/Margashirsha timing","Cross-checking of the total set of verses against planetarium output for a unique match year"],"buildsOn":["P. V. Vartak","P. V. Holay","Nilesh Oak","Traditional Puranic chronology"],"critiques":["Planetarium-software dating depends heavily on which verses are accepted as literal observations and how nakshatra and planet names are interpreted, so different researchers reach different dates from the same text.","Mainstream historians say the epic grew over centuries and its sky descriptions may be later interpolations or poetic omens rather than eyewitness records.","A war date near 3067 BCE falls in the Early Bronze Age, with no archaeological support for an Iron Age style epic world there; painted grey ware sites such as Hastinapura date from about 1200-800 BCE.","Some of the claimed positions are said to be only loosely matched, and the series is not published in peer reviewed astronomy or history journals.","The exact figures given in secondary sources vary, so the date should be checked against the proposers' own books and videos."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["I-SERVE (Institute of Scientific Research on Vedas) website, Dating the Epic of India publications","Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra, Dating the Era of Lord Rama / Mahabharata series, talks and videos","Wikipedia: Kurukshetra War (dating section)","Nilesh Oak, When Did the Mahabharata War Happen? The Mystery of Arundhati (2011)"]},{"id":"mb-saroj-bala-bhatnagar-3139-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Saroj Bala and Ashok Bhatnagar (I-SERVE)","proposerInfo":"Saroj Bala (retired Indian Revenue Service officer) and Ashok Bhatnagar, associated with I-SERVE (Institute of Scientific Research on Vedas), who date epic events with Planetarium Gold software","event":"Kurukshetra war (3139 BCE claim not confirmed)","dateLabel":"3139 BCE (unconfirmed attribution; their documented Mahabharata war date is 3067 BCE)","year":-3139,"yearLow":-3139,"yearHigh":-3067,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Saroj Bala and Ashok Bhatnagar, I-SERVE papers and talks on the date of the Mahabharata war (3067 BCE); related: Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (2013)","workYear":2013,"summary":"I-SERVE's published Mahabharata date, as I recall it, is 3067 BCE, obtained by matching the epic's sky descriptions to Planetarium Gold output. I could not find or recall a 3139 BCE claim by Saroj Bala and Bhatnagar, so that attribution looks doubtful. It may be confused with the traditional war date of about 3138 BCE, which is 36 years before the Kali epoch of 3102 BCE. If a 3139 BCE figure exists, it would be about 72 years earlier than 3067 BCE. I did not verify any of this online, so treat the entry as unconfirmed and keep 3067 BCE as their main date.","keyEvidence":["Their 3067 BCE argument uses the Mahabharata's planetary and eclipse descriptions, such as the two eclipses 13 days apart, matched in planetarium software (from memory, not verified)","No source for 3139 BCE was found"],"buildsOn":["Traditional Kali-era reckoning (Kali 3102 BCE, war c. 3138 BCE)","Aryabhata","P. V. Vartak (comparison)","Nilesh Oak (comparison)"],"critiques":["The 3139 BCE attribution could not be confirmed and may conflate the traditional 3138 BCE date with their 3067 BCE result.","Software matching of narrative sky descriptions depends on which verses are treated as reliable and how they are read.","Dates this early are well before the archaeological horizon of the Painted Grey Ware (c. 1200-600 BCE) usually linked to the epic's setting."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["I-SERVE (Institute of Scientific Research on Vedas) publications by Saroj Bala","Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra, Dating the Era of Lord Rama, 2013"]},{"id":"mb-vartak-5561-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"P. V. Vartak (Padmakar Vishnu Vartak)","proposerInfo":"Marathi-language Indian scholar-author (Pune, Maharashtra) of Vedic/epic chronology works. Biographical details are thinly documented; he is not a professional astronomer or archaeologist.","event":"Kurukshetra war (start of the 18-day battle)","dateLabel":"c. 16 October 5561 BCE (start of war, as stated in the task; the same year, 5561 BCE, is later adopted by Nilesh Oak)","year":-5561,"yearLow":-5561,"yearHigh":-5561,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Swayambhu (Marathi) and Vastav Ramayan (Marathi); publication years not verified, believed to be roughly 1970s-1980s","workYear":1980,"summary":"Vartak argues that the epic's astronomical references (Bhishma Parva omens, planetary and nakshatra positions, the winter-solstice timing of Bhishma's death) fit a single date in the sixth millennium BCE. He puts the war in 5561 BCE, which is far earlier than the traditional 3138/3102 BCE. His Ramayana dating is separate and much earlier (Rama c. 7300 BCE, as I recall). The same-year result in Nilesh Oak's work is often cited as independent support, but Oak's method differs. I could not verify Vartak's exact computations, publication years or full biography, so treat the details as provisional.","keyEvidence":["Reported reliance on Mahabharata sky descriptions (Bhishma Parva omens, unusual planetary positions, nakshatra references) read literally","Bhishma's death at the winter solstice (Uttarayana) used as a calendrical anchor (general to this school, not verified for Vartak specifically)","Linked with his Ramayana chronology (Vastav Ramayan) to form a long Vedic-era timeline","5561 BCE is also the year Nilesh Oak derived later, with planetarium software and a different set of verses"],"buildsOn":["Mahabharata Bhishma Parva astronomical verses","Traditional Indian calendrical reckoning"],"critiques":["The war date is far earlier than the archaeological record for Kuru/Painted Grey Ware settlements (c. 1200-600 BCE) would support.","Reading epic omen verses as precise observations is disputed, since the text is a layered composition with later interpolations.","Many verse-based astronomical fits can be matched to several dates, which gives non-unique solutions.","Vartak's work is Marathi, little peer-reviewed, and hard to verify; his computations are not independently reproduced.","A 5561 BCE date conflicts with the Sarasvati/Harappan-era archaeology and with the lack of iron, horses and chariots at that date."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["P. V. Vartak, Swayambhu (Marathi) - details not verified","P. V. Vartak, Vastav Ramayan (Marathi) - details not verified","Nilesh Oak, When Did the Mahabharata War Happen? The Mystery of Arundhati (2011)"]},{"id":"mb-nilesh-oak-5561-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Nilesh Nilkanth Oak","proposerInfo":"US-based engineer and independent researcher (not a professional historian or astronomer); author and lecturer on Mahabharata dating","event":"Kurukshetra (Mahabharata) war","dateLabel":"c. 5561 BCE (war in autumn of that year; the exact day, given as 1 Oct 5561 BCE in the brief, is not confirmed. Vartak's date is more often quoted as 16 Oct 5561 BCE.)","year":-5561,"yearLow":-5561,"yearHigh":-5561,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"When Did the Mahabharata War Happen? The Mystery of Arundhati (2011)","workYear":2011,"summary":"Oak collected the astronomical observations stated in the Mahabharata (he counts well over 200 and keeps those he judges consistent and observational). He then searched for the one epoch that fits them using planetarium software such as Stellarium and Planetarium Gold. He reports a unique match around 5561 BCE. A key piece is the omen that Arundhati is ahead of Vasishtha, which he reads as a real sky observation of the Mizar-Alcor pair. He presents this as independent confirmation of P. V. Vartak's earlier 5561 BCE date, while differing from Vartak on some interpretations. This record comes from background knowledge and was not checked against the book or other sources.","keyEvidence":["Arundhati-Vasishtha omen in the Bhishma Parva, read as Arundhati preceding Vasishtha","A large set of planetary, lunar and nakshatra observations from the epic, matched by software to a single epoch","Reported agreement with Vartak's 5561 BCE result"],"buildsOn":["P. V. Vartak"],"critiques":["Planetarium software cannot decide between competing readings of ambiguous Sanskrit verses, and the result depends on which verses are kept as observations.","A 5561 BCE war conflicts with the archaeological record, including the Painted Grey Ware horizon (about 1100-500 BCE) and the lack of any matching urban or iron-age context.","Mainstream scholars see the epic as layered, composed and revised over centuries, so its sky references are not treated as a single dated record."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Nilesh Oak, When Did the Mahabharata War Happen? The Mystery of Arundhati (2011)"]},{"id":"mb-iyengar-chandra-hari-1478-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"R.N. Iyengar and K. Chandra Hari (possibly separate papers, not a verified joint paper)","proposerInfo":"R.N. Iyengar: Indian civil engineer and historian of Indian astronomy (formerly Indian Institute of Science, Bangalore). K. Chandra Hari: Kerala-based researcher on Mahabharata astronomical dating. Affiliations are from memory and not checked.","event":"Kurukshetra (Mahabharata) war","dateLabel":"c. 1478 BCE (as attributed to these authors; unverified)","year":-1478,"yearLow":-1478,"yearHigh":-1478,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Papers in Indian Journal of History of Science (INSA). Exact titles, volumes and years not verified; Iyengar's is believed to be a 2013 review of Mahabharata astronomical dating, and Chandra Hari's an earlier paper (c. 2003) on astronomical data of the war.","workYear":2013,"summary":"The 1478 BCE date comes from matching the Mahabharata's planetary and eclipse references to modern computed skies in the second millennium BCE, then picking an epoch where a consistent subset fits. Chandra Hari's work is generally cited for a late-second-millennium date, and Iyengar's IJHS discussion is a critical review of the competing astronomical dates. The attribution of exactly 1478 BCE to both authors jointly could not be confirmed. I did not reach the papers, so the date, the pairing and the details are from background knowledge. Treat this entry as provisional.","keyEvidence":["Epic sky descriptions: two eclipses within 13 days before the war, Mars near Jyeshtha/Anuradha, the comet-like Ketu near Chitra, and Saturn near Rohini","Computed ephemerides for the second millennium BCE matched to these verses","Reported reliance on the Critical Edition text for verse selection (not verified)"],"buildsOn":["P.V. Vartak","P.C. Sengupta","K. Chandra Hari (for Iyengar)","Critical Edition of the Mahabharata (BORI)","Planetarium-software dating tradition (Nilesh Oak, Achar)"],"critiques":["Not independently verified: the joint authorship and the exact 1478 BCE figure may be conflated from separate works","Astronomical matching is very sensitive to which verses are accepted as original and how nakshatra names and eclipse visibility are interpreted","Epic sky descriptions are likely poetic or interpolated, so many incompatible dates fit (5561, 3067, 1478, 950 BCE and others)","A c. 1478 BCE date sits near the arrival of Painted Grey Ware and the Late Harappan phase, which is archaeologically less strained than 3000+ BCE, but there is no direct link between the epic and the archaeology"],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Indian Journal of History of Science (INSA), papers by R.N. Iyengar and K. Chandra Hari; exact citations to be located at insa.nic.in or via Google Scholar","Critical Edition of the Mahabharata, BORI, Pune"]},{"id":"mb-vedveer-arya-3162-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Vedveer Arya","proposerInfo":"Contemporary Indian author/researcher on Vedic and Mahabharata chronology, associated with Arya Samaj-style traditional scholarship (identity not independently confirmed).","event":"Kurukshetra war (Mahabharata war)","dateLabel":"c. 3162 BCE (as relayed; unverified)","year":-3162,"yearLow":-3162,"yearHigh":-3162,"method":"Other","work":"Not identified; I could not locate the specific book, paper or video","workYear":null,"summary":"The only source for this entry is the claim that Vedveer Arya dates the Mahabharata war to about 3162 BCE. I could not confirm the proposer's work, method or exact date, because the one search attempt was blocked by a bot wall. The date sits near the traditional Kali-era reckoning of 3102 BCE (war about 36 years earlier, 3138 BCE) and may come from a traditional or genealogical calculation that adjusts it by a few decades. That is a guess and not confirmed. Treat the entry as a placeholder with low confidence until the source is found.","keyEvidence":["No evidence verified. The specific verses or planetary positions used are unknown.","Date lies about 60 years before 3102 BCE, within the traditional Kali-era family of dates."],"buildsOn":["Traditional Puranic/Kali Yuga chronology (3102 BCE; war 3138 BCE) - presumed, not verified"],"critiques":["Cannot be assessed without the source.","Dates near 3100 BCE are generally criticised because the archaeology of northern India (Painted Grey Ware, c. 1200-600 BCE) shows no matching urban or iron-using Kuru culture."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Not found","references":[]},{"id":"mb-dayananda-3000-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Swami Dayananda Saraswati (with later echoes: Frawley/Rajaram, B.G. Sidharth, Sadhguru)","proposerInfo":"Dayananda: founder of the Arya Samaj, Vedic reformer. Frawley: Vedic scholar. Rajaram: engineer and Indologist. Sidharth: physicist, B.M. Birla Science Centre. Sadhguru: yoga teacher, Isha Foundation.","event":"Kurukshetra war","dateLabel":"c. 3000 BCE (Dayananda: roughly 4,900 years before the 1870s; Frawley/Rajaram 3000-3100 BCE; Sadhguru about 5000 years ago)","year":-3000,"yearLow":-3100,"yearHigh":-3000,"method":"Era / epigraphic reckoning","work":"Satyarth Prakash (Dayananda, 1875, revised 1884); Frawley and Rajaram, Vedic Aryans and the Origins of Civilization (1995)","workYear":1875,"summary":"This is a cluster of round-number dates near 3000 BCE, not one method. Dayananda, writing in the 1870s, treated the war as about 5,000 years in the past, in line with the Kali-yuga tradition. Frawley and Rajaram argue for about 3100 BCE, partly to fit their thesis that the Vedic and Sarasvati-Harappan cultures were one. Sidharth (as I recall, c. 3000 BCE) and Sadhguru (about 5000 years ago in talks) give approximate figures. None of them gives an independent planetary computation.","keyEvidence":["Dayananda: traditional reckoning that the war fell near the start of Kali, with a figure of about 4,900 years before his time; the exact passage was not re-checked.","Frawley/Rajaram: Sarasvati river drying and Vedic-Harappan identity, aligned with the traditional 3102 BCE.","Sidharth and Sadhguru: general assertions with no cited planetary-position study that I could verify."],"buildsOn":["Traditional Kali-yuga epoch 3102 BCE","Aryabhata","Puranic tradition"],"critiques":["These are traditional or round-number dates, not independent computations.","Archaeology shows no distinct Mahabharata horizon at 3000 BCE; Painted Grey Ware at Hastinapura is dated to roughly 1100-800 BCE.","Mainstream philology dates the core text's composition to about 400 BCE-400 CE, with older oral material."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Dayananda Saraswati, Satyarth Prakash (1875/1884)","Frawley and Rajaram, Vedic Aryans and the Origins of Civilization (1995)","B.G. Sidharth, The Celestial Key to the Vedas (1999)"]},{"id":"mb-bailly-kali-observation","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Jean-Sylvain Bailly","proposerInfo":"French astronomer and historian of astronomy, Academie des Sciences member, first mayor of Paris (1789-91); guillotined 1793","event":"Kali Yuga epoch (18 Feb 3102 BCE), traditionally tied to the Mahabharata war era. Bailly argued the epoch was a real astronomical observation; he did not newly date the war itself.","dateLabel":"Kali epoch 3102 BCE read as a real observation; war traditionally c. 3100 BCE","year":-3102,"yearLow":-3102,"yearHigh":-3100,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale (Histoire de l'astronomie indienne et orientale), 1787","workYear":1787,"summary":"Bailly analysed Indian astronomical tables brought to Europe by Le Gentil (the Tirvalore/Tiruvalur tables) and others. He concluded that the Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE, with planets near a common starting longitude, matched real sky conditions, so it came from genuine observation by a very ancient and advanced people. This gave apparent scientific backing to the traditional Kali date and, by extension, to a war c. 3100 BCE. Samuel Davis (1789) and John Playfair (1790) rebutted the core claim, showing the epoch could be produced by back-calculation from later tables. Bailly's claim concerns the Kali epoch. The tie to the Mahabharata war date is the traditional one, not his own argument.","keyEvidence":["Tirvalore (Tiruvalur) and other South Indian tables obtained by Le Gentil, with an epoch at 3102 BCE","Claim that planetary mean positions at the epoch agree with modern back-computation, which he took as proof of observation","Inference from the tables' accuracy that an advanced earlier civilisation existed"],"buildsOn":["Guillaume Le Gentil (Tirvalore tables)","Indian siddhantic Kali Yuga tradition"],"critiques":["Samuel Davis, 'On the Astronomical Computations of the Hindus', Asiatic Researches 2 (1789): the epoch is a computational construct","John Playfair, 'Remarks on the Astronomy of the Brahmins', Trans. Royal Society of Edinburgh 2 (1790): argued the epoch was back-calculated, while accepting some antiquity for the tables","Later critics such as John Bentley (1825) held the Indian tables to be of much later origin","A real conjunction of all planets at 3102 BCE is not borne out by modern computation","Even if the epoch were real, it would not date the war"],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["J.-S. Bailly, Traite de l'astronomie indienne et orientale, Paris, 1787","S. Davis, Asiatic Researches vol. 2, 1789","J. Playfair, Transactions of the Royal Society of Edinburgh vol. 2, 1790"]},{"id":"mb-megasthenes-heracles-6000","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Megasthenes (as reported by Arrian, Pliny, Strabo); modern writers who read Heracles as Krishna","proposerInfo":"Megasthenes was a Seleucid ambassador at Chandragupta Maurya's court (c. 300 BCE). His lost Indica survives only in fragments quoted by Arrian, Pliny the Elder, Strabo and others.","event":"Krishna's era (Mahabharata age), inferred from the Greek 'Heracles' of the Sourasenoi and the Indian king-counts Megasthenes reported","dateLabel":"c. 6400-6800 BCE (approximate, inferred). Arrian gives 6042 years from Dionysus to Sandracottus; Pliny gives 6451 years from Liber to Alexander. The sources date Dionysus, not Krishna, so a Heracles/Krishna date is a modern inference.","year":-6368,"yearLow":-6777,"yearHigh":-6000,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Megasthenes, Indica (c. 300 BCE), as preserved in Arrian's Indica 8-9 and Pliny's Natural History 6.21","workYear":-300,"summary":"Megasthenes reported that the Indians counted about 153 kings and 6042 years from Dionysus to Sandracottus (Chandragupta). Arrian also says Heracles was worshipped by the Sourasenoi, whose cities were Methora and Cleisobora and whose river was the Jobares. These are usually equated with Krishna or Vasudeva, Mathura, Krishnapura and the Yamuna. As I recall Arrian, Heracles stands about 15 generations after Dionysus. That would leave roughly 138 generations from Heracles to Sandracottus, which is probably the origin of the '138 generations' figure. The 6042 years belong to Dionysus, so any Krishna date has to be inferred. Writers who use this argument put Krishna near or before 6000 BCE. This summary is from memory and I did not check the passages against the texts.","keyEvidence":["Arrian, Indica 9: about 153 kings and 6042 years from Dionysus to Sandracottus (figure recalled from memory, not re-checked).","Pliny, Natural History 6.21: about 6451 years and 3 months, with 153 or 154 kings, from Liber Pater to Alexander (recalled from memory).","Arrian, Indica 8: Heracles is honoured by the Sourasenoi, with the cities Methora and Cleisobora and the river Jobares. These are read as Mathura, Krishnapura and the Yamuna.","The generation count from Heracles to Sandracottus (about 138) is my reconstruction from the 153-king total and a 15-generation gap, and I did not verify it."],"buildsOn":["Megasthenes' Indica","Arrian","Pliny the Elder","Strabo","Christian Lassen and other 19th-century Indologists who identified Heracles with Krishna"],"critiques":["Averaging 6042 years over 153 kings gives about 39 years per reign, which is implausible. The figure looks like a schematic or mythic count, not a real regnal record.","Pliny's and Arrian's totals disagree (6451 against 6042 years, and different start and end points), so the tradition is not stable.","Dionysus and Heracles are Greek interpretatio graeca labels. Their Indian equivalents (Shiva, Indra, Krishna, Balarama) are debated, and the sources do not state a Krishna date.","Megasthenes' text is lost and survives only second-hand.","The figure does not fit the archaeological record, which shows no Mathura urban culture of 6000+ BCE. Most scholars place the Mahabharata's historical core in the first millennium BCE, and its compilation c. 400 BCE-400 CE."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Arrian, Indica 8-9 (Megasthenes fragments)","Pliny the Elder, Natural History 6.21","Strabo, Geography 15.1.58","J.W. McCrindle, Ancient India as Described by Megasthenes and Arrian (1877)","Wikipedia: Megasthenes"]},{"id":"mb-jain-harivamsha-neminatha","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Jinasena I (Punnata Sangha, Digambara), author of the Harivamsha Purana","proposerInfo":"8th-century Digambara Jain monk-poet who completed his Sanskrit Harivamsha Purana in 783 CE at Vardhamanapura (usually identified with Wadhwan, Gujarat).","event":"Era of Krishna and the Pandava-Kaurava war, placed via the 22nd Tirthankara Neminatha (Arishtanemi), Krishna's first cousin. The text gives no calendar date for the war.","dateLabel":"No explicit date. Implied by Jain Tirthankara intervals as roughly 84,000 years before Parshvanatha (c. 877 BCE traditional), i.e. about -85000. The figure is a rough derived value, not something Jinasena states as the date of the war.","year":-85000,"yearLow":-85000,"yearHigh":-900,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Harivamsha Purana (Harivamsapurana) by Jinasena, 783 CE (Shaka 705)","workYear":783,"summary":"Jinasena's Harivamsha Purana retells the Yadava (Hari) lineage, Krishna and the Mahabharata war from a Jain viewpoint. Its central religious figure is Neminatha, son of Samudravijaya and cousin of Krishna and Balarama. Jain tradition makes Neminatha the 22nd Tirthankara, before Parshvanatha (23rd) and Mahavira (24th). The traditional interval between Nemi and Parshva is very long (about 83,750 years in Digambara reckoning, about 84,000 or more in Svetambara reckoning), so the Krishna era falls tens of thousands of years back. The work is a narrative and doctrinal source rather than a dating argument, and any year is inferred from these intervals. Some modern writers treat Nemi as a historical figure shortly before Parshva, which would give a far later date.","keyEvidence":["Yadava genealogy in the text: Samudravijaya is Neminatha's father, and his brother Vasudeva is Krishna's father.","Jain Tirthankara sequence puts Nemi 22nd, immediately before Parshvanatha, with a traditional gap of roughly 84,000 years.","Parshvanatha is conventionally placed c. 877-777 BCE and Mahavira c. 599 or 540 BCE, giving the anchor for the implied date.","No planetary or astronomical data is used to date the war."],"buildsOn":["Earlier Jain Puranic tradition of the Hari/Yadava lineage","Jain Tirthankara chronologies (Digambara and Svetambara)"],"critiques":["The tens-of-thousands-of-years interval is cosmological and schematic, with no archaeological support.","Jinasena composed in 783 CE, about two millennia after any plausible war date, so he is a late tradition rather than a witness.","The Jain account differs from the Vyasa Mahabharata in details and presents Krishna and the Pandavas through a Jain lens.","Historians such as Jacobi treated Nemi as possibly historical but did not accept the long gap, so the Jain framework does not yield a usable absolute date."],"plausibility":1,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Harivamsha Purana of Jinasena (783 CE), Bharatiya Jnanpith edition","Wikipedia: Neminatha","Wikipedia: Jinasena","Wikipedia: Harivamsa Purana (Jain)"]},{"id":"mb-buddhist-jataka-kuru","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Pali Buddhist canon and commentaries, as cited by scholars (T.W. Rhys Davids, H.C. Raychaudhuri, F.E. Pargiter)","proposerInfo":"Not a single proposer. Indologists and historians of ancient India who use Pali Jataka and Nikaya references to the Kurus as a relative terminus ante quem.","event":"Existence of the Kuru dynasty and the Yudhitthila (Yudhishthira) lineage before the Buddha. This is a relative terminus ante quem for the war and epic tradition, not an absolute date for the war.","dateLabel":"Before the Buddha (c. 5th-6th century BCE); relative placement only, plotted at c. 500 BCE as an upper bound","year":-500,"yearLow":-600,"yearHigh":-400,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Pali Jatakas (e.g. Kurudhamma Jataka 276, Dhumakari Jataka 413, Vidhurapandita Jataka 545) and Nikayas; discussed in Raychaudhuri, Political History of Ancient India (1923)","workYear":1923,"summary":"Pali Buddhist texts present the Kuru kingdom with its capital Indapatta (Indraprastha) as an existing, already-declining polity. Jataka stories name a Kuru king Dhananjaya Koravya of the Yudhitthila-gotta, i.e. of the Yudhishthira lineage. The Majjhima Nikaya also places the Buddha in Kuru towns such as Kammasadamma. Since these texts belong to the Buddhist period, scholars infer that the Kuru and Yudhishthira tradition predates the Buddha (c. 5th century BCE). This gives only a lower bound on the epic's antiquity and does not date the war itself.","keyEvidence":["Jataka references to a Yudhitthila-gotta king Dhananjaya Koravya ruling at Indapatta in the Kuru country (Kurudhamma, Dhumakari, Vidhurapandita Jatakas)","Kuru towns such as Kammasadamma appear in Nikaya discourses given by the Buddha","Names of Pandava-related figures appear in Jataka verse layers, though not the full Mahabharata story"],"buildsOn":["Rhys Davids","H.C. Raychaudhuri","F.E. Pargiter"],"critiques":["Gives only a relative terminus ante quem, not a date for the war.","The Jatakas were compiled and written down centuries after the Buddha, and the commentary prose is later still; the older verse layers are hard to date.","The Jatakas mention a Kuru king of the Yudhitthila clan but do not narrate the Kurukshetra war, so they show a dynastic memory, not the epic as we have it.","The Pali sources show the Kurus as a minor, declining power, which fits a war well before the Buddha but cannot fix how long before."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["H.C. Raychaudhuri, Political History of Ancient India (1923)","F.E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition (1922)","T.W. Rhys Davids, Buddhist India (1903)","Pali Jataka collection, nos. 276, 413, 545"]},{"id":"mb-bb-lal-hastinapura-900-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"B.B. Lal (with H.D. Sankalia and Suraj Bhan as related archaeological voices)","proposerInfo":"Braj Basi Lal (1921-2022), former Director General of the Archaeological Survey of India; excavated Hastinapura (1950-52) and other epic-linked sites.","event":"Kurukshetra war / Mahabharata-era Hastinapura (Painted Grey Ware culture and the Ganga flood that ended the settlement)","dateLabel":"c. 900 BCE (archaeological range c. 1000-800 BCE; Hastinapura flood layer after PGW, c. 800 BCE)","year":-900,"yearLow":-1000,"yearHigh":-800,"method":"Archaeological","work":"B.B. Lal, \"Excavation at Hastinapura and other explorations in the Upper Ganga and Sutlej basins 1950-52\", Ancient India 10-11 (1954-55); later restated in The Sarasvati Flows On (2002)","workYear":1954,"summary":"Lal excavated Hastinapura and found Period II with Painted Grey Ware (PGW), later followed by a Period III with Northern Black Polished Ware, with evidence of heavy Ganga flood erosion between them. The Puranas say King Nichakshu moved the capital from Hastinapura to Kausambi after the Ganga washed it away, so Lal tied the flood to the end of the PGW occupation, around 800 BCE. Allowing a few generations before Nichakshu, the war falls around 900 BCE. Sankalia independently favoured roughly 900-1000 BCE, and Suraj Bhan's Kurukshetra work put local PGW near 1000 BCE. The specific figures \"900-836 BCE\" could not be confirmed as Lal's own wording, and the dates are approximate.","keyEvidence":["Hastinapura Period II: Painted Grey Ware with early iron and mud-brick or wattle structures","Flood-eroded deposits at Hastinapura, matched with the Puranic story of Nichakshu moving to Kausambi","Puranic genealogy counted from Parikshit to Nichakshu","PGW radiocarbon dates from Upper Ganga-Yamuna sites, roughly 1100/1000-600 BCE","Suraj Bhan's Kurukshetra excavations showing PGW levels"],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists (Nichakshu shifts capital to Kausambi)","F.E. Pargiter's genealogical method","H.D. Sankalia","Mortimer Wheeler-era ceramic chronology"],"critiques":["The flood layer at Hastinapura was later dated to Period III, after PGW, so it does not directly date the war","PGW is not clearly identified with the Mahabharata; the match rests on the late Puranic tradition","Puranic genealogies have uneven reign lengths and gaps, so generation counting is imprecise","Astronomical daters such as Oak, Vartak and Achar argue for much earlier dates","Archaeology cannot confirm a single war event, and the Mahabharata describes a wider material culture than PGW"],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Lal, B.B. (1954-55) Excavation at Hastinapura, Ancient India 10-11","Lal, B.B. (2002) The Sarasvati Flows On, Aryan Books","Sankalia, H.D. Prehistory and Protohistory of India and Pakistan","Suraj Bhan, Excavations at Mitathal and Kurukshetra (1975)"]},{"id":"mb-sinauli-chariots-2000-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Sanjay Kumar Manjul (ASI excavation team), with wide popular/media amplification","proposerInfo":"Archaeologist, then Director of the Institute of Archaeology, Archaeological Survey of India; led the 2018 Sinauli (Baghpat, UP) excavation.","event":"Archaeological context popularly linked to the Mahabharata-era (Kuru/Vedic) warrior culture; chariot burials","dateLabel":"c. 2200-1800 BCE (Sinauli burials; popularly equated with the Mahabharata age)","year":-2000,"yearLow":-2200,"yearHigh":-1800,"method":"Archaeological","work":"ASI Sinauli excavation (2018 season; earlier 2005-06 season), press statements and media coverage 2018-2019","workYear":2018,"summary":"The ASI's 2018 excavation at Sinauli in Baghpat district, Uttar Pradesh, uncovered eight burials with wooden coffins, copper-covered fittings, antennae-hilted swords, shields, helmet-like items and three solid-wheeled carts or chariots. The finds were dated by the team to roughly 2200-1800 BCE, later Harappan to post-Harappan in its framing. Manjul and the media presented the site as a distinct warrior culture that might be linked to the Mahabharata or Vedic tradition. The claim is stronger in media framing than in formal excavation reports: the site is a Mahabharata-era marker only if one accepts a very early Mahabharata date. The date is archaeological and does not come from the epic's text.","keyEvidence":["Eight coffin burials, with copper-plated anthropomorphic motifs on the coffin lids","Three two-wheeled vehicles with solid disc wheels, described as chariots or carts","Antennae-hilted copper swords, shields and daggers","Associated pottery of the Late Harappan or Ochre Coloured Pottery horizon","The site is in the Kuru region near the Hastinapura area (upper Ganga-Yamuna doab)","Dating based on stratigraphy, material culture and comparison, with a radiocarbon framework still debated"],"buildsOn":["Earlier Sinauli excavation of 2005-06 (about 116 burials)","Traditional equation of the Kuru region (Hastinapura) with the Mahabharata","B. B. Lal's Painted Grey Ware-Hastinapura association (which is the older, much later archaeological link)"],"critiques":["The Mahabharata narrative describes spoked-wheel horse chariots; Sinauli's vehicles have solid wheels and are not clearly horse-drawn.","A cultural link to the Mahabharata is speculative; the date is archaeological and says nothing about the epic's events.","The mainstream archaeological date for Hastinapura and Painted Grey Ware (c. 1200-800 BCE) differs from the Sinauli horizon by close to a millennium.","Several archaeologists and historians cautioned against equating Sinauli with the epic; the Sinauli burials are better read as a regional Late Harappan or Copper Hoard-related culture.","Dating relies partly on style and comparison, and the exact chronology and cultural attribution remain debated."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Wikipedia: Sinauli (archaeological site)","Archaeological Survey of India press releases and media coverage on Sinauli, June 2018","Press coverage by The Hindu, Indian Express and BBC, 2018-2019"]},{"id":"mb-sr-rao-dwarka-1500-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"S.R. (Shikaripura Ranganatha) Rao, with later NIO Goa teams (A.S. Gaur, Sundaresh, Sila Tripati)","proposerInfo":"Rao was an Archaeological Survey of India archaeologist who later led marine archaeology at NIO Goa; Gaur, Sundaresh and Tripati are NIO Goa marine archaeologists who continued the Dwarka work.","event":"Submergence of the Dwarka of Krishna's time, used as an archaeological anchor for the Mahabharata period","dateLabel":"c. 1500 BCE (Rao's date for Dwarka's submergence and Krishna's era); NIO radiocarbon and TL dates later cluster around c. 1500-1000 BCE for the earliest levels, with most submerged structures much younger","year":-1500,"yearLow":-1700,"yearHigh":-1000,"method":"Archaeological","work":"S.R. Rao, The Lost City of Dvaraka (Aditya Prakashan, 1999); earlier reports from Rao's marine excavations at Dwarka and Bet Dwarka (1979 onward); NIO papers by Gaur, Sundaresh and Tripati (Current Science and other journals, 1990s-2000s)","workYear":1999,"summary":"Rao excavated on land at Dwarka and underwater off Dwarka and Bet Dwarka from the late 1970s. He found submerged stone walls and bastion-like structures, along with pottery and inscribed items. He argued these were the Dvaraka of the Mahabharata and Puranas, which the epic says the sea swallowed after Krishna's death. He placed the city's drowning and the Mahabharata-era Krishna around 1500 BCE, linking it to Late Harappan/Late Bronze Age material and a Bet Dwarka seal. NIO Goa teams later re-surveyed the sites and obtained radiocarbon and thermoluminescence dates. They reported occupation from roughly the late 2nd millennium BCE through historical periods, and interpreted the structures as shoreline and harbour features affected by sea-level change. They were more cautious than Rao about equating the finds with the epic city. I am giving these details from memory, so treat specifics as unverified.","keyEvidence":["Submerged stone walls, bastions and anchor-like stone objects off Dwarka, interpreted as a drowned fortified port","Late Harappan or Late Bronze Age style pottery in the lower levels, and an inscribed seal from Bet Dwarka that Rao read as Late Harappan-related","Epic and Puranic tradition (Mausala Parva, Harivamsa) that the sea submerged Dvaraka after Krishna's death","NIO radiocarbon and TL dates on shells, wood and pottery from Bet Dwarka and Dwarka, spanning the late 2nd millennium BCE into the historical period","Gujarat coastal sea-level and shoreline change studies (Gaur and Vora, c. 1999)"],"buildsOn":["H.D. Sankalia (Deccan College, 1963 Dwarka excavations)","Z.D. Ansari and M.S. Mate (1960s-70s Dwarka work)","Puranic and Mahabharata Mausala Parva tradition","Pargiter and conventional Late Bronze Age chronology"],"critiques":["Dating the structures does not date the epic war; the equation of this Dwarka with Krishna's city is an inference, not shown by any inscription naming it.","Later NIO work and independent critics (for example Gaur and colleagues) suggest many submerged structures are historical or medieval, and that some stone features are natural or reused material.","Wood and shell dates are open to reservoir-effect and reuse problems, and the underwater stratigraphy is poor.","Rao's reading of the Bet Dwarka seal as Late Harappan-linked is disputed.","Continuous occupation at Dwarka makes the 'city drowned after Krishna' story hard to isolate archaeologically.","A c. 1500 BCE date sits earlier than the mainstream view of c. 1000-900 BCE for the Kuru/Painted Grey Ware milieu."],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["S.R. Rao, The Lost City of Dvaraka, Aditya Prakashan, 1999","S.R. Rao, Marine Archaeology in India (Publications Division, 1988)","A.S. Gaur and K.H. Vora, Ancient shorelines of Gujarat, Current Science 77(1), 1999","A.S. Gaur, Sundaresh and S. Tripati, NIO Goa reports and papers on Dwarka and Bet Dwarka, 1990s-2000s (exact titles not checked)","H.D. Sankalia, Excavations at Dwarka 1963 (Deccan College)"]},{"id":"mb-witzel-kuru-1000-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Michael Witzel (with parallel mainstream kernels from Kochhar, Upinder Singh, Thapar, Kane, Kosambi, Vincent Smith, Max Muller, Oldenberg)","proposerInfo":"Michael Witzel, Wales Professor of Sanskrit at Harvard; Indologist and Vedic philologist.","event":"Kuru kingdom of the Late Vedic period and the historical kernel of the Bharata (Kurukshetra) conflict","dateLabel":"Kuru kingdom c. 1200-900 BCE; historical kernel c. 1000 BCE (the text itself accreted c. 400 BCE-400 CE)","year":-1000,"yearLow":-1200,"yearHigh":-900,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"The Realm of the Kuru: Origins and Development of the First State in India (1989); Early Sanskritization (1995)","workYear":1995,"summary":"Witzel uses the relative and absolute chronology of Vedic texts to place the Kuru state, the first Indian polity, in the Ganga-Yamuna doab around 1200-900 BCE. Kuru kings such as Parikshit and Janamejaya appear in Atharvaveda and Brahmana literature, which fits the setting of the Bharata story. He treats a Bharata battle around 1000 BCE as the plausible historical kernel, later expanded into a pan-Indian epic. The Mahabharata as a text accreted over roughly 400 BCE to 400 CE. Other scholars reach similar kernels by other routes: Vincent Smith (about 1000 BCE), Thapar and Upinder Singh (10th-9th c. BCE), Kochhar (about 950 BCE from Puranic genealogies), and Max Muller, Oldenberg, Kosambi and Kane for early first-millennium BCE cores with later redaction. Figures for the secondary scholars are from background knowledge and were not checked online.","keyEvidence":["Vedic texts (Atharvaveda, Brahmanas) name Kuru kings Parikshit and Janamejaya, anchoring the Kuru state in the Late Vedic period.","Painted Grey Ware culture in the Kuru-Panchala region (c. 1200-600 BCE) matches the Late Vedic horizon.","Relative sequencing of Vedic texts plus Puranic genealogies suggest a Bharata-era event around the early first millennium BCE."],"buildsOn":["Max Muller","Oldenberg","Vincent Smith","D. D. Kosambi","P. V. Kane","Romila Thapar","Rajesh Kochhar"],"critiques":["Rejects traditional 3102/3138 BCE and planetarium-based 5000+ BCE dates, which conflict with the Vedic and archaeological record.","The Kurukshetra war is not attested in Vedic texts as such, so the kernel is inferred, and the epic is a layered text.","Traditionalists argue the Vedic dating itself (and Aryan migration framework) is circular or contested."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Witzel, M. (1989). The Realm of the Kuru","Witzel, M. (1995). Early Sanskritization: Origin and Development of the Kuru State","Thapar, R. (2013). The Past Before Us","Upinder Singh (2008). A History of Ancient and Early Medieval India"]},{"id":"mb-weber-9th-century-bce","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Albrecht Weber","proposerInfo":"German Indologist (1825-1901), professor of Sanskrit at Berlin; author of Akademische Vorlesungen ueber indische Literaturgeschichte and editor of the White Yajurveda","event":"Historical core of the Kuru-Pandava conflict / earliest Mahabharata kernel (attributed claim, unverified)","dateLabel":"9th century BCE (c. 900-800 BCE) for a possible core; the epic as a whole judged late. Attribution could not be confirmed.","year":-850,"yearLow":-900,"yearHigh":-800,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Akademische Vorlesungen ueber indische Literaturgeschichte (1852; English transl. The History of Indian Literature, 1878); also essays in Indische Studien and on the Ramayana (1870)","workYear":1852,"summary":"Weber, a leading 19th-century philological Indologist, treated the Mahabharata as a long, layered and largely late compilation, with Vedic-era traces (Kuru-Panchala material, Parikshit and Janamejaya in the Brahmanas) hinting at an older heroic nucleus. The specific figure of a 9th-century BCE core could not be traced to a passage in his works; it may be a later paraphrase or a reconstruction from Vedic-literature chronology. He offered no astronomical date. His better-documented positions are that the epic reached its present form late and that foreign (Greek or Christian) influences touched parts of Indian epic and Krishna tradition, views later scholars largely rejected.","keyEvidence":["Kuru and Panchala kings such as Parikshit and Janamejaya attested in Vedic Brahmana literature, used to place a heroic nucleus in the late Vedic period","Linguistic and stylistic layering of the text, indicating long accretion rather than single-date authorship","No planetary or verse-based astronomical argument is known for Weber"],"buildsOn":["Christian Lassen","Horace Hayman Wilson"],"critiques":["The precise 9th-century BCE figure is not securely attributable to Weber; treat as unverified","Weber's Greek-influence theories (for the Ramayana and Krishna cult) were rejected by later scholarship, e.g. Jacobi and Winternitz","Winternitz and Hopkins favored a gradual growth c. 400 BCE to 400 CE, with the war's historical kernel older","Archaeology (Painted Grey Ware Kuru sites c. 1100-600 BCE) is broadly compatible with a 9th-century BCE kernel, but this is circumstantial"],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Weber, A. Akademische Vorlesungen ueber indische Literaturgeschichte, 1852","Weber, A. The History of Indian Literature, transl. Mann and Zachariae, 1878","Winternitz, M. A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1","Hopkins, E. W. The Great Epic of India, 1901"]},{"id":"mb-ravi-prakash-arya-3139","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Ravi Prakash Arya","proposerInfo":"Indian Vedic scholar and publisher/translator of Vedic and epic texts, associated with the Vedic Research Institute and Arya Samaj-linked publishing (affiliation from background knowledge, not independently confirmed).","event":"Kurukshetra war (Mahabharata war)","dateLabel":"c. 3139 BCE (traditional Kali-era reckoning, about 36 years before Kali Yuga began in 3102 BCE; attribution to this proposer unverified)","year":-3139,"yearLow":-3139,"yearHigh":-3138,"method":"Era / epigraphic reckoning","work":"Not identified. No specific book or paper by Ravi Prakash Arya dating the war could be confirmed.","workYear":null,"summary":"The claim places the Mahabharata war around 3139 BCE, which sits in the traditional Indian band of 3138-3139 BCE. That band is derived from the Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE plus the tradition that Krishna died about 36 years after the war. It matches the Yudhishthira-era and Aryabhata-style Kali reckoning favoured in Arya Samaj and traditional circles. I could not verify that this proposer published an independent derivation. The date is most likely an adoption of the traditional chronology rather than a new astronomical computation. Treat the attribution as low confidence.","keyEvidence":["Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE, with the war placed about 36 years earlier (Puranic and Aryabhata tradition)","Traditional Yudhishthira-era / Kali-gata reckoning, which gives 3138-3139 BCE","No planetary-position computation attributable to this proposer was found"],"buildsOn":["Aryabhata (Kali epoch 3102 BCE)","Puranic king-lists and Kali Yuga tradition","Varahamihira / Kalhana-style Yudhishthira era reckonings"],"critiques":["Archaeology does not support a literate, urban epic-age society in the 4th millennium BCE; Painted Grey Ware at Kuru-region sites is dated to about 1200-600 BCE.","The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is widely held to be a retrospective construction of later astronomers, not a contemporary record.","No verifiable independent method or publication was found for this attribution."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Traditional Kali Yuga epoch 3102 BCE (Aryabhatiya)","Searches were not run, so no source for the proposer's specific claim was confirmed"]},{"id":"mb-composition-winternitz-hopkins","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Moriz Winternitz, E. Washburn Hopkins, J.A.B. van Buitenen, Hermann Jacobi (Western Indological consensus)","proposerInfo":"Western Indologists: Winternitz (Prague), Hopkins (Yale Sanskritist), van Buitenen (Chicago, translator of the Mahabharata), Jacobi (Bonn).","event":"Composition and growth of the Mahabharata text (not the date of the war)","dateLabel":"c. 400 BCE to 400 CE (Winternitz); Hopkins: core c. 400-200 BCE, expanded to c. 200 CE","year":-200,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":400,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Winternitz, Geschichte der indischen Litteratur vol. 1 (1908; English trans. 1927); Hopkins, The Great Epic of India (1901); van Buitenen, The Mahabharata vol. 1 introduction (1973)","workYear":1901,"summary":"Mainstream philological scholarship treats the Mahabharata as a text that accreted over roughly eight centuries, not as one datable composition. Winternitz placed its growth between about the 4th century BCE and the 4th century CE. Hopkins argued for a core Bharata/Pandava epic in about 400-200 BCE, with later Krishnaite and didactic layers reaching roughly final form by about 200 CE. Van Buitenen and Jacobi broadly agree on a span from the late first millennium BCE to the early centuries CE. These dates concern the text, not the historical war, which scholars usually place loosely in the late Vedic period (c. 1000-800 BCE or earlier), if it was historical at all.","keyEvidence":["Language: epic Sanskrit is post-Vedic and partly non-Paninian, with Prakritisms","Panini (c. 4th c. BCE) and Asvalayana grihyasutra mention Bharata/Mahabharata-related names, giving a lower bound","Dio Chrysostom (late 1st-early 2nd c. CE) reports an Indian epic of about 100,000 verses, giving an upper bound for the large form","Inscriptions: a Gupta-era copper plate (c. 5th c. CE) cites the 'Mahabharata of 100,000 verses'","Internal evidence of religious development, such as the Krishna-Vishnu cult and Bhagavad Gita layers, and references to Yavanas, Sakas and Pahlavas"],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber","Adolf Holtzmann Jr. (layered-text theory)","Hermann Oldenberg","Panini and Asvalayana as anchors","Critical Edition (BORI, Pune) later used by van Buitenen"],"critiques":["These are wide ranges that say little about when the war happened; the composition date and the event date are separate questions.","Traditionalist and astronomical daters (Vartak, Oak, Achar and others) reject the late dating as colonial-era and argue the text or its core is far older.","Hopkins's stratigraphy and the Holtzmann-style reconstructions are disputed; the Critical Edition shows a complex, regional transmission history rather than clean layers.","Oral transmission means linguistic dating of a living tradition is unreliable, and some scholars (e.g. Fitzgerald) argue for a more unified composition in a narrower window."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["M. Winternitz, A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1, Calcutta 1927","E. W. Hopkins, The Great Epic of India, New York 1901","J.A.B. van Buitenen, The Mahabharata, vol. 1, Univ. of Chicago Press 1973","H. Jacobi, Das Ramayana (1893) and Mahabharata (1903)","Wikipedia: Mahabharata (Dating and development of the text)"]},{"id":"mb-composition-fitzgerald-brockington","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Mainstream Indological consensus: James L. Fitzgerald, John Brockington, Alf Hiltebeitel","proposerInfo":"Western Sanskritists: Fitzgerald (Brown Univ., translator of MBh books 11-12), Brockington (Univ. of Edinburgh, author of The Sanskrit Epics), Hiltebeitel (George Washington Univ., historian of religions)","event":"Composition of the Mahabharata text, not the war. The three give a written-text window, not a war date.","dateLabel":"Text composed c. 400 BCE to c. 400 CE. Fitzgerald c. 400 BCE-100 CE (as relayed), Brockington 4th c. BCE-4th c. CE, Hiltebeitel c. 200 BCE-100 CE (as relayed). These are scholars' ranges, not a war date.","year":-100,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":400,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Brockington, The Sanskrit Epics (Brill, 1998); Fitzgerald, The Mahabharata vol. 7 (Univ. of Chicago Press, 2004), Introduction; Hiltebeitel, Rethinking the Mahabharata (Univ. of Chicago Press, 2001)","workYear":1998,"summary":"Academic Indology dates the composition of the Mahabharata as a text, not the war, to a long window from roughly the 4th century BCE to the 4th century CE. Brockington sees oral core material from perhaps the 5th-4th c. BCE and a stabilised form by about the 4th c. CE. Fitzgerald treats the main body of the epic as taking shape around the Mauryan to early Gupta era. Hiltebeitel argues for a more compact, largely post-Ashokan composition around the 2nd c. BCE to 1st c. CE, in dialogue with Buddhism and the Mauryan collapse. None of them dates the Bharata war itself. They treat it as a poorly recoverable event, possibly in the early first millennium BCE, behind a much later text. The exact bounds attributed to each scholar in the claim are from the task and not independently checked.","keyEvidence":["Panini (c. 4th c. BCE) mentions Vasudeva and Arjuna as devotional figures but shows no clear knowledge of the epic as a text.","Asvalayana Grhyasutra mentions Bharata and Mahabharata, but its date is itself uncertain.","Greek writer Dio Chrysostom (c. 100 CE) reports an Indian poem of 100,000 verses, which suggests a large epic by then.","Epigraphy: a Gupta-era copper-plate (Khoh, 5th c. CE) mentions a 100,000-verse Mahabharata.","Late linguistic layers, Yavana/Saka/Pahlava references, and Buddhist and Jain polemic are used to argue for late layers.","Critical Edition (Poona, Sukthankar and others) stratification supports a long accretion."],"buildsOn":["Moriz Winternitz","E. W. Hopkins","V. S. Sukthankar","Georg Buhler","Hermann Oldenberg"],"critiques":["Traditionalist and astronomical daters (e.g. Oak, Vartak, Achar) say text-composition dates cannot fix the war date, and argue the war is far earlier.","Some scholars, such as Fitzgerald and Hiltebeitel, dispute the stratigraphy model of Winternitz and Hopkins, so dates are not a settled consensus.","Late-layer evidence only dates specific interpolations, not the epic as a whole. Oral antecedents are undatable.","Hiltebeitel's single-author, post-Ashokan thesis is minority among Indologists."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Brockington, John. The Sanskrit Epics. Brill, 1998.","Fitzgerald, James L. The Mahabharata, vol. 7 (Books 11 and 12), University of Chicago Press, 2004, Introduction.","Hiltebeitel, Alf. Rethinking the Mahabharata. University of Chicago Press, 2001.","Wikipedia: Mahabharata, section on Textual history and structure"]},{"id":"mb-composition-terminus-evidence","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Georg Bühler (with Kielhorn, Winternitz and the mainstream Indological school)","proposerInfo":"Bühler was a German Indologist and epigraphist (Vienna). The wider framework is the standard academic consensus on the epic's textual history.","event":"Composition of the Mahabharata (terminus ante quem for the text's growth to its c.100,000-verse form)","dateLabel":"Text grew over c. 400 BCE to 400 CE. Fixed lower bounds: Panini/Ashvalayana c. 5th-4th c. BCE (epic in some form); Ashvaghosha c. 1st-2nd c. CE; Sankheda grant 532/533 CE for the 100,000-verse (shatasahasri) text. Not a war date.","year":532,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":533,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Bühler, on the Mahabharata's inscriptional references (Indian Antiquary, c. 1892), building on Kielhorn's and Bühler's readings of copper-plate grants. Not independently re-checked here.","workYear":1892,"summary":"This is not a date for the war. It is a set of external citations that fix the latest dates by which the Mahabharata existed and had reached a given size. Panini's grammar (Ashtadhyayi) mentions Vasudeva and Arjuna as objects of worship, and Mahabharata as a compound. Ashvalayana Grihyasutra lists Bharata and Mahabharata among texts offered to in the tarpana. Ashvaghosha (Buddhacarita, Sutralamkara) shows knowledge of the epic. Patanjali's Mahabhashya shows the Krishna and Kaurava-Pandava legends were current. The Khoh copper-plate and the Sankheda grant of 532/533 CE call the work a shatasahasri samhita of 100,000 verses, so the epic had reached full size by then. Together these bracket the growth to roughly 400 BCE to 400 CE, with a terminus ante quem of c. 530 CE for the full text.","keyEvidence":["Panini, Ashtadhyayi 4.3.98 (Vasudeva-Arjunabhyam vun) and the Mahabharata compound: devotion to Vasudeva and Arjuna, and the epic's name already in use","Ashvalayana Grihyasutra 3.4.4: Bharata and Mahabharata named among the texts honoured in a ritual","Patanjali's Mahabhashya (c. 2nd c. BCE): Kamsa-vadha and Bali-bandha narratives and Kaurava-Pandava allusions","Ashvaghosha (c. 1st-2nd c. CE): references to the epic","Khoh copper-plate of Maharaja Sharvanatha, Gupta year 214, is often cited; the Sankheda grant dated 532/533 CE names the Mahabharata as a 100,000-verse text. Exact plate details and years come from background knowledge and are not rechecked."],"buildsOn":["Georg Bühler","Franz Kielhorn","Moriz Winternitz","Panini","Patanjali"],"critiques":["Gives only a terminus ante quem, not the date of the war or of the first composition.","References by Panini and in the Grihyasutras may be to an earlier, shorter Bharata, not the full epic.","The dating of Panini, Patanjali and Ashvalayana is itself debated, so the lower bounds are soft.","Traditionalists such as Oak, Vartak and Achar argue the epic's core is far older, and reject the idea of a gradual accretion."],"plausibility":9,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Winternitz, A History of Indian Literature, Vol. 1","Bühler, G., Indian Antiquary (1892) on Mahabharata inscriptional references","Fleet, Corpus Inscriptionum Indicarum vol. 3 (Gupta inscriptions, incl. Khoh and Sankheda grants)","Hiltebeitel, The Ritual of Battle / Rethinking the Mahabharata"]},{"id":"mb-composition-sukthankar-holtzmann-dahlmann","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"V. S. Sukthankar (BORI Critical Edition), Adolf Holtzmann Jr., Joseph Dahlmann","proposerInfo":"Three philological schools: Sukthankar (Indologist, general editor of the BORI Critical Edition, Pune), Holtzmann Jr. (German Sanskritist), Dahlmann (German Jesuit Indologist).","event":"Composition and textual growth of the Mahabharata (text formation, not the war)","dateLabel":"Text grew c. 400 BCE to c. 400 CE (mainstream philological range, approximate); Dahlmann argued for an earlier unitary composition","year":-200,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":400,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Holtzmann Jr., Zur Geschichte und Kritik des Mahabharata (1892-93); Dahlmann, Das Mahabharata als Epos und Rechtsbuch (1895); Sukthankar, Prolegomena to the Adiparvan critical edition (1933)","workYear":1933,"summary":"These are three competing philological models of how the epic text arose, not dates of the war. Holtzmann Jr. proposed layers: an older heroic epic sympathetic to the Kauravas, later reworked by Pandava and Vaishnava partisans. Dahlmann held the epic was largely a unitary, deliberately didactic and legal work by one early author, with limited later additions. Sukthankar and the BORI editors collated manuscripts to reconstruct the oldest recoverable archetype. They distrusted speculative stratification and treated the epic as a continuously growing tradition. The date range is the common scholarly consensus (e.g. Winternitz, Hopkins), not a figure I could fix from these three authors; it is from background knowledge.","keyEvidence":["Holtzmann Jr.: inconsistent sympathies and Krishna's divine status taken as signs of revision","Dahlmann: thematic coherence and dharma/legal-text character argued as evidence of a single plan","Sukthankar: manuscript collation yielding a Northern and Southern recension and a reconstructed archetype"],"buildsOn":["Adolf Holtzmann Sr.","Hermann Oldenberg","E. W. Hopkins","Moriz Winternitz"],"critiques":["Holtzmann's layer model is considered speculative and has little manuscript support","Dahlmann's unitary view is largely rejected given linguistic and doctrinal diversity","The critical edition reconstructs an archetype, not the original epic, and says nothing about the war's date"],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Sukthankar, V. S. (1933). Prolegomena, Adiparvan, BORI, Pune","Holtzmann, A. Jr. (1892-93). Zur Geschichte und Kritik des Mahabharata","Dahlmann, J. (1895). Das Mahabharata als Epos und Rechtsbuch"]},{"New":"x","id":"mb-composition-vyasa-traditional","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Indian Puranic and epic tradition (Vyasa as author; no single modern proposer)","proposerInfo":"Traditional attribution to Krishna Dvaipayana Vyasa, upheld by Puranic and pandit tradition and by modern traditionalist writers","event":"Composition of the Mahabharata (Jaya/Bharata) by Vyasa shortly after the war","dateLabel":"c. 3100 BCE (shortly after the traditional war date of c. 3138 BCE and the Kali Yuga start of 3102 BCE)","year":-3100,"yearLow":-3138,"yearHigh":-3000,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Mahabharata itself (Adi Parva, Anukramanika and Parvasamgraha sections); Puranas (Vishnu, Bhagavata); Aryabhatiya (c. 499 CE) for the Kali epoch","workYear":null,"summary":"The tradition holds that Vyasa, an eyewitness and contemporary of the Pandavas, composed the epic soon after the war, which tradition places near the start of Kali Yuga (3102 BCE, with 3138 BCE also cited for the war). The text describes growth in stages: a core Jaya (traditionally about 8,800 verses), an expanded Bharata (about 24,000), then the 100,000-verse Mahabharata recited by Vaisampayana to Janamejaya and later by Ugrasravas to Saunaka. Adi Parva says Vyasa composed it over about three years and taught it to his son Suka and to disciples such as Vaisampayana. The c. 3100 BCE date is an inference from the war date and the Kali epoch, not an independently measured date. Modern scholarship rejects it, treating the work as accreting from roughly 400 BCE to 400 CE, though some traditionalists accept a layered process with a very early core.","keyEvidence":["Internal claim in Adi Parva that Vyasa composed the epic, with the three-stage Jaya, Bharata, Mahabharata growth (8,800, 24,000 and 100,000 verses, as traditionally cited).","Kali Yuga epoch of 3102 BCE, used in the Aryabhatiya and Puranic reckoning, tied to Krishna's departure after the war.","Puranic genealogies that place Janamejaya, the epic's first public reciting, a few generations after the war.","Later external attestations of the epic: Ashvalayana Grihyasutra mentions Bharata and Mahabharata, and the 5th-century CE Khoh copper plate (c. 533 CE) cites a 100,000-verse Mahabharata. These show the text existed by then but do not date it to 3100 BCE."],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists","Aryabhata (Kali epoch 3102 BCE)","Varahamihira and Kalhana (alternative war dates)"],"critiques":["No manuscript, inscription or archaeological evidence for any text in c. 3100 BCE; Indian writing is attested only from Indus seals (undeciphered) and then Ashokan Brahmi (3rd c. BCE).","The Sanskrit of the epic is Epic/Classical Sanskrit, later than Vedic and Brahmanical prose; it cannot be c. 3100 BCE by linguistic stratigraphy.","The epic mentions iron, cavalry, Yavanas and other late features, and the text shows many interpolations and a long accretion history (Critical Edition, BORI Pune, Sukthankar).","The 3102 BCE Kali epoch is a back-calculated astronomical convention from the siddhantic period (c. 5th c. CE), not a contemporary record.","Mainstream scholars (e.g. Winternitz, Hopkins, Fitzgerald) date composition from c. 400 BCE to 400 CE, with an oral core perhaps earlier."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Mahabharata, Adi Parva (Critical Edition, BORI, Pune)","Aryabhatiya, Kalakriyapada (Kali epoch 3102 BCE)","Winternitz, M. A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1","Hiltebeitel, A. The Ritual of Battle / Rethinking the Mahabharata","Fitzgerald, J. Introduction to the Mahabharata, Book 11 and 12 translation"]},{"id":"mb-yukteswar-holy-science","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Swami Sri Yukteswar Giri","proposerInfo":"Bengali Kriya Yoga guru (1855-1936) and guru of Paramahansa Yogananda, with some astronomy and Sanskrit training; wrote The Holy Science.","event":"Yuga-cycle reckoning (descending Kali starting about 700 BCE); not a Kurukshetra war date","dateLabel":"Descending Kali from ~701 BCE (to 499 CE); descending Dwapara ~3101-701 BCE. A 24,000-year precessional cycle, so this is a yuga proposal and not a war date. The year -700 is the implied Kali start.","year":-700,"yearLow":-3101,"yearHigh":-700,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"The Holy Science (Kaivalya Darsanam), Serampore, 1894","workYear":1894,"summary":"Yukteswar rejects the 4.32 million-year yuga scheme and the 3102 BCE Kali start. He treats the yugas as phases of a 24,000-year cycle linked to the precession of the equinoxes: 12,000 years descending and 12,000 ascending. In his scheme the lowest point (Kali) was about 499 CE, the ascending Dwapara began about 1699 CE, and the descending Kali began about 700 BCE. He says Indian astrologers lost the true count after the time of Parikshit. As I recall, he places Yudhishthira's age in the descending Dwapara. He gives no war year and uses no planetary positions. This card is therefore context for the yuga debate and not a rival dating of the war.","keyEvidence":["Cycle of 24,000 years tied to precession, with the sun moving about a companion star (his own model, not mainstream astronomy)","Yuga lengths of 1,200 (Kali), 2,400 (Dwapara), 3,600 (Treta) and 4,800 (Satya) years per arc, with junction periods","Claims the long yuga counts came from later misreading of Manu and the Puranas after Parikshit","Equinox position around 499 CE used to anchor the lowest point of the cycle","Not verified online in this session; details are from background knowledge"],"buildsOn":["Manusmriti yuga verses","Puranic yuga schemes (which he refutes)","Indian siddhantic precession (ayanamsa) ideas"],"critiques":["The companion-star, 24,000-year model has no support in modern astronomy; precession is about 25,800 years and is not caused by a binary companion","He gives no war date, so it cannot be tested against Mahabharata sky observations or archaeology","The scheme is a reinterpretation of yuga numbers, not derived from the epic's text","Later writers such as Sadhguru's circle and other yoga writers repeat the cycle idea; this is not an independent confirmation"],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Sri Yukteswar, The Holy Science (Kaivalya Darsanam), 1894; Self-Realization Fellowship editions","Paramahansa Yogananda, Autobiography of a Yogi (1946), chapter 16 on the yugas"]},{"id":"mb-skeptics-critics-danino-saranathan-kak","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Michel Danino, Jayasree Saranathan, Subhash Kak, Shrikant Talageri, Georges Dumezil, Abul Fazl (Razmnama)","proposerInfo":"Composite marker row: Indologists, a commentator on Indian astronomy, a computer scientist and Indologist, an independent Indian historian, a French comparative mythologist and Akbar's court chronicler. None gives a firmly argued war date of their own, apart from Abul Fazl's traditional figure.","event":"Kurukshetra war (positions on whether and when it can be dated)","dateLabel":"No firm war date. Only Abul Fazl (1580s) gives a figure, about 4,696 years before his time, which is roughly 3100 BCE and matches the traditional Kali reckoning. The others decline to date it or give only loose ranges.","year":null,"yearLow":null,"yearHigh":null,"method":"Other","work":"Danino, The Invasion That Never Was (1996) and later essays; Dumezil, Mythe et epopee I (1968); Abul Fazl, preface to the Razmnama (c. 1582-86); Talageri, The Rigveda: A Historical Analysis (2000); Kak, various papers on Vedic astronomy","workYear":null,"summary":"This row groups scholars who do not endorse the planetarium-style war dates of Vartak, Oak and others. Dumezil reads the epic as a transposition of Indo-European tri-functional myth, so a historical war date is not his concern. Danino, as I recall, allows a possible historical kernel but says the text grew over centuries, and he gives no precise date. Kak, Talageri and Saranathan have engaged with epic chronology and Vedic astronomy, but I could not confirm one specific war date for any of them. Abul Fazl is the only one with an explicit figure. His Razmnama preface, following the Brahmin Kali-era tradition, puts the war about 4,696 years before his time, close to the traditional 3138-3102 BCE. Treat this card as a placeholder for these markers, not a single dating.","keyEvidence":["Dumezil: structural and comparative mythology (the three functions) in Mythe et epopee, which treats the war as mythic transposition","Danino: the epic's long, layered composition and the lack of firm archaeological correlates for a single war","Abul Fazl: the Razmnama preface records a Brahmin-tradition count of about 4,696 years from the war (unverified figure, from memory)","Talageri: relative chronology from Rigvedic and Puranic king-lists, with no precise war year confirmed here","Kak: Vedic astronomy and the reliability of epic astronomical data, with no confirmed war date"],"buildsOn":["Traditional Kali-yuga reckoning (Abul Fazl)","Indo-European comparative mythology (Dumezil)","Puranic genealogies (Talageri)"],"critiques":["This is a grouped, non-dating row, so it is not a single testable claim","Positions for Saranathan, Kak and Talageri on a specific war date could not be verified and are stated cautiously","Abul Fazl's number comes from a traditional count and is not independent evidence","Dumezil's mythic reading is contested by scholars who see a historical core"],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Michel Danino, The Invasion That Never Was (1996)","Georges Dumezil, Mythe et epopee I (1968)","Abul Fazl, Razmnama preface (c. 1580s); Ain-i-Akbari","Shrikant Talageri, The Rigveda: A Historical Analysis (2000)"]},{"id":"mb-manish-pandit-documentary","epic":"Mahabharata","proposer":"Dr. Manish Pandit (identity unverified)","proposerInfo":"Documentary presenter named in the task; I could not verify who this is, their field or their affiliation.","event":"Kurukshetra war (presumed; not verified)","dateLabel":"Unknown - documentary and claimed date could not be found or verified","year":null,"yearLow":null,"yearHigh":null,"method":"Other","work":"Unverified documentary attributed to Dr. Manish Pandit (title and year not found)","workYear":null,"summary":"I could not find or verify any Mahabharata dating documentary by a Dr. Manish Pandit. The one search I ran was blocked by a bot-check, and I have no reliable background knowledge of this work. I am not stating a date, method or evidence, to avoid inventing them. This entry is a placeholder. It needs a manual check of YouTube or documentary listings for the exact title, date and method.","keyEvidence":[],"buildsOn":[],"critiques":["Cannot be assessed: no verified claim exists to critique."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Not found","references":[]},{"id":"rm-bala-isserve-5114-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra (I-SERVE)","proposerInfo":"Saroj Bala, Indian Revenue Service officer (Income Tax) and Vedic-chronology researcher; Kulbhushan Mishra, I-SERVE (Institute of Scientific Research on Vedas), Delhi.","event":"Birth of Rama (Ayodhya)","dateLabel":"10 January 5114 BCE (around noon, Ayodhya)","year":-5114,"yearLow":-5114,"yearHigh":-5114,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Papers and presentations by I-SERVE on the date of Rama's birth (commonly cited as 'Date of Ramayana' / 'Dating the Era of Lord Rama'; exact titles and years not confirmed). Possibly also Bala and Mishra's I-SERVE publications around 2007-2018.","workYear":2013,"summary":"Bala and Mishra take the planetary configuration Valmiki gives for Rama's birth (Bala Kanda 1.18.8-9) and search for the date when it recurs, using Planetarium software. They find 10 January 5114 BCE, at about 12:30 pm in Ayodhya. They place the Sun in Aries, Mars in Capricorn, Saturn in Libra, Venus in Pisces, and Jupiter with the Moon in Cancer with Cancer rising. The Moon is in Punarvasu on the ninth tithi of the bright fortnight, which is Chaitra Shukla Navami. They explain the January date by arguing that the Hindu month and season correspondence has shifted with precession since then. Most of this is from background knowledge, not from sources I could open, so treat exact titles and years with caution.","keyEvidence":["Valmiki Ramayana 1.18.8-9: Chaitra month, Shukla Navami, Punarvasu nakshatra, Cancer lagna, five planets exalted, Jupiter with the Moon in Cancer","Exaltation positions: Sun in Aries, Mars in Capricorn, Saturn in Libra, Venus in Pisces, Jupiter in Cancer","Planetarium software match yielding 10 Jan 5114 BCE near midday","Claimed support from other Ramayana astronomical references (e.g. the Sun and planets during the exile and the war)"],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana (Bala Kanda 1.18)","P. V. Vartak (Ramayana astronomy)","Pushkar Bhatnagar (Dating the Era of Lord Rama, 7323 BCE, Voyager/Skyglobe-style approach)","Planetarium-software tradition in Indian chronology"],"critiques":["Rama's birth is in Chaitra (spring, 'madhu masa'), yet 10 January is mid-winter. The precession explanation is contested because it requires redefining the month-season link.","Planetarium matching of exaltation positions is not unique. Similar configurations recur, and different authors get different dates (Bhatnagar 7323 BCE, others about 4400 BCE or 1000 BCE).","5114 BCE predates any archaeological evidence for urban Ayodhya (earliest layers roughly 1000-700 BCE, per excavations by B.B. Lal and others). It also falls long before the Iron Age material the epic describes.","Treats the Balakanda verse as a literal observation, although the text is a later composite.","Attributes dating details to Bhatnagar's work; some sources conflate the two authors."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda 1.18.8-9","I-SERVE (Institute of Scientific Research on Vedas) publications by Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra","Pushkar Bhatnagar, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (Rupa, 2004)"]},{"id":"rm-vedveer-arya-5114-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Vedveer Arya (with Saroj Bala / I-SERVE)","proposerInfo":"Vedveer Arya: Indian researcher associated with I-SERVE (Institute of Scientific Research on Vedas); Saroj Bala is a retired Indian Revenue Service officer and I-SERVE researcher. Details not independently verified.","event":"Birth of Rama (Chaitra Shukla Navami, Punarvasu nakshatra, Cancer lagna)","dateLabel":"10 January 5114 BCE (as commonly attributed)","year":-5114,"yearLow":-5114,"yearHigh":-5114,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Not verified. Attributed to I-SERVE work on the Valmiki Ramayana (Saroj Bala and Vedveer Arya) using Planetarium Gold software; exact book/paper title and year could not be confirmed.","workYear":null,"summary":"The date is attributed to Vedveer Arya working with Saroj Bala under I-SERVE. It uses the Valmiki Ramayana (Bala Kanda 1.18.8-10) description of planets at Rama's birth: Sun in Mesha, Saturn in Tula, Jupiter in Karka with the Moon, Venus in Meena, Mars in Makara, Punarvasu nakshatra and Navami tithi. These positions are searched in Planetarium Gold software, which yields 10 January 5114 BCE at about 12:30 pm in Ayodhya. It is mostly one joint I-SERVE result rather than an independent study. It is also the reasoning that Nilesh Oak and Bala-linked circles popularised. I could not confirm that Arya's work is independent of Bala's.","keyEvidence":["Valmiki Ramayana Bala Kanda 1.18.8-10 planetary configuration at Rama's birth","Planetarium Gold software matching of Sun, Moon, Jupiter, Saturn, Venus and Mars positions","Additional sky references in the epic (e.g. Vanavasa-era eclipses and Mars/Saturn positions) used as cross-checks, per proponents"],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","Saroj Bala","P. V. Vartak","Pushkar Bhatnagar (similar software method, different date)"],"critiques":["Software searches of a handful of planetary placements can produce matches in many eras, so uniqueness is weak and depends on how Rashi sign conventions (sidereal vs tropical) are set","Verse interpretation is contested, since the verses may be later interpolations","5114 BCE predates any archaeological evidence of a Gangetic urban Ayodhya by millennia","Different practitioners using similar software get very different dates (e.g. 7323 BCE, 4439 BCE)","Likely not independent of Bala, so it should not count as separate corroboration"],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vedveer_Arya (404 - no article found)","I-SERVE website (serveveda.org / iserveveda) - not checked","Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda 1.18.8-10"]},{"id":"rm-bhatnagar-7323-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"P. V. Vartak (7323 BCE is his figure; misattributed to Pushkar Bhatnagar, whose own date is 10 Jan 5114 BCE)","proposerInfo":"Vartak: Marathi author (Swayambhu) arguing for an ancient Vedic chronology. Bhatnagar: US-based software professional and Vedic-chronology researcher.","event":"Birth of Rama (Chaitra Shukla Navami, per Valmiki Ramayana)","dateLabel":"c. 7323 BCE (4 December 7323 BCE per Vartak); not Bhatnagar's figure. Bhatnagar's is 10 January 5114 BCE","year":-7323,"yearLow":-7323,"yearHigh":-7323,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"P. V. Vartak, Swayambhu (Marathi), 1980s. Compare Pushkar Bhatnagar, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (2004).","workYear":1986,"summary":"The 7323 BCE date, with a 4 December day and month, comes from P. V. Vartak's Marathi work Swayambhu. It is often repeated in popular Ramayana-dating lists, sometimes wrongly credited to Pushkar Bhatnagar. Bhatnagar's 2004 work \"Dating the Era of Lord Rama\" used Planetarium software and concluded Rama was born on 10 January 5114 BCE, which is a different date. Both read the Valmiki Ramayana's astrological verses about the planets at Rama's birth (Bala Kanda 18.8-9, five planets exalted, Sun in Mesha, Moon in Punarvasu with Karka rising). Vartak's method rests on his own reading of the sky, and Bhatnagar's date is the software-based one. Because the entry was framed as Bhatnagar's 7323 BCE, treat that attribution as an error. This record was written from memory and could not be checked against the sources.","keyEvidence":["Valmiki Ramayana Bala Kanda 18.8-9: Rama born on Chaitra Shukla Navami, Moon in Punarvasu, five planets exalted, Jupiter with the Moon in Karka lagna.","Vartak's Swayambhu is the origin of the 4 Dec 7323 BCE figure (recalled from memory, not verified).","Bhatnagar's Planetarium-software result of 10 Jan 5114 BCE is widely cited as a separate and competing date."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana astronomical verses","P. V. Vartak (Swayambhu)"],"critiques":["Exaltation of five planets at once with the Sun in Mesha is hard to fit uniquely; the verses are read differently by different scholars.","A date of 7323 BCE predates the Neolithic of the Gangetic plain and the archaeology of Ayodhya, which shows no matching urban culture.","Interpolated astrological verses are suspected, and the late Ramayana text is argued to be centuries rather than millennia old.","Popular lists conflate Vartak's 7323 BCE with Bhatnagar's 5114 BCE, so attributions need checking."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["P. V. Vartak, Swayambhu (Marathi)","Pushkar Bhatnagar, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (2004)","Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda 18.8-9"]},{"id":"rm-vartak-7323-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"P. V. Vartak","proposerInfo":"Marathi-language Indian author on Vedic/epic astronomy and chronology (Pune); known for the book Swayambhu; biographical details not verified","event":"Rama's birth / the Ramayana era","dateLabel":"c. 7323 BCE (as reported; unverified)","year":-7323,"yearLow":-7323,"yearHigh":-7323,"method":"Astronomical (classical / siddhantic)","work":"Vastav Ramayan (Marathi, Swayambhu Prakashan; year not verified)","workYear":null,"summary":"Vartak is reported to place Rama's birth around 7323 BCE, based on his reading of the astronomical references in Valmiki's Ramayana, in the book Vastav Ramayan. He is also known for an earlier Mahabharata dating in Swayambhu, which Nilesh Oak later built on. I could not access the book, so the exact date, the exact event and the method are all unconfirmed. His Ramayana date is much earlier than Pushkar Bhatnagar's c. 5114 BCE, which also uses Valmiki's planetary positions, so the two disagree rather than agree. Treat the figure as low confidence.","keyEvidence":["Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda 1.18 (Rama's birth): the sun, Mars, Saturn, Jupiter and Venus are said to be in particular signs, and the moon is in Punarvasu in Chaitra Shukla Navami. His specific reading of these is not verified.","Reportedly his own reading of the Ramayana's sky observations, for example the sage's account of the planets at Rama's birth. The details are not verified.","Reportedly fixes the date by working backward through the Puranic king-lists and the precession of the equinoxes. Not verified."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","Puranic genealogies"],"critiques":["The Ramayana's astronomical verses are few and ambiguous. Different readers get dates thousands of years apart, for example about 7323 BCE here against about 5114 BCE for Bhatnagar.","A date of 7000 BCE or earlier precedes any known urban or iron-age culture in the Gangetic plain. This conflicts with the archaeological record, which the Ramayana's descriptions of cities, chariots and metal tools suggest were much later.","Self-published, Marathi-only work with little independent peer review."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Vartak, P. V., Vastav Ramayan (Marathi), Swayambhu Prakashan (bibliographic details unverified)","Vartak, P. V., Swayambhu (Marathi)","Bhatnagar, P., Planetarium Software dating of Rama's birth (c. 5114 BCE)"]},{"id":"rm-oak-7000-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Nilesh Oak","proposerInfo":"Nilesh Oak, US-based engineer and independent researcher (not a professional historian or astronomer) who dates the epics from astronomical references, and is best known for the 5561 BCE Mahabharata date.","event":"Era of Rama (Rama's life and the Ramayana events), from astronomical references in Valmiki Ramayana","dateLabel":"Uncertain. The brief's \"c. 7000 BCE\" could not be confirmed. My recollection is that Oak places Rama much earlier, in the 12th-13th millennium BCE (roughly 12,000+ BCE). Plotted at about 12,000 BCE as a placeholder.","year":-12000,"yearLow":-12500,"yearHigh":-7000,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Dating the Era of Lord Rama (Oak; I recall a 2011 or 2012 date, not the 2016 in the brief)","workYear":2011,"summary":"Nilesh Oak applies his Mahabharata method to the Valmiki Ramayana. He collects the sky observations the text records, mainly planetary and nakshatra positions at Rama's birth and during the exile, and searches planetarium software (Stellarium, Planetarium Gold) for the epoch where they all match. He treats the text as a record of real observations. I could not check the exact figure, the title or the year with web tools in this session. My recollection is that his answer is far earlier than the Ramayana dates of Bhatnagar (5114 BCE) and Vartak. The \"c. 7000 BCE\" in the brief should be treated as unverified and probably wrong. Please check Oak's book or his talks before publishing.","keyEvidence":["Planetary positions given for Rama's birth in Valmiki Ramayana Bala Kanda (Sun, Mars, Saturn, Jupiter, Venus, plus the Moon in Punarvasu)","Sky observations during the exile and the Lanka war (e.g. omens, an eclipse-like or comet-like phenomenon, Mars and other planets near particular nakshatras)","Software matching of the full set of observations to one epoch, as in his Mahabharata work (Arundhati-Vasishtha reversal and others)"],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","Pushkar Bhatnagar (planetarium dating of Rama to 5114 BCE)","P.V. Vartak","Oak's own Mahabharata dating (5561 BCE)"],"critiques":["Many Ramayana sky references are poetic or omen-like, so they are weak as observational data.","Interpolations and manuscript variants make the choice of verses subjective.","A date of 12,000 BCE or earlier precedes the Indian Neolithic and urban archaeology, with no matching material evidence for Ayodhya or Lanka.","Matching several loosely specified positions can fit more than one epoch, and the choices are made after the fact.","Mainstream scholars hold that the Ramayana was composed centuries BCE, not a record of events from millennia earlier."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Nilesh Oak, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (verify edition and year)","Nilesh Oak, When Did the Mahabharata War Happen? The Mystery of Arundhati (2011)","Pushkar Bhatnagar, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (the same title is used for his work; check which is meant)"]},{"id":"rm-oak-12209-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Nilesh Oak","proposerInfo":"US-based engineer (not a trained historian or Sanskritist) who dates the epics by matching in-text astronomical observations to planetarium-software sky simulations.","event":"Birth of Rama","dateLabel":"c. 12209 BCE (Rama's birth, per Oak's Ramayana sky-observation analysis)","year":-12209,"yearLow":-12209,"yearHigh":-12209,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"The Historic Rama (Oak's Ramayana dating work, 2020s; book title and year not independently confirmed here)","workYear":2021,"summary":"Nilesh Oak extends his Mahabharata method (his Kurukshetra war date is 5561 BCE) to the Valmiki Ramayana. He collects the astronomical observations the text reports, such as the planetary and zodiac placements at Rama's birth, and searches a planetarium program for the one time window in which they all hold. He reports that Rama was born around 12209 BCE, far earlier than the traditional Treta Yuga reckoning or the roughly 5100 BCE date proposed by P.V. Vartak and Pushkar Bhatnagar. I could not verify this from sources in this session. The year and the book title come from background knowledge and should be checked.","keyEvidence":["Valmiki Ramayana Bala Kanda verse describing the planetary positions at Rama's birth (Chaitra Shukla Navami, Punarvasu or Cancer rising, with the Sun, Saturn, Jupiter, Mars and Venus in exalted positions). Oak's matching of these is claimed, but the specific verses were not checked here.","Other in-text sky observations, such as those from the Aranya and Kishkindha Kandas, used as cross-checks. Details unverified.","Software-based search for a unique match across tens of thousands of years, the same approach as his Mahabharata work."],"buildsOn":["P. V. Vartak","Pushkar Bhatnagar (Dr. P.V. Vartak and Bhatnagar used Planetarium software for ~5114 BCE)","Valmiki Ramayana (critical edition)"],"critiques":["No archaeological support: there is no urban, iron-using or even Neolithic-agricultural civilisation in the Ganga plain around 12000 BCE, which falls in the Late Pleistocene and the end of the last Ice Age. The Ayodhya and Lanka descriptions of cities and palaces do not fit that period.","Date conflicts with the textual and linguistic evidence that places the Ramayana's composition in the first millennium BCE, with later Gupta-era layers.","Fitting a set of verses to a planetarium match can be done for many windows if verse readings are chosen selectively. Mainstream scholars also doubt that the verses are literal observations rather than poetic or astrological conventions.","Different Indian scholars using the same method get different dates, e.g. about 5114 BCE (Vartak, Bhatnagar), which weakens the claim that the match is unique.","The date is incompatible with the Puranic genealogies, which put Rama only a few dozen generations before the Mahabharata war."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Nilesh Oak, When Did the Mahabharata War Happen? The Mystery of Arundhati (2011) - his Mahabharata method, 5561 BCE","Nilesh Oak, The Historic Rama (title and year need checking against a bookseller listing or Oak's own site)","Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda (planetary positions at Rama's birth)","P. V. Vartak, Swayambhu / Ramayana dating (about 5114 BCE), for contrast"]},{"id":"rm-ganatra-5114-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Ami Ganatra","proposerInfo":"Indian popular speaker/author on the Ramayana (podcasts and talks); not a professional astronomer or archaeologist. Specific credentials not verified.","event":"Rama's birth / the Ramayana era (popularised, not independently computed)","dateLabel":"c. 5114 BCE (about 7,000 years ago), as adopted from Bala/Arya; not an independent date","year":-5114,"yearLow":-5114,"yearHigh":-5114,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Talks and podcast appearances on the Ramayana, c. 2021-24 (specific episodes not verified; the title 'Ramayana Unravelled' could not be confirmed)","workYear":2022,"summary":"Ami Ganatra is described as presenting Rama as about 7,000 years old. This matches the 5114 BCE date (10 January 5114 BCE, Rama's birth on Chaitra Shukla Navami) computed by Pushkar Bhatnagar (Planetarium software) and popularised by Vedveer Arya and others. I could not verify that she proposes her own calculation. The most likely reading is that she cites the 5114 BCE result as a working date and focuses on narrative and cultural exposition. Treat this entry as a secondary or popularising citation, not an original dating. No web verification was done.","keyEvidence":["The 5114 BCE date rests on the Valmiki Ramayana verses (Bala Kanda 1.18.8-10) giving Sun in Mesha, Saturn in Tula, Jupiter in Karka, Venus in Meena, Mars in Makara, and Moon in Punarvasu at Rama's birth","These positions are matched by planetarium software (Planetarium Gold / Stellarium), which gives 10 Jan 5114 BCE in Pushkar Bhatnagar's computation","Ganatra's own contribution, if any, is not documented in anything I could verify"],"buildsOn":["Pushkar Bhatnagar (Dating the Era of Lord Rama, 2004)","Vedveer Arya","Subhash Kak"],"critiques":["The date is not original to her, so the critiques apply to the underlying 5114 BCE computation","Sceptics note that the planetary verses may be later interpolations, and that a software match to sparse sky data is easy to find in some year","5114 BCE predates the Indian Chalcolithic and Iron Age cultures by millennia. The archaeology shows no urban or kingdom-level society at Ayodhya at that date. Excavations at Ayodhya show the earliest settlement at about 1000-700 BCE (Northern Black Polished Ware at about 700 BCE).","Mainstream scholars generally place the Ramayana's composition between about 500 and 100 BCE, with its earliest core possibly older"],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Pushkar Bhatnagar, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (2004)","Valmiki Ramayana, Bala Kanda 1.18.8-10","Ami Ganatra podcast and talk appearances (not located or verified)"]},{"id":"rm-project-shivoham","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Project Shivoham (YouTube channel; popularizer, not original dater)","proposerInfo":"Hindu-heritage YouTube channel; no verifiable academic affiliation found","event":"Rama's birth / Ramayana era, with Ram Setu said to be about 7000 years old","dateLabel":"c. 5114 BCE (Rama's birth, borrowed from Pushkar Bhatnagar's planetarium dating); Ram Setu about 7000 years old (popular claim)","year":-5114,"yearLow":-5114,"yearHigh":-5000,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Ramayana videos on the Project Shivoham YouTube channel (specific titles not verified)","workYear":null,"summary":"The channel is reported to follow the 5114 BCE date for Rama's birth and to pair it with the popular claim that Ram Setu is about 7000 years old. The two figures are roughly consistent, since 5114 BCE is about 7100 years before present. The 5114 BCE date originates with Pushkar Bhatnagar, who matched Valmiki's planetary descriptions for Rama's birth using Planetarium Gold software. I could not retrieve the channel's video list (YouTube search returned no usable content), so exact titles and stated dates are unconfirmed. This is a secondary popularizer entry, not an independent dating.","keyEvidence":["Valmiki Ramayana Bala Kanda 1.18.8-9: five planets exalted, Moon in Punarvasu, Cancer rising, Chaitra Shukla Navami at Rama's birth (the verse set used by the software dating)","Ram Setu (Adam's Bridge) age figure of about 7000 years, a popular claim"],"buildsOn":["Pushkar Bhatnagar (5114 BCE)","Popular Ram Setu age claims"],"critiques":["Planetary exaltation patterns are not unique and software results depend on how verses are interpreted.","Geologists generally treat Adam's Bridge as a natural shoal/tombolo, and its 7000-year age claim is not established as human-built evidence.","Archaeology does not support a Rama-era urban Ayodhya in the 5th millennium BCE."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=project+shivoham+ramayana+5114+BCE (fetch returned no usable content)"]},{"id":"rm-praveen-mohan","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Praveen Mohan (YouTube channel \"Praveen Mohan\")","proposerInfo":"Indian independent YouTuber and temple-sculpture explorer (India-based); not an academic archaeologist, astronomer or Sanskritist","event":"Ramayana events (Ram Setu bridge, Rameswaram Rama sites, Sri Lankan Ravana-linked sites) treated as ancient and historical; no explicit date for the war or Rama's birth is asserted","dateLabel":"No explicit date claimed; only an implied \"thousands of years old\" Ramayana (Treta Yuga tradition, if accepted, ~870,000+ years ago)","year":null,"yearLow":null,"yearHigh":null,"method":"Other","work":"YouTube videos on Ram Setu / Adam's Bridge, Rameswaram temples and Sri Lankan Ravana/Ramayana sites (channel \"Praveen Mohan\", c. 2019-2024)","workYear":null,"summary":"Praveen Mohan's videos present Ram Setu, Rameswaram temples and Sri Lankan sites associated with Ravana as physical evidence that the Ramayana has a real, very ancient basis, typically via site visits, photographs of sculptures/geology and discussion of local tradition. From memory, he does not offer a computed calendar date, astronomical derivation or year for Rama; the age is left implied (\"thousands of years\") or deferred to others, such as the traditional Treta Yuga timeline or the NASA-image and geological claims about the causeway. I could not verify specific videos via web access, so I cannot confirm whether any individual video states a number. For the timeline, this is best treated as a non-dating, advocacy-by-site-evidence entry rather than a real dating proposal.","keyEvidence":["Visual and geological discussion of the Ram Setu / Adam's Bridge limestone shoal chain between Pamban and Mannar","Rameswaram temple sites and local Rama traditions (Dhanushkodi, Ramanathaswamy)","Sri Lankan sites linked by tradition to Ravana (e.g. Ravana Ella, Sigiriya-type sites, Sri Lanka's Ramayana Trail)","No verse-level planetary positions or astronomical software output (as used by Vartak, Oak, Achar) are presented as far as I recall"],"buildsOn":["Popular Ram Setu claims about NASA satellite imagery (2002)","Sri Lanka Tourism Ramayana Trail","Traditional Treta Yuga chronology"],"critiques":["No explicit dated claim, so it cannot be tested or placed on an archaeological timeline","Adam's Bridge is a natural chain of shoals; geological studies (e.g. Geological Survey of India and other work) date its sands and corals to a few thousand years or less, and NASA stated its images do not show a man-made bridge","Site-linked tradition is later pilgrimage/localization, not independent evidence for the epic's date","Presenter is not a trained archaeologist; claims are popular-media level and not peer reviewed"],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["https://www.youtube.com/@PraveenMohan","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam%27s_Bridge","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ram_Setu"]},{"id":"rm-jayasree-saranathan","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Jayasree Saranathan","proposerInfo":"Chennai-based independent researcher and writer on Vedic astronomy and Indian history; blogs at jayasreesaranathan.blogspot.com and contributes to Indian heritage websites.","event":"Rama's birth (Chaitra Shukla Navami, Punarvasu), the anchor of the Ramayana chronology","dateLabel":"c. 5114 BCE (Rama's birth; the planetarium-software date from Vartak and Pushkar Bhatnagar, which she is reported to support)","year":-5114,"yearLow":-5114,"yearHigh":-5114,"method":"Astronomical (planetarium software)","work":"Blog posts on Ramayana astronomy and Rama's date, jayasreesaranathan.blogspot.com (undated here; I found no single book)","workYear":null,"summary":"Jayasree Saranathan is usually cited as a supporter and popularizer of the roughly 5114 BCE Ramayana date. That date comes from reading the planetary positions in the Valmiki Ramayana with planetarium software, first by P.V. Vartak and later by Pushkar Bhatnagar. She is better described as an advocate and expositor than as the original computer of the date. I could not open her posts in this session, so I cannot confirm whether she endorses the date unchanged or adjusts it. Treat her stance as endorsement with unverified details.","keyEvidence":["Balakanda verses (1.18.8-9) give the sky at Rama's birth: Sun, Mars, Saturn, Jupiter and Venus in their exaltation signs, Jupiter with the Moon in Cancer, Punarvasu nakshatra, Chaitra Shukla Navami.","Later sky references in the epic (for example in the exile and war books) are used to cross-check the timeline.","Her specific posts and any adjustments were not verified."],"buildsOn":["P.V. Vartak","Pushkar Bhatnagar","Valmiki Ramayana"],"critiques":["Planetarium-software dates rest on how the verses are read. Critics say the exaltation-sign list may be a later literary insertion.","A 5114 BCE Ayodhya has no archaeological support. Excavated Ayodhya layers are far later.","The date conflicts with Puranic king-list counts and with other astronomical proposals for Rama.","Her own position is unverified here."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["https://jayasreesaranathan.blogspot.com","Pushkar Bhatnagar, Dating the Era of Lord Rama (2004)","P.V. Vartak, Vastava Ramayana"]},{"id":"rm-puranic-treta-868k","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Traditional Puranic reckoning (Vishnu, Bhagavata, Vayu Puranas) as repeated by popular preachers","proposerInfo":"Anonymous Puranic tradition; popularised by contemporary kathavachaks, temple/pandit circles and some online Hindu apologetics","event":"Rama's birth and reign (Treta Yuga)","dateLabel":"Late Treta Yuga, c. 867,100-869,000 years before Kali start (popularly \"8+ lakh years ago\"); plotted at about -870,000. Some counts give 24th-Treta figures near 18 million years (1.8 crore), not 1.8 million","year":-870000,"yearLow":-18100000,"yearHigh":-867000,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Vishnu Purana Book 6 ch. 3 (yuga lengths); Bhagavata Purana 3.11 and 12.2; Vayu Purana; popular lectures and articles (no single authoritative work)","workYear":null,"summary":"Puranic cosmology fixes the Dvapara Yuga at 864,000 years and the Kali Yuga at 432,000. Kali is held to have begun in 3102 BCE, so Dvapara ended in 3102 BCE and the Treta before it ended 864,000 years earlier, about 867,100 BCE. Rama is placed late in Treta, so his date is roughly 867,000-869,000 years before the Kali start, or about 870,000 years before present (rounded to \"over 8 lakh years\"). Some preachers instead place Rama in the 24th Treta of the Vaivasvata Manvantara. Counting forward through 27 and 28 mahayugas gives about 18.1 million years, which matches the \"1.81 crore\" figure. The \"1.8 million\" in the claim is likely a mis-scaled version of this. The method is scriptural arithmetic with no observational check.","keyEvidence":["Vishnu Purana 6.3 and Manusmriti 1.69-71 give the yuga lengths in divine years (1 divine year = 360 human years): Krita 1,728,000, Treta 1,296,000, Dvapara 864,000, Kali 432,000 human years","Kali Yuga start is taken as 18 Feb 3102 BCE (Aryabhata and later siddhantas), so Treta's end is 864,000 years earlier","Ramayana itself locates Rama in the Treta-Dvapara transition ('sandhi') in some readings and in Treta in tradition; Valmiki gives no yuga name or year count","Kali Yuga Mahabharata-era anchor of 3102 BCE is itself a calculated epoch, not an observed date (a conventional epoch, often noted as a back-calculated zero point)","Arithmetic check: 3102 + 864,000 = 867,102 BCE; adding about 2,000 years of Treta-end spread and about 5,100 years since Kali gives about 869,000-872,000 years before present"],"buildsOn":["Vishnu Purana","Bhagavata Purana","Vayu Purana","Surya Siddhanta and Aryabhata (Kali epoch 3102 BCE)","Manusmriti 1.69-71"],"critiques":["No modern archaeology supports human civilisation at this scale: ~870,000 years ago is Lower Palaeolithic, with Homo erectus or heidelbergensis, no cities, metallurgy or writing; 18 million years predates Homo itself","Yuga lengths are cosmological, symbolic numbers; many Indologists and some traditional scholars (e.g. Sri Yukteswar's 24,000-year cycle) treat them as inflated or misread","Valmiki Ramayana contains no yuga year count; the numbers come from later Puranic systematisation","Internal tension: a Treta ending 864,000 years before Kali clashes with a Mahabharata set at the Dvapara-Kali junction only some generations after Rama, as genealogies of Ikshvaku kings count dozens of generations, not hundreds of thousands of years","Popular figures vary (8.7 lakh vs 1.8 crore vs 'over 1 million'), showing the claim is not a single published calculation","Cited claims such as 'Rama Setu is 1.7 million years old' (from a geological or NASA-image argument) conflict with geology, which dates Adam's Bridge as natural shoals of about 7,000 years or less"],"plausibility":1,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Vishnu Purana, Book 6, chapter 3 (yuga durations)","Bhagavata Purana, Canto 3 chapter 11 (time divisions); Canto 12 chapter 2","Manusmriti 1.69-73 (yuga lengths)","Wikipedia: Yuga; Rama; Kali Yuga; Adam's Bridge (background, not fetched this session)"]},{"id":"rm-puranic-24th-treta","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Puranic manvantara/mahayuga scheme, popularised by various modern Hindu writers and preachers (no single identified originator)","proposerInfo":"Traditional Puranic chronology (Vayu, Brahmanda and related Puranas) as restated by popular modern Hindu authors and speakers; no single scholar is tied to the 1.8 crore figure","event":"Birth and reign of Rama (Ramayana events), placed in the 24th Treta Yuga of the Vaivasvata Manvantara","dateLabel":"24th Treta Yuga of Vaivasvata Manvantara, c. 18 million years ago (1.8 crore); implied ~18.1 million years before present by summing the intervening yugas","year":-18100000,"yearLow":-18144000,"yearHigh":-17280000,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Puranic avatara-by-yuga lists (Vayu Purana and parallel Brahmanda Purana passages naming Rama Dasharathi in the 24th Treta); modern popular restatements, no single canonical book","workYear":null,"summary":"The Puranas divide time into manvantaras of 71 mahayugas, each of 4.32 million years (Krita, Treta, Dvapara, Kali). We are in the 28th mahayuga of the 7th (Vaivasvata) manvantara. Vayu Purana and related texts are said to list Rama Dasharathi as the avatara of the 24th Treta Yuga. Counting forward from that Treta gives about 18.1 million years: the rest of the 24th mahayuga (Dvapara 864,000 plus Kali 432,000), three full mahayugas (12.96 million), then the 28th mahayuga's Krita (1.728M), Treta (1.296M) and Dvapara (0.864M). That is the popular 1.8 crore figure. It is far older than the common reading of about 870,000 years, which counts only the current 28th mahayuga. The scheme is a theological cycle count, not an empirical dating.","keyEvidence":["Puranic statement that Rama Dasharathi appears in the 24th Treta of the Vaivasvata Manvantara (Vayu Purana; exact verse numbers not re-checked online, so not cited)","Standard yuga arithmetic: Krita 1,728,000; Treta 1,296,000; Dvapara 864,000; Kali 432,000 years; 28th Kali began 3102 BCE","Sum from the 24th Treta to the present is about 18.14 million years"],"buildsOn":["Vayu Purana","Brahmanda Purana","Vishnu Purana yuga and manvantara scheme"],"critiques":["Not an empirical date: it is a cosmological cycle count with no astronomical observation behind it","Anatomically modern humans appear only about 300,000 years ago, so a human Rama 18 million years ago contradicts palaeontology and archaeology","Conflicts with other Hindu counts that put Rama in the 24th Treta of the current 28th mahayuga (about 870,000 to 1.2 million years ago) or in a historical Treta near 5000 to 7000 BCE","Different sources count the 24th Treta differently; the figure depends on whether the 28th Krita and Treta are added","Sri Lanka's Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu) is geologically dated to a few thousand to ~18,000 years, not millions"],"plausibility":1,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Vayu Purana, avatara and yuga lists (Rama Dasharathi in 24th Treta); not re-checked online","Vishnu Purana, Book 3 on manvantaras and mahayugas","Wikipedia: Yuga; Manvantara"]},{"id":"rm-prabhupada-iskcon","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"A.C. Bhaktivedanta Swami Prabhupada (ISKCON)","proposerInfo":"Founder-acarya of ISKCON (1896-1977), Gaudiya Vaishnava translator and commentator of the Bhagavad-gita and Srimad-Bhagavatam","event":"Rama's appearance and pastimes (Ramayana events) in Treta Yuga","dateLabel":"Treta Yuga, \"millions of years ago\" (popularly quoted ~2 million; a strict Puranic count of the 24th Treta would give ~17-18 million). Plotted at about -2,000,000.","year":-2000000,"yearLow":-18000000,"yearHigh":-870000,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Srimad-Bhagavatam Canto 9, Ch. 10 \"Lord Ramachandra's Pastimes\" (translation and purports, 1970s); also Bhagavad-gita As It Is 4.1 purport and lectures","workYear":1975,"summary":"Prabhupada follows Puranic and Vaishnava tradition. Rama is an avatara of Vishnu who appeared in Treta Yuga, long before the current Dvapara and Kali yugas, so the Ramayana belongs millions of years in the past. His writings and lectures stress the immense antiquity of Vedic history and dismiss modern short chronologies. The figure \"~2 million years, 24th Treta\" is the commonly cited ISKCON formulation. It is a theological and cosmological claim, not a dating derived from observation. I could not verify exact lecture or purport quotes in this session, so none are given.","keyEvidence":["Puranic yuga scheme: Treta (1,296,000 yrs) precedes Dvapara (864,000) and Kali (432,000); Rama is placed in Treta.","Bhagavad-gita As It Is 4.1 purport gives a Vaivasvata Manu / sun-god timeline of about 120,400,000 years, used to argue for deep antiquity (from memory, not re-checked).","The '24th Treta' tag comes from the Puranic mahayuga count of the present Vaivasvata manvantara; I could not trace Prabhupada's exact wording.","Arithmetic caveat: the 24th Treta of 28 mahayugas is about 4 mahayugas (~17 million years) back, so '~2 million' does not match a strict count. It matches the looser popular claim that Rama lived ~1.8 million years ago."],"buildsOn":["Puranic yuga and manvantara chronology (Vishnu, Vayu, Bhagavata Puranas)","Gaudiya Vaishnava tradition","Valmiki Ramayana"],"critiques":["Not testable: no astronomical or archaeological anchor is offered.","The timespan predates Homo sapiens (~300,000 years) and any known city, writing or bronze/iron technology, so it conflicts with the archaeological record.","The internal arithmetic of '24th Treta' versus '~2 million years' is inconsistent under the standard 4.32 million-year mahayuga.","Yuga lengths are a late Puranic scheme and are not in the Valmiki Ramayana text itself; the text's own astronomy and genealogy imply a historical timescale of millennia.","Exact lecture or purport quotes were not verified."],"plausibility":1,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Bhaktivedanta VedaBase: Srimad-Bhagavatam 9.10 (vedabase.io/en/library/sb/9/10/)","Bhagavad-gita As It Is 4.1 purport (vedabase.io/en/library/bg/4/1/)","Vishnu Purana, Book 6 on yuga durations"]},{"id":"rm-pargiter-2350-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"F.E. Pargiter","proposerInfo":"Frederick Eden Pargiter (1852-1927), British Indian Civil Service judge and Indologist, Puranic scholar","event":"Reign of Rama, the Ikshvaku king of Ayodhya, as a historical figure","dateLabel":"c. 2350 BCE as circulated (range roughly 2350-1950 BCE); the exact figure in Pargiter is NOT verified","year":-2350,"yearLow":-2350,"yearHigh":-1950,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Ancient Indian Historical Tradition (Oxford University Press, 1922)","workYear":1922,"summary":"Pargiter treated the Puranic dynastic lists as a real historical tradition and fixed the Bharata war at about 950 BCE by counting generations. He then counted back along the Ikshvaku (Ayodhya) lineage, assigning each generation an average span, to place Rama in the third to second millennium BCE. The figure of c. 2350 BCE attributed to him in secondary writing could not be checked against the book here. A 1950 BCE number also circulates and is likewise unverified. The arithmetic is shaky. The Puranas put Rama roughly 30 generations before the war, and at 20-25 years per generation that gives something nearer 1500-1700 BCE, not 2350 BCE. So the quoted figure may come from a different generation count or from a different source.","keyEvidence":["Puranic dynastic lists (Vayu, Matsya, Vishnu, Bhagavata) for Ikshvaku and Kuru/Paurava lines","Synchronisms between the Ikshvaku, Paurava and Aila lines, counted in generations","Anchor of the Bharata war at about 950 BCE, derived from his own generation counts for the Paurava line","Assumed average generation length (roughly 18-25 years, depending on the line)"],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists","Vedic and Puranic synchronisms (Pargiter's own earlier Dynasties of the Kali Age, 1913)"],"critiques":["Generation-averaging is arbitrary, and the Puranic lists differ between texts and are probably telescoped or padded.","Pargiter treated Rama as a human king without separating the epic's mythic layers.","His 950 BCE war date is too low for many later scholars, who prefer c. 1400-900 BCE on archaeological grounds.","Excavations at Ayodhya have not shown an urban settlement of 2350 BCE, so the date does not fit the archaeology.","Traditionalists reject the whole approach because it compresses the Puranic and yuga chronology."],"plausibility":4,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["F.E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, Oxford University Press, 1922 (archive.org has scans)","F.E. Pargiter, The Purana Text of the Dynasties of the Kali Age, 1913","Wikipedia, Dating the Mahabharata and Ramayana (general background)"]},{"id":"rm-pusalker-genealogy","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"A. D. Pusalker","proposerInfo":"Anant Dharmaji Pusalker, 20th-century Indologist (Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan, Bombay); author of Studies in the Epics and Puranas (1955) and contributor to the Majumdar-edited History and Culture of the Indian People.","event":"Rama's era (Ikshvaku king of Ayodhya) by genealogy, and growth of the Valmiki Ramayana text to its final form","dateLabel":"Rama c. 2000 BCE or earlier (approximate, genealogical); text in final form by c. 2nd century CE","year":-2000,"yearLow":-2500,"yearHigh":-1900,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"History and Culture of the Indian People, Vol. I The Vedic Age (Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan, 1951); also Studies in the Epics and Puranas (1955)","workYear":1951,"summary":"Pusalker followed the Pargiter-school method of counting generations in the Puranic dynastic lists, anchored to a Bharata war of about 950 BCE, to place Rama of the Ikshvaku line in roughly the early second millennium BCE, about 2000 BCE or somewhat earlier. Separately, using internal textual analysis in the manner of Jacobi and Winternitz, he treated the Ramayana as a growing text: a core Valmiki narrative later expanded (notably the first and last books), reaching roughly its present form by about the 2nd century CE. The two figures date different things: the legendary king and the poem. The exact figures come from the task statement; I could not retrieve the original wording, so they are approximate.","keyEvidence":["Puranic Ikshvaku and lunar-line king-lists counted by generations back from a c. 950 BCE Bharata war","Pargiter's synchronisms of Ikshvaku and other dynasties (Ancient Indian Historical Tradition)","Textual layering: Books I and VII seen as later additions; Rama as avatara of Vishnu seen as a later stratum","Jacobi-style view that the epic reached roughly its present form by about 200 CE"],"buildsOn":["F. E. Pargiter","H. Jacobi","M. Winternitz","R. C. Majumdar (editor of HCIP)"],"critiques":["Generation-counting from Puranic lists is unreliable: lists are telescoped, uneven in length and compiled late","Average reign or generation lengths are arbitrary and swing the result by centuries","No archaeological or epigraphic anchor for Rama; Ayodhya and Lanka excavations do not confirm a c. 2000 BCE horizon","Traditionalists reject the text-growth model and the late final-form date; critics of the 950 BCE anchor say it is too low or too high depending on school"],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["A. D. Pusalker, 'Traditional History from the Earliest Times to the Accession of Parikshit', in History and Culture of the Indian People, Vol. I: The Vedic Age, Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan, 1951","A. D. Pusalker, Studies in the Epics and Puranas of India, Bharatiya Vidya Bhavan, 1955","F. E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, 1922"]},{"id":"rm-ikshvaku-genealogy-puranas","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Puranic Ikshvaku king-list method (F. E. Pargiter and later genealogical chronologists)","proposerInfo":"Not a single proposer. It is a method applied to the Puranic lists by Pargiter (colonial judge and Indologist) and many later scholars and traditionalists.","event":"Rama's reign, dated by counting Ikshvaku-line generations back from Brihadbala (killed in the Kurukshetra war)","dateLabel":"No fixed date. It is relative: Rama is roughly 64th-65th in the line, about 30 generations before Brihadbala. At 18-25 years per generation, that is about 550-750 years before the war. Anchored to a Mahabharata date of c. 950-3100 BCE, Rama falls between c. 1500 and c. 3800 BCE. Plotted at about 2000 BCE as an illustrative midpoint.","year":-2000,"yearLow":-3800,"yearHigh":-1500,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Vishnu Purana Book IV ch. 4 and Bhagavata Purana Canto IX ch. 10-12 (Ikshvaku lists). Method discussed in Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition (1922).","workYear":1922,"summary":"The Puranas list the Ikshvaku (Solar) dynasty of Kosala in sequence. Rama appears about 64th-65th from Ikshvaku. The list continues through his sons Kusha and Lava and about 30 further kings to Brihadbala, who the Mahabharata says was killed by Abhimanyu in the war. The method multiplies the generation gap by an assumed average reign or generation length and subtracts it from a chosen Mahabharata date. It gives only a relative date, so the result depends entirely on the war date and the generation length chosen. The exact generation counts differ between the Vishnu, Bhagavata, Vayu and Matsya Puranas and between editions, so the figures here need checking against each text.","keyEvidence":["Vishnu Purana IV.4 and Bhagavata Purana IX.10-12 give the Ikshvaku succession through Rama, Kusha, and later kings to Brihadbala.","The Mahabharata (Drona Parva) records Brihadbala of Kosala dying in the war, which fixes the lower end of the chain.","The Vayu and Matsya Puranas give overlapping but not identical lists, so counts vary by a few generations."],"buildsOn":["Pargiter","Puranic king-lists","Mahabharata war date anchors (e.g. Aryabhata-based 3102 BCE or Pargiter's c. 950 BCE)"],"critiques":["Puranic lists were compiled late and may be incomplete, telescoped or partly schematic.","Generation or reign length is an arbitrary assumption, which gives a wide range of results.","The result is not independent: it inherits the uncertainty of whatever Mahabharata date is used as the anchor.","Counts differ between Puranas and manuscripts, and some lists were merged or padded.","The archaeological record shows no clear evidence of a Kosala kingdom at Ayodhya at these early dates."],"plausibility":4,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Vishnu Purana, Book IV, ch. 4 (Ikshvaku dynasty)","Bhagavata Purana, Canto IX, ch. 10-12","F. E. Pargiter, Ancient Indian Historical Tradition, 1922"]},{"id":"rm-vaidya-2000-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"C.V. Vaidya (Chintaman Vinayak Vaidya)","proposerInfo":"Bombay-based historian, lawyer and author (1861-1938), former Dewan of Indore; wrote on epic and medieval India.","event":"Era of Rama (Treta-Dvapara junction), with the Ramayana placed before the Mahabharata","dateLabel":"c. 2000 BCE or earlier (as stated in the task; exact figure unverified)","year":-2000,"yearLow":-2500,"yearHigh":-2000,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"The Riddle of the Ramayana (Bombay, 1906); related arguments in Epic India (1907) and The Mahabharata: A Criticism (1904)","workYear":1906,"summary":"Vaidya was a traditionalist-leaning historian who accepted a historical Rama who came before the Mahabharata war. He dated Rama through the Ikshvaku (Solar dynasty) king-lists, counting generations back from the Bharata-war era, which he put near 3100 BCE. The \"c. 2000 BCE or earlier\" figure comes from the task text, and I could not confirm it from the book. I also could not confirm that he put the Ramayana before the Mahabharata or how he treated the poem's composition date. No web check was done; this rests on background knowledge only.","keyEvidence":["Ikshvaku genealogies in the Puranas and epics, counted in generations from Rama","Relative ordering of Rama and the Bharata-war era in the epics","Traditional Kali-era reckoning for the Mahabharata war (c. 3100 BCE)"],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists (Vishnu, Bhagavata, Vayu Puranas)","Traditional Kali-era (3102 BCE) reckoning for the Mahabharata war"],"critiques":["Puranic genealogies are idealised, inconsistent between Puranas and compiled late, so generation counting is unreliable.","The result depends on the assumed years per generation, and small changes shift it by centuries.","No archaeological layer matches the epic Ayodhya or Lanka at that date. Excavated Ayodhya is mainly NBPW-era (c. 700 BCE onward).","A 2000 BCE date is hard to reconcile with a Treta-Dvapara junction near 3100 BCE, so the numbers need checking against the book."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["C.V. Vaidya, The Riddle of the Ramayana, Bombay, 1906","C.V. Vaidya, Epic India, Bombay, 1907","C.V. Vaidya, The Mahabharata: A Criticism, Bombay, 1904"]},{"id":"rm-bhagavad-datta","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Bhagavad Datta (Pt. Bhagavaddatta, 1893-1979)","proposerInfo":"Arya Samaj Vedic scholar and historian, associated with DAV College Lahore research; wrote on Vedic literature and Indian history.","event":"Rama's era (Treta/Dvapara transition)","dateLabel":"c. 2000 BCE or earlier (approximate, relayed claim; exact figure in the book not verified)","year":-2000,"yearLow":-3000,"yearHigh":-2000,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"Bharatavarsha ka Itihas (History of India), Lahore","workYear":1939,"summary":"Bhagavad Datta, writing from an Arya Samaj viewpoint, reconstructed ancient Indian history from Vedic literature, Brahmanas and Puranic dynastic lists. He argued for high antiquity of Vedic texts and a Mahabharata war near the traditional 3100 BCE horizon. Rama, an earlier Ikshvaku king, falls before that war, which gives a rough figure of c. 2000 BCE or older in the claim as relayed to me. I could not confirm the exact date or wording from the book, so treat the number as approximate. His method is genealogical and textual, not astronomical.","keyEvidence":["Puranic Ikshvaku and Paurava genealogies counted back from the Mahabharata war","Vedic and Brahmana literature used to argue for high antiquity","Rejection of late Western chronologies for Vedic texts"],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists","Dayananda Saraswati and Arya Samaj tradition of Vedic antiquity","Traditional Kali-era dating of the Mahabharata (3102/3138 BCE)"],"critiques":["No archaeological confirmation links Ramayana sites to a 2000 BCE or earlier horizon.","Puranic genealogies have gaps and uneven generation lengths, so counting back is very uncertain.","Mainstream scholarship dates the Ramayana text much later and treats the king-lists as non-historical for this period.","Figure as relayed is internally loose: 'or earlier' gives no fixed year."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Bhagavad Datta, Bharatavarsha ka Itihas (Lahore, c. 1939)"]},{"id":"rm-william-jones-2029-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Sir William Jones","proposerInfo":"British orientalist and judge of the Supreme Court at Calcutta; founder of the Asiatic Society (1784)","event":"Reign of Rama (Ramachandra) of Ayodhya, placed in a synchronised table of Hindu and Western chronology","dateLabel":"c. 2029 BCE (about 2000 BCE). The exact year is from memory of Jones's table and was not checked against the text.","year":-2029,"yearLow":-2100,"yearHigh":-1950,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"\"On the Chronology of the Hindus\", Asiatic Researches vol. 2 (Calcutta, 1790); read to the Asiatic Society in 1788","workYear":1790,"summary":"Jones treated the Hindu yuga figures as astronomical or mythical, not historical. He used the Puranic lists of the Solar and Lunar dynasties to build a human-scale chronology. He anchored it to a few synchronisms: the Flood or Vaivasvata Manu (about 2350 BCE by his reckoning), the identification of Sandracottus with Chandragupta Maurya, and the Kali-era reckonings of Hindu astronomers. Counting generations from these anchors, he put Rama's reign at roughly 2000 BCE, and the Mahabharata war (Yudhishthira and Krishna) much later, in the 14th century BCE. I recall the figure 2029 BCE from his table but could not check it against the page.","keyEvidence":["Puranic genealogies of the Solar (Ikshvaku) dynasty, counted by generations","Sandracottus = Chandragupta synchronism, which Jones is credited with proposing","Kali-era dates from Hindu astronomers, used as cross-checks","Biblical Flood chronology, matched to Manu's deluge"],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists (Vishnu and Bhagavata Puranas)","Greek accounts of Sandracottus","Hindu astronomers' Kali-era reckonings","Biblical Flood chronology"],"critiques":["Generation-counting rests on an assumed average reign length and on Puranic lists of uncertain reliability.","The Biblical Flood anchor and Jones's early Buddha and Vedic dates are no longer accepted.","It is an early, pre-archaeological estimate. Later work on Ayodhya's excavated levels does not support a city of this kind around 2000 BCE.","It treats Rama as a historical king, which modern scholarship does not establish."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["William Jones, 'On the Chronology of the Hindus', Asiatic Researches, vol. 2, Calcutta, 1790, pp. 111-147 (page range from memory)","Thomas R. Trautmann, Aryans and British India (1997), on Jones's chronology"]},{"id":"rm-kota-venkatachalam","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Kota Venkatachalam","proposerInfo":"Telugu-speaking Indian chronologist from Andhra (Vijayawada), 20th century; revisionist of ancient Indian chronology, not a professional academic. Details from background knowledge.","event":"Rama's date (placeholder). No explicit Rama date found. His anchor is the Mahabharata war (1194 BCE), so any Rama placement would be earlier and is not quantified here.","dateLabel":"No explicit Rama date found. Anchored to Mahabharata war c. 1194 BCE; Rama is earlier but unquantified","year":null,"yearLow":null,"yearHigh":null,"method":"Genealogical / Puranic king-lists","work":"The Plot in Indian Chronology (1953)","workYear":1953,"summary":"Venkatachalam argued that conventional Indian chronology was distorted by a \"plot\" that pushed Indian history later, chiefly by identifying Chandragupta Maurya with the Greeks' Sandrocottus. He recast the Puranic king-lists into a compressed scheme placing the Mahabharata war around 1194 BCE. I could not verify that he gives any Rama date. If he does, it would come from counting back through the Ikshvaku list from the war. This entry is a placeholder pending access to the book.","keyEvidence":["Rejection of the Chandragupta Maurya = Sandrocottus identification (background knowledge, not verified)","Puranic dynastic lists and reign lengths used to reckon back from the war (not verified)","Reported Mahabharata date of 1194 BCE; exact derivation not checked"],"buildsOn":["Puranic king-lists","Earlier Indian revisionists of the Sandrocottus identification"],"critiques":["The Sandrocottus = Chandragupta Maurya synchronism with Seleucus (c. 305 BCE) anchors the standard chronology and is not credibly overturned","No Rama date located, so this entry cannot be tested","Compressing Puranic lists is arbitrary and conflicts with the archaeological record"],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Kota Venkatachalam, The Plot in Indian Chronology, Vijayawada, 1953 (not accessed)"]},{"id":"rm-dikshit-placeholder","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Shankar Balkrishna Dikshit","proposerInfo":"Marathi scholar and historian of Indian astronomy (1853-1898); author of Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra (Marathi, 1896; English translation by R.V. Vaidya, 1931).","event":"No Ramayana event dated; the work is a general history of Indian astronomy","dateLabel":"No Rama or Ramayana date attributed (placeholder, recommend dropping)","year":null,"yearLow":null,"yearHigh":null,"method":"Other","work":"Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra (1896)","workYear":1896,"summary":"Dikshit's Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra is a history of Indian astronomy. It is known for dating the Vedic period from astronomical hints, such as the Krittika-equinox references in the Brahmanas and the solstice positions in the Vedanga Jyotisha (around 1400 BCE). He also treated the Kali era as a later astronomical construct. From memory, I know of no Ramayana-era astronomical date given by him. I could not check the text during this task, so the absence is not verified. Treat the entry as a placeholder and drop it unless a specific passage is found.","keyEvidence":["Known contributions: Vedanga Jyotisha date and Vedic-era astronomical inferences, not Rama's birth","Later Ramayana dating literature (Jacobi, Vartak, Oak, Achar) cites other works, not Dikshit, for a Rama date","No Rama date from Dikshit found in memory; not checked against the text"],"buildsOn":[],"critiques":["No evidence that he dated the Ramayana, so listing him as a Rama-dating source risks misattribution","His Vedic-era astronomical dates were later revised by other scholars"],"plausibility":1,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["S.B. Dikshit, Bharatiya Jyotish Shastra (Marathi, 1896); English translation by R.V. Vaidya, History of Indian Astronomy (1931)"]},{"id":"rm-upadhyay-arun-kumar","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Arun Kumar Upadhyay","proposerInfo":"Retired Indian civil servant (IPS, Odisha cadre) who writes and lectures on Indian astronomy, the Surya Siddhanta and Vedic/Puranic chronology (identity from background knowledge, not re-verified here)","event":"Rama's era (Treta-yuga Ramayana events), placed within Puranic/Siddhantic cycles","dateLabel":"Several thousand years BCE (Puranic/siddhantic cycles); exact year not verified","year":null,"yearLow":-10000,"yearHigh":-4000,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Scattered articles, lectures and Hindi/English essays on Vedic chronology and Indian astronomy (specific title and year not verified)","workYear":null,"summary":"Upadhyay reads Puranic king-lists, Manvantara and yuga cycles and Surya Siddhanta time units as encoded astronomical and historical chronology, rather than as literal hundreds of thousands of years. On that reading Rama falls in a Treta-era layer placed several millennia BCE. I could not retrieve a specific article or a firm single year, so no precise date is asserted. Treat this entry as a placeholder for his approach, not a confirmed figure.","keyEvidence":["Puranic dynastic lists (Ikshvaku line) tied to cycle lengths","Surya Siddhanta and Manvantara/yuga time units reinterpreted","No verified planetary-position verse set was located"],"buildsOn":["Surya Siddhanta","Puranic king-lists","Traditional yuga schemes"],"critiques":["Reinterpreting yuga cycles to fit a chosen date is flexible and hard to falsify","Archaeology shows no urban Ayodhya or Lanka matching multi-millennial dates","Puranic genealogies are late compilations with inconsistent king counts","Exact date and method could not be verified"],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["No web search performed; no URLs verified. Look up his published articles on Vedic chronology to find the exact year."]},{"id":"rm-sankalia-1500-1000-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"H.D. Sankalia","proposerInfo":"Hasmukh Dhirajlal Sankalia (1908-1989), pioneering Indian archaeologist, Professor of Proto-Indian and Ancient Indian History, Deccan College, Pune.","event":"Historical kernel of the Rama story (Rama as a small-kingdom prince, exile and conflict in the south); the Valmiki text itself is placed much later","dateLabel":"c. 1500-1000 BCE or somewhat earlier (approximate range attributed to Sankalia; exact figure in his books not verified)","year":-1250,"yearLow":-1500,"yearHigh":-1000,"method":"Archaeological","work":"The Ramayana in Historical Perspective (Macmillan India, 1982), a revised extension of Ramayana: Myth or Reality? (People's Publishing House, 1973)","workYear":1973,"summary":"Sankalia, an archaeologist, treated the Ramayana as an epic that may preserve a small historical core, not as literal Treta-Yuga history. He compared its geography and material culture (small kingdoms, simple settlements, forest tribes) with excavated Chalcolithic and early Iron Age cultures. On that basis he placed the kernel story roughly in the second to early first millennium BCE. He treated the Valmiki text as a much later, layered composition. He also doubted that Lanka was Sri Lanka and suggested a location in central India. The 1500-1000 BCE range is the commonly cited approximation; I could not verify the exact wording in either book, and the two titles reflect an earlier (1973) and a revised (1982) treatment.","keyEvidence":["Excavations at Ayodhya (B.B. Lal), Nandigram, Shringaverapura and other epic sites show no urban, Treta-age levels; early occupation is modest and the sequence at Ayodhya begins around the Northern Black Polished Ware period","Comparison of epic descriptions with Chalcolithic and early Iron Age cultures of central and southern India","Internal textual layering of the Valmiki Ramayana, with books 1 and 7 seen as later additions","Argument that Lanka lay in central India, since Sri Lanka lacks matching early archaeology"],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","B.B. Lal (Ayodhya and epic-site excavations)","F.E. Pargiter (Puranic king-list tradition)","Hastinapura and Painted Grey Ware excavations"],"critiques":["Sankalia's conclusion that the epic holds at most a thin historical core is rejected by traditionalists who date Rama to the Treta Yuga or to c. 5114 BCE (Nilesh Oak, Vartak and others).","Archaeological silence at Ayodhya is argued by some to reflect limited excavation, while others use the same evidence to place the story later than Sankalia does.","The kernel date is inferential: no inscription or datable event ties it to a specific year, so the range is broad and partly impressionistic.","His Lanka-in-central-India proposal is not widely accepted."],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["H.D. Sankalia, Ramayana: Myth or Reality? (People's Publishing House, New Delhi, 1973)","H.D. Sankalia, The Ramayana in Historical Perspective (Macmillan India, Delhi, 1982)","B.B. Lal, Excavations at Ayodhya (Archaeological Survey of India)"]},{"id":"rm-sankalia-1700-1000-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"H.D. Sankalia","proposerInfo":"Hasmukhlal Dhirajlal Sankalia (1908-1989), pioneering Indian archaeologist and Professor at Deccan College, Pune","event":"Possible historical kernel of the Rama story (not a date for the Valmiki text)","dateLabel":"c. 1700-1000 BCE (tentative range attributed to Sankalia; exact figures unconfirmed; text composition placed much later, c. 500 BCE or after)","year":-1350,"yearLow":-1700,"yearHigh":-1000,"method":"Archaeological","work":"The Ramayana: Myth or Reality? (People's Publishing House, New Delhi)","workYear":1973,"summary":"Sankalia examined the Ramayana as an archaeologist, asking which real cultures and places could lie behind the epic's geography. He treated the text as largely myth and legend, with at most a small historical core about a prince's exile and a conflict in the south. He did not accept the Treta Yuga chronology. Any dating he gave was a loose, hedged bracket for a possible kernel in the Chalcolithic or Late Bronze Age, with the epic itself composed and expanded much later. The specific range 1700-1000 BCE is the task's attribution, and I could not confirm the exact figures from a primary source.","keyEvidence":["Archaeology of Ayodhya and other north Indian sites: excavations, notably B.B. Lal's, found no substantial early occupation matching the epic's city.","Material culture in the epic (iron, large cities, chariots) compared with Chalcolithic and Early Iron Age sites; he judged much of it to be later accretion.","Discussion of Lanka's location: he argued it need not be modern Sri Lanka and may have been in central India, consistent with a Deccan Chalcolithic setting.","Textual analysis: the Bala and Uttara Kandas are later additions, and the core was composed centuries after any historical event."],"buildsOn":["Pargiter (Puranic king-lists)","H. Jacobi (Ramayana composition)","B.B. Lal (Ayodhya excavations)"],"critiques":["The range is hedged and not derived from a calibrated method; there are no dated synchronisms with the epic.","Traditionalists such as Nilesh Oak and Saroj Bala reject it, saying it ignores astronomical references and king-lists.","Mainstream scholars note that Ayodhya has no urban habitation before about 700 BCE.","It is unconfirmed whether Sankalia gave a specific numeric range or only a general comment; the 1500-1000 BCE merge could not be confirmed."],"plausibility":5,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["H.D. Sankalia, The Ramayana: Myth or Reality? (People's Publishing House, 1973)","H.D. Sankalia, Ramayana in Historical Perspective (1982, Macmillan India)","B.B. Lal, Excavations at Ayodhya, Ancient India reports (1970s)"]},{"id":"rm-sankalia-ramayana-later-unhistorical","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"H.D. Sankalia (Hasmukhlal Dhirajlal Sankalia)","proposerInfo":"Indian archaeologist (1908-1989), Deccan College Pune, pioneer of Indian prehistory; excavated Nevasa, Maheshwar-Navdatoli, Nasik-Jorwe.","event":"Historicity and relative chronology of the Rama story and Lanka war (versus the Mahabharata war and the texts)","dateLabel":"No fixed calendar date. Rama story treated as legend with a possible core in the 2nd-1st millennium BCE; Valmiki text as a layered composition later than the Mahabharata core. Plotted at c. 1000 BCE (range 1400-500 BCE) as an editorial approximation.","year":-1000,"yearLow":-1400,"yearHigh":-500,"method":"Archaeological","work":"The Ramayana in Historical Perspective (Macmillan India, 1982); earlier Ramayana: Myth or Reality? (People's Publishing House, 1973)","workYear":1973,"summary":"Sankalia, an archaeologist, tested the Ramayana against excavated evidence rather than astronomy. He argued it is not a historical chronicle: Ravana's golden Lanka and the bridge-and-war narrative have no archaeological counterpart. He questioned whether Lanka was Sri Lanka at all, and discussed proposals (such as Hiralal's) placing it in central India. He treated the poem as a layered text whose final form is later than the Mahabharata core, and read the rakshasas and vanaras as tribal or early-farming groups of the Deccan. Caution: \"Ramayana later than Mahabharata\" is only safe for composition of the texts. In the dynastic lists Rama precedes the Kuru heroes, so he did not place Rama's events after the Bharata war. This is from memory and not checked against the books.","keyEvidence":["No Bronze Age urban or fortified site in Sri Lanka matching Ravana's Lanka; island prehistory shows Mesolithic and early Iron Age communities, with urbanism later","Excavations at Ayodhya and other epic sites (e.g. B.B. Lal's work) show no early grand city at the time implied","Textual layering: Ramayana's Books I and VII are widely regarded as later additions, and the language and geography reflect an expanded Gangetic world","Cultural reading of Deccan Chalcolithic and Neolithic groups as the basis for rakshasa and vanara tribes"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi (Ramayana later in composition than the Mahabharata core)","Hiralal (Lanka in central India, Amarkantak region)","B.B. Lal (Ayodhya and epic-site excavations)","F.E. Pargiter (Puranic genealogies)"],"critiques":["Traditionalists and Sri Lankan researchers cite the Ravana-related sites and Adam's Bridge (Ram Setu) as supporting Lanka in Sri Lanka","Absence of archaeological evidence is weak for a short-lived event described in poetry","The Rama story's place before the Kuru genealogies sits uneasily with 'Ramayana later' unless limited to text composition","Some critics say he leaned too heavily on tribal-ethnographic parallels"],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["H.D. Sankalia, Ramayana: Myth or Reality? (People's Publishing House, New Delhi, 1973)","H.D. Sankalia, The Ramayana in Historical Perspective (Macmillan India, 1982)","Hermann Jacobi, Das Ramayana (1893)","Wikipedia: Hasmukh Dhirajlal Sankalia"]},{"id":"rm-lal-ayodhya-7th-bce","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"B.B. Lal (Braj Basi Lal)","proposerInfo":"Indian archaeologist (1921-2022), Director General of the Archaeological Survey of India 1968-72; excavated Hastinapura, Ayodhya and other epic sites.","event":"Earliest archaeological occupation at Ayodhya (ASI 'Archaeology of Ramayana Sites' project), and what it implies for the Ramayana's historicity","dateLabel":"c. 7th century BCE (about 700 BCE) for the earliest Ayodhya occupation (NBPW). Lal personally placed Rama's era earlier, before c. 1000 BCE, with no precise date.","year":-700,"yearLow":-1000,"yearHigh":-700,"method":"Archaeological","work":"ASI project 'Archaeology of the Ramayana Sites' (Ayodhya excavations 1975-76 and 1976-77, plus Nandigram, Sringaverapura, Bharadvaja Ashram, Chitrakoot); B.B. Lal, 'Rama, His Historicity, Mandir and Setu: Evidence of Literature, Archaeology and Other Sciences' (Aryan Books International, 2008)","workYear":2008,"summary":"Lal directed the ASI project (1975-80) that excavated places named in the Ramayana. At Ayodhya the lowest layer above natural soil (Period I) held Northern Black Polished Ware and associated pottery, which he dated to about the 7th century BCE. The excavations found no remains tied to a Ramayana-era city. Lal did not treat this as disproof of Rama. He pointed to the small area of the trenches and the deep, disturbed early levels. In his 2008 book he leaned toward a Rama era before c. 1000 BCE, and he elsewhere dated the Mahabharata-linked Painted Grey Ware horizon at Hastinapura to about 900 BCE. This entry rests on background knowledge, and I did not check the exact periodisation or page-level statements against the sources.","keyEvidence":["Ayodhya trenches of 1975-77 show the earliest occupation (Period I) with NBPW directly above natural soil, dated c. 7th century BCE.","Later periods run from Sunga-Kushana through Gupta to medieval. This is the standard sequence as I recall it, but the exact period labels are unverified.","No excavated remains at Ayodhya or the other Ramayana sites are attributable to a pre-1000 BCE city of the epic's description.","Lal argued that the small excavated area and deep deposits limit what the negative result can show, and that the tradition is older than the archaeology can reach.","Sringaverapura and other sites in the project are cited by Lal for features that match the epic's geography. I have not verified their dates."],"buildsOn":["ASI Ramayana Sites project design, 1970s","Lal's own Hastinapura excavations and Painted Grey Ware chronology","Traditional Valmiki Ramayana geography"],"critiques":["Critics such as D. Mandal and others argue the Ayodhya mound shows no pre-NBPW habitation and no urban culture of Rama's supposed era, so archaeology does not support the epic's description.","Defenders of a deep antiquity argue that the excavations were limited in area and that absence of evidence is not evidence of absence.","Lal's own dating of Rama before c. 1000 BCE is an inference from literature and tradition, not something the Ayodhya stratigraphy establishes.","Lal's later Babri Masjid temple-pillar-base claims are disputed by other archaeologists. They concern a separate question from the epic's date."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["B.B. Lal, Rama, His Historicity, Mandir and Setu (Aryan Books International, 2008)","Indian Archaeology: A Review, 1976-77 and 1977-78 (ASI), notes on Ayodhya excavations","B.B. Lal, 'Excavations at Hastinapura and other explorations in the Upper Ganga and Sutlej basins', Ancient India 10-11 (1954-55)"]},{"id":"rm-lal-ramayana-sites-1000-700","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"B.B. Lal (Braj Basi Lal)","proposerInfo":"Archaeologist, former Director General of the Archaeological Survey of India (1968-72); directed the ASI \"Archaeology of the Ramayana Sites\" project (1975-80s) and the Hastinapura excavations (1950-52).","event":"Archaeological occupation of Ramayana-associated sites (Ayodhya, Bharadwaj Ashram, Nandigram, Shringaverapura, Chitrakoot), used as a lower bound for the Ramayana's setting","dateLabel":"c. 1000-700 BCE onward (site occupation; earliest at Shringaverapura around 1000 BCE, Ayodhya and others mainly from c. 700 BCE; per-site ranges approximate)","year":-1000,"yearLow":-1000,"yearHigh":-700,"method":"Archaeological","work":"Excavations at Sringaverapura 1977-86 (ASI Memoir, c. 1993); \"Archaeology and the Two Indian Epics\" (1976); \"Rama, His Historicity, Mandir and Setu\" (2008)","workYear":1993,"summary":"Lal's ASI project excavated places the Ramayana names along Rama's route: Ayodhya, Nandigram, Bharadwaj Ashram (Prayag), Shringaverapura and Chitrakoot. On stratigraphy and pottery (Black-and-Red Ware, in places Painted Grey Ware, then Northern Black Polished Ware) the earliest substantial occupation begins around the early first millennium BCE. Ayodhya's earliest levels are about 7th century BCE. Shringaverapura starts earlier, around 1000 BCE, and is known for its sophisticated tank. Lal used this as archaeological context for the epic and, in later writings, argued its core is historical. This is a site-occupation date, not a direct dating of Rama. The exact per-site ranges were not checked against the Memoir and are recalled from background knowledge.","keyEvidence":["Stratigraphic sequences and pottery at five Ramayana-named sites from the ASI project (1975-80s)","Shringaverapura excavations 1977-86, including the large tank and early-first-millennium BCE levels","Ayodhya earliest levels around 7th century BCE (NBPW horizon)","Absence of habitation at Ayodhya in the millennia traditionally assigned to Rama"],"buildsOn":["Hastinapura excavations (Lal 1950-52)","Valmiki Ramayana geography"],"critiques":["Archaeology shows when sites were occupied, not when a story is set; the result is a floor, not a date for Rama.","Ayodhya's early occupation is thin and late, which conflicts with traditional or early-millennia dates.","Critics argue the site-to-text identifications are assumptions, and the epic grew over centuries.","Some scholars see Lal's later writings as advocacy shaped by Ayodhya temple-dispute debates."],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Lal, B.B. Excavations at Sringaverapura 1977-86, Memoirs of the Archaeological Survey of India","Lal, B.B. (1976) Archaeology and the Two Indian Epics","Lal, B.B. (2008) Rama, His Historicity, Mandir and Setu, Aryan Books International"]},{"id":"rm-asi-mani-2003","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Archaeological Survey of India (excavation team led by B.R. Mani and Hari Manjhi)","proposerInfo":"ASI, a government archaeology agency. B.R. Mani was a senior ASI archaeologist who later became ASI Additional Director General and then Director General of the National Museum. The excavation was ordered by the Allahabad High Court.","event":"Earliest dated human occupation at the Ayodhya Ramjanmabhoomi-Babri site. This is an archaeological bound for the settlement. It is not a date for Rama.","dateLabel":"Earliest occupation c. 1300-1000 BCE (early NBPW or earlier), with disputed figures. Structures below the Babri site are dated to the 10th-12th c. CE. No date for Rama is claimed.","year":-1000,"yearLow":-1300,"yearHigh":-700,"method":"Archaeological","work":"ASI report \"Ayodhya: Report of the Excavation at the Disputed Site (Ramajanma Bhumi-Babri Masjid)\", submitted to the Allahabad High Court in August 2003. Excavation ran from March to August 2003, after a ground-penetrating radar survey by Tojo-Vikas International in early 2003.","workYear":2003,"summary":"Under a 2003 Allahabad High Court order, the ASI excavated the disputed Ayodhya mound. It reported a stratigraphic sequence starting with the Northern Black Polished Ware (NBPW) phase, with the earliest levels placed roughly in the later 2nd to 1st millennium BCE. Radiocarbon dates from the lowest levels were reported at about 1300-1000 BCE, but I am working from memory and the exact figures need checking. The sequence continues through Shunga, Kushana, Gupta and early medieval phases. Above these the team reported a large structure of the 10th-12th c. CE (Gahadavala period) with about 50 pillar bases, which it read as a temple-like building beneath the mosque. The report does not mention Rama or date the Ramayana. At most it shows that Ayodhya was occupied from the late 2nd or early 1st millennium BCE, far later than the traditional or astronomical Rama dates.","keyEvidence":["Stratigraphic sequence from Period I (NBPW and possibly earlier) through the medieval periods.","Radiocarbon dates from the lowest levels, reportedly c. 1300-1000 BCE (unverified figures).","A 10th-12th c. CE 'huge structure' beneath the Babri Masjid, with about 50 pillar bases in rows, a circular shrine and a divine couple sculpture.","Ground-penetrating radar anomalies that were used to choose trench locations.","Terracotta figurines, Kushana and Gupta period remains, and later medieval structures."],"buildsOn":["B.B. Lal's 1970s Ayodhya excavations (Archaeology of Ramayana Sites project), which reported pillar bases and NBPW-era occupation","Earlier ASI work on Ramayana sites"],"critiques":["Irfan Habib, D. Mandal, Shireen Moosvi, Sita Ram Roy and others said the pillar-base claim was flawed. They argued the 'pillar bases' were not contemporaneous, were not load-bearing and were not aligned, and that pillar numbers were adjusted to fit the interpretation.","Supriya Varma and Jaya Menon, who observed the dig, alleged that the excavation methods were irregular and that the stratigraphy was misread.","Critics pointed to animal bones and to glazed ware and lime-surkhi floors from the Sultanate period, which they said were ignored or reinterpreted.","The report's radiocarbon and phase dates were disputed, and the 'earlier occupation' reading was treated as soft.","The 2019 Supreme Court (M. Siddiq v. Mahant Suresh Das, 9 Nov 2019) accepted the ASI findings as evidence. It noted that the underlying structure was non-Islamic, but said the ASI did not establish that a temple was demolished for the mosque, and that title could not rest on archaeology alone.","The excavation is silent on Rama's historicity or date. Even taken at face value, it only gives a lower bound on settlement. It does not support dates such as 5114 BCE or earlier."],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Allahabad High Court, Ayodhya title suit judgment, 30 Sep 2010 (ASI report extracts)","Supreme Court of India, M. Siddiq (D) Thr Lrs v. Mahant Suresh Das, 9 Nov 2019","Supriya Varma and Jaya Menon, 'Was there a temple under the Babri Masjid? Reading the Archaeological Evidence', Economic and Political Weekly, 2010","Wikipedia: Ayodhya dispute and Archaeological Survey of India excavations at Ayodhya"]},{"id":"rm-ram-setu-geology","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Prof. S. M. Ramasamy (with GSI / CSIR-NIO studies cited in the 2007 Sethusamudram debate)","proposerInfo":"Geologist and remote-sensing specialist, then at Bharathidasan University, Tiruchirappalli (later Vice-Chancellor of Periyar University and Tamil Nadu Geological Survey figure); background-knowledge attribution, not re-verified online.","event":"Geological age of the Ram Setu / Adam's Bridge shoal chain between Pamban and Mannar. This is not a date for Rama, and Ramasamy did not claim it was.","dateLabel":"Shoals and corals roughly 3,500-7,000 years old (c. 5000-1500 BCE), as reported in 2007 press. This is a geological age and is not a date for the Ramayana.","year":-3500,"yearLow":-5000,"yearHigh":-1500,"method":"Geological / other science claim","work":"Statements by Ramasamy and press coverage during the 2007 Sethusamudram Ship Channel Project controversy, with GSI and NIO studies. I could not identify a single formal paper that fits the quoted figures.","workYear":2007,"summary":"During the 2007 Sethusamudram Ship Channel Project row, geologists described Adam's Bridge as a natural chain of sand shoals, limestone shoals and corals. The chain sits on a shallow shelf between India and Sri Lanka and was shaped by sea-level change, longshore drift and sediment deposition. Ramasamy and others cited radiocarbon dates of the carbonate and sediment material of a few thousand years (about 3,500 to 7,000 years). Supporters of the Ramayana read this as consistent with an event in antiquity, while others read it as proof that the structure is natural. Neither reading dates Rama, because the age of a sediment or coral body says nothing about whether humans built a causeway on it.","keyEvidence":["Radiocarbon dating of corals and shell material from the Rameswaram-Dhanushkodi area, with ages reported in the range of a few thousand years up to about 7,000 years.","The Rameswaram island beach ridges and the shoal chain are described as natural sedimentary and coral features.","Satellite imagery (the 2002 NASA/ISRO-type images) shows a curved shoal chain. The images do not show construction.","The 2007 Archaeological Survey of India affidavit in the Supreme Court said there was no historical evidence for the Ramayana events. The government withdrew it after protests.","The Sethusamudram project controversy put the age and origin of the bridge into the public debate."],"buildsOn":["GSI and NIO Goa surveys of the Palk Strait","Satellite imagery of Adam's Bridge (2002)"],"critiques":["It is not a date for Rama. It dates a geological formation, and does not date any human act.","Overlapping ranges (c. 5000-1500 BCE) are not evidence of a link, because traditional Rama dates run from about 880,000 years ago to roughly 5000 BCE and later.","The numbers vary between sources and between sampling sites, and I could not tie the quoted 3,500-7,000 year range to a specific peer-reviewed paper.","Some of the Rameswaram landform is much older than 7,000 years, so a single age is a simplification.","Supporters and opponents of the project both used the same figures to argue opposite conclusions, so the figures do not decide the question.","A 2021 underwater archaeology study by ASI with CSIR-NIO was approved, and a 2022 government reply in Parliament said it was difficult to establish the bridge as man-made. That leaves the question open, and gives no Ramayana date."],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adam%27s_Bridge","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sethusamudram_Shipping_Canal_Project","Press coverage of the Sethusamudram controversy, 2007 (The Hindu, Frontline). Specific articles not retrieved in this run."]},{"id":"rm-land-bridge-18000","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Geological consensus (Last Glacial Maximum sea-level reconstructions); not a single author and not a Rama dater","proposerInfo":"Paleoclimate and marine geology literature (e.g. Clark et al. 2009 on LGM sea level) plus Indian studies of Palk Strait and Adam's Bridge geomorphology","event":"India-Sri Lanka dry-land connection across the Palk Strait/Gulf of Mannar shelf (geological backdrop of Adam's Bridge/Rama Setu), not a date for Rama","dateLabel":"c. 18,000 years ago (about 16,000 BCE), a geological baseline and not a Rama date","year":-16000,"yearLow":-24500,"yearHigh":-6000,"method":"Geological / other science claim","work":"Global LGM sea-level syntheses (e.g. Clark et al., Science 2009, \"The Last Glacial Maximum\") and Palk Strait bathymetry/Holocene transgression studies","workYear":2009,"summary":"During the Last Glacial Maximum (roughly 26,000 to 19,000 years ago) global sea level stood about 120 m below today. The Palk Strait and Gulf of Mannar shelf is only a few to tens of metres deep, so India and Sri Lanka were joined by a broad land corridor. Post-glacial sea-level rise flooded it in stages, and the connection was finally lost in the early to mid Holocene (commonly put around 10,000 to 7,000 years ago, with uncertainty). The 18,000-year figure is a mid-deglaciation marker, not a date anyone proposes for Rama. It serves as a scientific baseline: any natural land link long predates every proposed Ramayana date, so a 'bridge' of Rama's time would be a later shallow-water shoal structure. The task's wording \"c. 18,000 years\" is approximate; I could not verify a specific source giving that exact number for the Palk link.","keyEvidence":["LGM lowstand of about -120 to -130 m from coral and shelf records (Barbados, Sunda shelf)","Palk Strait is very shallow (mostly under about 10 m, Adam's Bridge shoals about 1 to 3 m), so modest sea-level rise severed the link","Deglacial sea-level rise including meltwater pulse 1A near 14,500 years ago","Adam's Bridge is a chain of limestone shoals and sandbars; sea-level fall of tens of metres exposes the whole corridor"],"buildsOn":[],"critiques":["Not a Ramayana date: it dates a geological state and is sometimes misused to argue the epic is either very old or invalid.","The timing of the last land connection is debated; many studies put final submergence in the early to mid Holocene, which is much later than 18,000 years ago.","Natural origin of Adam's Bridge as shoals and sandbars is what geologists say; this neither confirms nor refutes a constructed Setu.","Sea-level curves are global averages; local isostasy and tectonics in the Palk region add uncertainty."],"plausibility":9,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Clark et al. 2009, The Last Glacial Maximum, Science 325:710-714","Wikipedia: Adam's Bridge","Wikipedia: Last Glacial Maximum","Wikipedia: Palk Strait"]},{"id":"rm-nasa-ram-setu-1-75m","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Anonymous viral chain message attributed to \"NASA\" (no named author); later amplified by a Science Channel segment, \"Ancient Land Bridge\" (2017)","proposerInfo":"No identifiable individual. It is a recurring internet and email rumour, not a scholarly claim. The 2017 Science Channel clip used archaeologists and a geologist, not NASA.","event":"Age of Ram Setu (Adam's Bridge) as the bridge built by Rama's army, used to date Rama and the Treta Yuga","dateLabel":"Claimed 1.75 million years old (matched to Treta Yuga); plotted as about -1,750,000 (implied year)","year":-1750000,"yearLow":-1750000,"yearHigh":-1750000,"method":"Geological / other science claim","work":"Viral chain emails and social media posts claiming NASA images show a 1.75-million-year-old man-made bridge (circulating from about 2002); Science Channel, \"Ancient Land Bridge\" (2017)","workYear":2002,"summary":"A rumour says NASA satellite images of the shoals between Rameswaram (India) and Mannar (Sri Lanka) show a man-made bridge that NASA dated to about 1.75 million years. That figure is said to match the Treta Yuga, when Rama lived in Puranic reckoning. The photograph is real, but NASA has not dated the feature or called it man-made. The claim resurfaced in 2017 when a Science Channel promo suggested the structure might be man-made, and the clip was widely shared as scientific confirmation. Fact-checkers and geologists treat the 1.75-million-year NASA dating as false or unsupported. This entry is based on background knowledge; I did not retrieve the original sources in this session.","keyEvidence":["Orbital photograph of the Palk Strait shoals (Adam's Bridge), circulated as proof of an artificial bridge","The 1.75 million figure comes from the popular Puranic Treta Yuga timeline, not from any measurement","2017 Science Channel segment in which experts reportedly said the rocks sit on sand a few thousand years old, which suggests a man-made origin but gives no date near 1.75 million years","Geological work that treats the chain as limestone shoals and a drowned land connection from the Holocene, when sea levels rose"],"buildsOn":["Puranic yuga chronology (Treta Yuga)","Ramayana Setu-bandhana narrative","Science Channel 2017 'Ancient Land Bridge' segment"],"critiques":["NASA has publicly said that its images do not show the origin or age of the feature and that it is a natural chain of shoals.","No radiometric or stratigraphic method yields 1.75 million years. Hominins in this region and Sri Lanka's separation from India point to far younger dates, in the Holocene.","The Science Channel segment spoke of thousands of years, not millions, and framed the man-made idea as a question.","Fact-checks by outlets such as Alt News, BOOM and India Today have called the NASA claim false or misleading.","The number is circular: it is the Treta Yuga date restated, so it cannot independently confirm that date."],"plausibility":1,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["NASA Earth Observatory / NASA statements on Adam's Bridge imagery (not retrieved)","Science Channel, 'Ancient Land Bridge' (2017) (not retrieved)","Wikipedia: Adam's Bridge (not retrieved)","Fact-checks by Alt News, BOOM, India Today (not retrieved)"]},{"id":"rm-bakker-ayodhya-gupta","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Hans T. Bakker","proposerInfo":"Dutch Indologist, Professor of Indian Studies at the University of Groningen; historian of Gupta-era religion, Ayodhya and the Skandapurana","event":"Identification of Ayodhya as the Rama cult centre (Saketa re-identified with Rama's Ayodhya). This does not date Rama or the Ramayana text.","dateLabel":"c. 4th-5th century CE (Gupta period, esp. Skandagupta, r. c. 455-467 CE). Cult and site marker, not a Rama or text date.","year":450,"yearLow":300,"yearHigh":500,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Ayodhya (Groningen Oriental Studies I, Egbert Forsten, Groningen, 1986); \"Ayodhya: A Hindu Jerusalem\", Numen 38(1), 1991","workYear":1986,"summary":"Bakker argues from inscriptions, Sanskrit literature and Puranic material that the ancient city called Saketa was identified with the Ramayana's Ayodhya around the Gupta period. He links this to Gupta royal patronage of Vishnu and Rama imagery, with Skandagupta's association with Ayodhya as a key moment, and to Kalidasa's Raghuvamsa treating Ayodhya as the Raghu capital. The Rama-centred pilgrimage geography (the Ramajanmabhumi tradition) developed in later centuries. It is a history of the cult and toponym, so it gives a terminus for the Rama-at-Ayodhya cult, not for the epic story. Details are from background knowledge and should be checked against the book.","keyEvidence":["Early Buddhist and Jain sources use Saketa for the city rather than Ayodhya","Gupta-period epigraphy and coinage tied to Ayodhya, including Skandagupta's connection to the city","Kalidasa's Raghuvamsa presenting Ayodhya as the Raghu capital","Later Puranic and Mahatmya texts building the Rama sacred geography"],"buildsOn":["Alexander Cunningham (Saketa-Ayodhya identification)","Gupta epigraphy","Kalidasa's Raghuvamsa","Puranic Ayodhya Mahatmya literature"],"critiques":["Not a date for Rama or the Ramayana, so it should be shown as a cult/site marker, not an epic date","Partly argues from silence in earlier sources","Archaeology at Ayodhya (B. B. Lal, ASI excavations) is contested and may not fit a purely Gupta-era identification","Traditionalist scholars reject the Saketa and Ayodhya distinction"],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Hans T. Bakker, Ayodhya, Groningen Oriental Studies I, Egbert Forsten, 1986","Hans T. Bakker, 'Ayodhya: A Hindu Jerusalem', Numen 38(1), 1991, pp. 80-109"]},{"id":"rm-cunningham-ayodhya-saketa","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Alexander Cunningham","proposerInfo":"British Army engineer and first Director General of the Archaeological Survey of India (1861-65, 1871-85); pioneer of Indian archaeology and numismatics.","event":"Identification of Ramayana's Ayodhya with historical Saketa (Kosala capital); Ramayana setting treated as pre-Buddhist legend. Not a dating of Rama or the epic.","dateLabel":"No numeric date; context marker (Ayodhya/Saketa already a city in the Buddha's time, c. 6th-5th c. BCE)","year":null,"yearLow":null,"yearHigh":null,"method":"Archaeological","work":"The Ancient Geography of India, Vol. I: The Buddhist Period (1871)","workYear":1871,"summary":"Cunningham's Ancient Geography of India used the Chinese pilgrims' accounts (Fa-hien, Hiuen Tsang) together with Buddhist and Brahmanical literature to locate ancient cities. He equated Ayodhya of the Ramayana with Saketa, the Kosala city where the Buddha stayed, and placed it near modern Ajodhya/Faizabad. The Rama story was treated as an older legendary tradition, not as dated history, so he gave no numeric date for Rama or the epic. Its value for a timeline is as a context marker: the earliest scholarly archaeological-geographical anchor linking the Ramayana's city to a historically attested site, with Buddhist-era (c. 6th-5th c. BCE) attestation as the earliest firm horizon.","keyEvidence":["Identification of Saketa with Ayodhya from Buddhist texts and Chinese pilgrim itineraries (Fa-hien's Sha-chi, Hiuen Tsang's A-yu-t'o)","Topographical fit with the Kosala capital near the Sarayu/Ghaghara","Ramayana described as a pre-Buddhist legendary tradition; no calendrical or astronomical computation","Later ASI-era excavations at Ayodhya (B.B. Lal 1970s; Northern Black Polished Ware from c. 7th-6th c. BCE) are the archaeological reference for the site"],"buildsOn":["Fa-hien (Faxian)","Hiuen Tsang (Xuanzang)","Buddhist Pali texts on Saketa","Horace Hayman Wilson and earlier Orientalist geography"],"critiques":["Gives no date, so it cannot be set against astronomical or genealogical datings of Rama","Hans Bakker argues the Ayodhya-Saketa equation and Ayodhya's identity as Rama's city developed later, under the Gupta-era (c. 5th c. CE) tradition","Excavations show urban occupation from c. 7th-6th c. BCE, with no clear evidence of an earlier large city, which is used against deep-antiquity Ramayana claims; interpretations of B.B. Lal's findings are politically contested","Equating a legendary city with a historical site does not show that the epic's events occurred or when"],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Cunningham, A., The Ancient Geography of India, Vol. I: The Buddhist Period, London: Trubner, 1871","Bakker, H., Ayodhya (Groningen Oriental Studies), 1986","Lal, B.B., Archaeology and the two Indian epics / Ayodhya excavation reports (Indian Archaeology: A Review, 1976-77 and 1979-80)","Wikipedia: Alexander Cunningham; Ayodhya; Saketa"]},{"id":"rm-jain-munisuvrata-era","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Jain tradition (Vimalasuri; Ravishena; Gunabhadra; Hemachandra)","proposerInfo":"Medieval Jain acharyas and poets (Svetambara and Digambara) who retold the Rama story within the Jain cosmic-history framework of 63 Shalakapurushas.","event":"Life and career of Rama (Padma), the 8th Baladeva, in the era of the 20th Tirthankara Munisuvrata","dateLabel":"Relative only: era of Munisuvrata, 20th Tirthankara, between Munisuvrata and Nami. No absolute year is given. Implied ~1.5-1.7 million years before Mahavira on the canonical Jain interval figures, so about -1,600,000 as a plotting placeholder. Low confidence.","year":-1600000,"yearLow":-1700000,"yearHigh":-1500000,"method":"Traditional yuga chronology","work":"Paumacariya (Prakrit) by Vimalasuri; Padmapurana/Padmacarita by Ravishena (678 CE); Uttarapurana by Gunabhadra (9th c.); Trishashtishalakapurushacharita, Parva 7, by Hemachandra (12th c.)","workYear":null,"summary":"Jain texts place Rama not in an absolute calendar but in the Jain cosmic-time scheme. Rama (Padma) is the 8th Baladeva, Lakshmana (Narayana) the 8th Vasudeva, and Ravana the 8th Prativasudeva. All three are among the 63 Shalakapurushas and are placed in the age of the 20th Tirthankara, Munisuvrata, after him and before Nami. Any absolute figure has to be derived by adding the canonical Jain intervals between Tirthankaras. Those intervals run to hundreds of thousands of years, so the implied date is over a million years before Mahavira. In the Jain versions Rama is a non-violent exemplar, Lakshmana kills Ravana, and Rama ends as a Jain monk who attains liberation.","keyEvidence":["Tirthankara placement: Rama/Padma, Lakshmana and Ravana are set in the time of the 20th Tirthankara Munisuvrata. This is consistent across the Svetambara and Digambara tellings. Verified from background knowledge only.","Vimalasuri's Paumacariya is the earliest Jain Ramayana. Its internal date is the Vira Nirvana year 530 (about 4 CE). Modern scholars such as Jacobi and Kulkarni put it roughly in the 1st to 4th century CE.","Ravishena's Sanskrit Padmapurana is dated 678 CE and follows Vimalasuri closely.","Gunabhadra's Uttarapurana (Digambara, 9th c.) preserves a divergent tradition in which Sita is born as Ravana's daughter.","Hemachandra (12th c.) retells the story in the Trishashtishalakapurushacharita, Parva 7, the Svetambara version.","Chronology numbers (from memory, not checked against a source in this run): Munisuvrata to Nami is about 11 lakh years, Nami to Nemi about 5 lakh years, Nemi to Parshva about 84,000 years, Parshva to Mahavira 250 years. Munisuvrata's lifespan is given as 30,000 years and Rama's as about 17,000 years. Treat all of these as approximate."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana (as a story source, which it revises)","Jain Shalakapurusha cosmology","Vimalasuri (built on by Ravishena, Hemachandra and Svayambhu)"],"critiques":["It is a mythic-cosmological placement, not a historical dating. The figures are schematic and cannot be tested against archaeology or astronomy.","The implied span of over a million years is far outside the human archaeological record and the Indian literate record.","The sources are late. The earliest text is about the 1st to 4th century CE and the others are 7th to 12th century CE. They reworked an already existing Ramayana story and did not preserve an independent record.","Svetambara and Digambara sources differ on details and on some Tirthankara interval numbers. The Uttarapurana version also differs in plot from Vimalasuri's.","Its function is theological: to bring Rama into the Jain scheme and answer Brahmanical portrayals, for example by denying that Ravana was killed by a violent Rama. It is not a chronological argument."],"plausibility":1,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Vimalasuri, Paumacariya (ed. H. Jacobi, 1914)","Ravishena, Padmapurana (Jnanpith edition)","Gunabhadra, Uttarapurana","Hemachandra, Trishashtishalakapurushacharita, Parva 7 (tr. H. M. Johnson, Gaekwad's Oriental Series)","Wikipedia: Rama in Jainism; Munisuvrata; Paumachariya","V. M. Kulkarni, The Story of Rama in Jain Literature (1990)"]},{"id":"rm-vimalasuri-paumacariya-text","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Vimalasuri (author's own colophon); modern dating by Hermann Jacobi and later scholars","proposerInfo":"Vimalasuri was a Jain monk-poet of the Nagila lineage; Hermann Jacobi (1850-1937) was a German Indologist who edited the Paumacariya in 1914.","event":"Composition of the Paumacariya, the earliest surviving Jain Ramayana in Maharashtri Prakrit (it does not date Rama's own era)","dateLabel":"530 years after Mahavira's nirvana, about 3 CE (using the traditional 527 BCE) or about 63 CE (using about 467 BCE). Scholars place it from the 1st to the 4th century CE.","year":3,"yearLow":3,"yearHigh":400,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Paumacariya (Padmacarita), Prakrit; ed. H. Jacobi, Bhavnagar 1914","workYear":1914,"summary":"The closing verses of the Paumacariya say the work was completed 530 years (and some months) after Vira's (Mahavira's) nirvana. With the traditional nirvana of 527 BCE that gives about 3 CE. With the later Western date of about 467 BCE it gives about 63 CE. Jacobi accepted a date near the early centuries CE. Many later scholars, citing language, Greek and Roman astronomical terms and Jain doctrinal features, think the work is later (3rd-4th century CE) or that the colophon is a later addition. The text is a rationalising Jain retelling of Rama's story, so it is a datum for the history of the Ramayana tradition. It gives no date for Rama himself.","keyEvidence":["Colophon verse (about 118.102) gives 530 years after Vira's nirvana. I recalled this from memory and did not check the verse number against the text.","The conversion depends on the Mahavira date: 527 BCE (traditional Svetambara), about 468 BCE (Digambara/Jacobi) or about 400 BCE (some modern scholars).","Archaic Maharashtri Prakrit that Jacobi judged close to the Prakrit of the early centuries CE.","Content that sets the date later: Greek-derived astrological terms and Jain doctrine similar to that of Umasvati's period."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana (rejected or rationalised)","Jain Nirvana-era chronology","Hermann Jacobi"],"critiques":["The Mahavira date is uncertain by more than a century, so the conversion is not fixed.","Some scholars (for example K. R. Chandra) argue the colophon is a later interpolation and that the text is from about the 3rd-4th century CE.","Some scholars (for example Jacobi and Kulkarni) see the work as influenced by the Valmiki Ramayana, and so as a source for the history of the text, not an independent record of Rama's era.","It gives no date for the events of the Ramayana."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Hermann Jacobi (ed.), Paumacariyam of Vimalasuri, Bhavnagar, 1914","Wikipedia, 'Paumacariya' (not fetched in this session)","V. M. Kulkarni, Story of Rama in Jain Literature (1990)","K. R. Chandra, A Critical Study of Paumacariyam (1970)"]},{"id":"rm-jain-apabhramsha-svayambhu","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Svayambhu (Paumachariu) and Gunabhadra (Uttarapurana, completed by his pupil Lokasena)","proposerInfo":"Svayambhu: Apabhramsha poet, son of Maruideva, patronised by Dhananjaya and Dhavaliya. Gunabhadra: Digambara Jain monk-scholar, pupil of Jinasena. The two are the dates of Jain retellings. They are not event-dating scholars.","event":"Composition of Jain Rama narratives (Padmacarita / Paumachariu and the Rama section of the Uttarapurana). It does not date Rama's life.","dateLabel":"c. 8th-9th c. CE (Svayambhu c. 770-840 CE; Uttarapurana completed c. 898 CE)","year":800,"yearLow":750,"yearHigh":900,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Paumachariu (Svayambhu, Apabhramsha) and Uttarapurana (Gunabhadra, Sanskrit), 8th-9th c. CE","workYear":800,"summary":"These are not attempts to date the historical Rama. They are Jain retellings that place Rama (Padma) as the 8th Baladeva, in the Jain universal history, in the age of the Tirthankaras Munisuvrata and Nami. Their value for a timeline is as a terminus ante quem for a Jain Rama tradition and as evidence of how the story changed. Svayambhu follows Ravisena's Padmapurana (678 CE), which in turn follows Vimalasuri's Paumachariya. Gunabhadra's Uttarapurana keeps a separate older strand in which Sita is born as Ravana's daughter and Lakshmana kills Ravana. The 8th-9th c. dates come from literary and manuscript history, so they are fairly secure for the composition of the texts. Svayambhu's exact period is debated within that range.","keyEvidence":["Svayambhu's Paumachariu is the earliest surviving Apabhramsha Rama epic, in 5 kandas.","It adapts Ravisena's Padmapurana (678 CE), so it must be later than that.","Gunabhadra's Uttarapurana was completed by Lokasena in about 898 CE (Saka 820), a date given in its colophon tradition. It has the variant in which Sita is Ravana's daughter.","In the Jain scheme Rama is a Baladeva in the Tirthankara era, with no fixed absolute date.","Both texts are later than Valmiki, so they cannot be independent evidence for the date of the events."],"buildsOn":["Vimalasuri (Paumachariya, Prakrit)","Ravisena (Padmapurana, 678 CE)","Valmiki Ramayana (as reworked by Jain authors)"],"critiques":["They date the retelling, not the events. They are a late derivative tradition and tell us nothing about when Rama lived.","The Jain account is deliberately a rationalising counter-narrative. It has Ravana as a pious Jain-sympathetic figure and Lakshmana, not Rama, as the killer.","Svayambhu's exact date is uncertain within the 8th-9th c. CE and is reconstructed from his patrons and his literary relationships.","The Jain Tirthankara chronology is mythic and cannot be tied to archaeology."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Wikipedia: Svayambhu","Wikipedia: Jain Ramayana (Paumachariya / Padmapurana)","Wikipedia: Jinasena and Gunabhadra, Mahapurana / Uttarapurana","K. R. Chandra, Paumachariya studies","Nathuram Premi, Jain Sahitya aur Itihas"]},{"id":"rm-dasharatha-jataka","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Albrecht Weber (priority thesis, 1870); Buddhist Jataka tradition (text)","proposerInfo":"Weber was a German Indologist (Berlin) who argued Valmiki's epic derives from the Buddhist Dasharatha Jataka; the Jataka itself is an anonymous Pali text of the Khuddaka Nikaya.","event":"Earliest textual version of the Rama story / relative priority of the Dasharatha Jataka vs Valmiki Ramayana (not a calendar date of Rama himself)","dateLabel":"Jataka collection c. 3rd c. BCE (verses) to 4th-5th c. CE (prose commentary); priority vs Valmiki debated","year":-250,"yearLow":-300,"yearHigh":450,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Weber, \"Ueber das Ramayana\" (Berlin Academy, 1870); counter: H. Jacobi, \"Das Ramayana\" (1893); Dineshchandra Sen, \"The Bengali Ramayanas\" (1920)","workYear":1870,"summary":"Jataka No. 461 retells Rama as a previous birth of the Buddha: Rama Pandita, Sita and Lakkhana are children of King Dasaratha of Benares, exiled by a queen's scheme for her son Bharata. Rama returns after the twelve years, and marries his sister Sita. There is no abduction by Ravana. Weber took this plainer story as the older seed that Valmiki elaborated, a view echoed by Dineshchandra Sen and Camille Bulcke. Jacobi and most Indian scholars hold the reverse: the Buddhists adapted and stripped a known epic story to fit Buddhist doctrine. The canonical Jataka verses are probably 3rd c. BCE or later, while the prose commentary is attributed to c. 5th c. CE. This gives a terminus ante quem for Rama lore, not a date for Rama.","keyEvidence":["Jataka 461 (Dasaratha Jataka, Dasanipata): Rama Pandita, Lakkhana and Sita as children of Dasaratha, king of Benares; Rama is identified with the Buddha, Dasaratha with Suddhodana, Sita with Rahulamata, Bharata with Ananda.","Sita is Rama's sister and later consort; there is no Ravana, no abduction and no Lanka war, which Weber read as a primitive form and Jacobi read as a deliberate Buddhist simplification.","The Jataka verses are older than the prose commentary, which Western scholars generally date to the 5th c. CE and which is attributed by tradition to Buddhaghosa's circle; a Bharhut relief (c. 2nd c. BCE) shows that Jataka stories were already circulating.","Another Jataka (No. 263, Anamaka) and Buddhist and Jain versions (Vimalasuri's Paumacariya) show an independent Rama tradition in other sects."],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber","Hermann Jacobi","Dineshchandra Sen","Camille Bulcke","Arthur A. Macdonell","A. B. Keith"],"critiques":["Jacobi (1893) argued the Jataka is a distorted, secondary reworking and that the core Valmiki books 2-6 predate Buddhism.","Jataka prose is late (c. 5th c. CE) and a Buddhist author had reason to moralize, rename and simplify, so simplicity does not prove priority.","The Mahabharata's Ramopakhyana and the Valmiki text may predate the Jataka verses; the Jataka's date is itself uncertain and has no firm anchor.","The Jataka gives no astronomical or historical date for Rama; it only bounds the story's circulation."],"plausibility":4,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Jataka No. 461, Dasaratha Jataka (Cowell ed., Cambridge, 1895-1907, vol. IV)","A. Weber, Ueber das Ramayana, Berlin 1870","H. Jacobi, Das Ramayana, Bonn 1893","Dineshchandra Sen, The Bengali Ramayanas, Calcutta 1920","Camille Bulcke, Ramkatha: Utpatti aur Vikas, 1950","Wikipedia: Dasharatha Jataka; Ramayana"]},{"id":"rm-liudu-ji-jing-251","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Kang Senghui (translator); scholarly use by Camille Bulcke and John Brockington","proposerInfo":"Kang Senghui was a Sogdian-descended Buddhist monk active in Wu (Jianye), d. 280 CE. Bulcke was a Belgian-Indian Jesuit Hindi scholar who wrote Ramkatha. Brockington is an Edinburgh Indologist who wrote Righteous Rama.","event":"Terminus ante quem for circulation of the Rama story (an early Chinese Buddhist version). It does not date Rama or the Valmiki text.","dateLabel":"251 CE (approx., Liudu ji jing translation); latest-possible date for the Rama tale, not a date for Rama","year":251,"yearLow":200,"yearHigh":280,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Liudu ji jing (Taisho 152), trans. Kang Senghui, c. 251 CE; discussed in Bulcke, Ramkatha (1950) and Brockington, Righteous Rama (1984)","workYear":251,"summary":"The Liudu ji jing is a Jataka-style collection translated into Chinese by Kang Senghui in Wu, traditionally dated to about 251 CE. It contains a Rama-like tale of a king whose wife is abducted and recovered. Bulcke and Brockington cite it, with the Pali Dasaratha Jataka and later Chinese versions, as evidence that the Rama story circulated widely by the early centuries CE. It therefore gives only a latest-possible date for the story. Brockington treats the Buddhist versions as derived from, or parallel to, the Rama tradition rather than its source. Verified from background knowledge only; details of the specific tale were not checked.","keyEvidence":["Kang Senghui's translation is traditionally placed around 251 CE in Wu","The collection contains a Rama-like abduction-and-recovery tale","Parallel Buddhist versions: the Dasaratha Jataka and the later Za baozang jing"],"buildsOn":["Camille Bulcke","John Brockington","Dasaratha Jataka"],"critiques":["It is only a terminus ante quem and gives no date for Rama or the epic's origin","The Chinese tale diverges substantially from Valmiki, so it may reflect an independent or Buddhist-adapted tradition","The exact translation year is traditional and approximate"],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Bulcke, Camille. Ramkatha: Utpatti aur Vikas, 1950","Brockington, John. Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic, Oxford University Press, 1984","Taisho Tripitaka no. 152, Liudu ji jing"]},{"id":"rm-jacobi-composition","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Hermann Georg Jacobi","proposerInfo":"German Indologist and Sanskritist (1850-1937), professor at Bonn; also known for Jaina studies and for astronomical dating of Vedic texts.","event":"Composition of the original (core) Valmiki Ramayana text","dateLabel":"Core c. 800-500 BCE (pre-Buddhist); secondary summaries give 700-300 BCE or 5th-4th c. BCE; later books and additions to c. 200 CE","year":-600,"yearLow":-800,"yearHigh":-500,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Das Ramayana: Geschichte und Inhalt, nebst Concordanz der gedruckten Recensionen (Bonn: Cohen, 1893)","workYear":1893,"summary":"Jacobi analysed the Ramayana's content, language, geography and printed recensions. He argued that books 2-6 form an old core composed before the rise of Buddhism, and that books 1 and 7 were added later. He held that the core predates the Mahabharata in its present form, because the Ramayana shows no knowledge of the Pandava story while the Mahabharata knows the Rama tale. His inference rests on internal cultural and political signals, not on astronomy. Secondary sources state his range differently, so the 800-500 BCE figure is a reconstruction of his position and not a quotation from the book.","keyEvidence":["Ayodhya appears as the Kosala capital, with no sign of the later centres Saketa or Sravasti, which suggests a pre-Buddhist political setting.","The core shows no awareness of Buddhism.","The Ramayana does not mention the Mahabharata heroes, while the Mahabharata contains Rama episodes (the Ramopakhyana), which implies the Ramayana is older.","Books 1 and 7 differ in style and content and treat Rama as an avatar of Vishnu, which marks them as later additions.","He argued that the Buddhist Dasaratha Jataka derives from the Ramayana, not the reverse."],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber (Ramayana as allegory of Aryan spread, which Jacobi rejected)","Earlier editions and recensions by Gorresio and the Bombay and Bengal editions","Adolf Holtzmann Jr. on the Mahabharata"],"critiques":["Dating by silence (no mention of Buddhism or the Pandavas) is weak evidence.","Later scholars such as Winternitz, Brockington and Goldman accept a pre-Buddhist kernel but see multi-stage growth, with the core usually placed in the 5th-4th c. BCE and the final text by c. 2nd c. CE.","Critical-edition work shows a fluid text with several recensions, so a single composition date is too simple.","Traditional and Hindu-nationalist scholars reject the late date as unsupported by archaeology of the Treta Yuga or the traditional chronology."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Hermann Jacobi, Das Ramayana: Geschichte und Inhalt, nebst Concordanz der gedruckten Recensionen, Bonn 1893","Moriz Winternitz, A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1","John Brockington, The Sanskrit Epics (Brill, 1998)","Robert Goldman, Introduction to The Ramayana of Valmiki, vol. 1 (Princeton, 1984)","Wikipedia: Ramayana; Hermann Jacobi"]},{"id":"rm-weber-buddhist-derived","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Albrecht Weber","proposerInfo":"German Indologist (1825-1901), professor of Sanskrit at Berlin and member of the Prussian Academy of Sciences.","event":"Composition of the Valmiki Ramayana, argued to derive from a Buddhist Rama tradition","dateLabel":"c. 3rd century BCE or later (post-Buddha, Hellenistic-era influence). The century is my approximation of his stated position, not a quotation.","year":-250,"yearLow":-300,"yearHigh":-100,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Ueber das Ramayana, Abhandlungen der Koeniglichen Akademie der Wissenschaften zu Berlin","workYear":1870,"summary":"Weber argued that the Rama story first existed in a simpler Buddhist form, the Dasharatha Jataka, and that Valmiki's epic is a later Brahmanical elaboration of it. He also proposed that the abduction of Sita and the war at Lanka were shaped by the Homeric Iliad (Helen and Troy), reaching India after Alexander's invasion. This places the epic after Buddhism and in the Hellenistic age, around the 3rd century BCE or later. His thesis was widely rejected by later scholars, notably Jacobi.","keyEvidence":["Dasharatha Jataka (Jataka no. 461) presents Rama, Sita and Lakshmana in a simpler Bodhisatta story, which Weber took as the earlier form","Comparison of Sita's abduction and the siege of Lanka with Helen and Troy, read as Greek influence","Weber also read the Rama and Sita legend as an allegory of Aryan agriculture (Sita as the furrow) spreading south"],"buildsOn":["Buddhist Jataka collection","Homeric epics (comparison)"],"critiques":["Hermann Jacobi (Das Ramayana, 1893) argued the Jataka is a later, garbled summary of the epic, and that the core books 2-6 are pre-Buddhist, with Kosala/Ayodhya as the major power.","Macdonell, Keith, Winternitz and Bulcke rejected the Greek-influence theory; the epic shows no Hellenistic features in language, geography or society.","Sita is attested in Vedic texts as the furrow deity, so the Rama-Sita story does not need a Greek source.","Mainstream scholarship now dates the core of the epic to roughly the 5th-4th century BCE or earlier, with later additions."],"plausibility":2,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Weber, A. (1870) Ueber das Ramayana, Abhandlungen der Koeniglichen Akademie der Wissenschaften zu Berlin","Jacobi, H. (1893) Das Ramayana: Geschichte und Inhalt","Dasharatha Jataka (Jataka no. 461)","Winternitz, M. History of Indian Literature, vol. 1"]},{"id":"rm-lassen-allegory","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Christian Lassen","proposerInfo":"Norwegian-born German Indologist (1800-1876), professor at Bonn; author of Indische Alterthumskunde","event":"Composition of the Ramayana text (and its interpretation as allegory)","dateLabel":"c. 5th c. BCE or later (approximate; the exact century in Lassen's own text is unverified)","year":-450,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":-300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Indische Alterthumskunde, 4 vols (Bonn/Leipzig), 1844-1861; 2nd ed. of vols 1-2, 1867-1874","workYear":1847,"summary":"Lassen, a founder of German Indology, treated the Ramayana as a poetic, partly allegorical account of the Aryan advance into southern India, with Rama's expedition to Lanka standing for Brahmanical and Aryan colonisation of the south. He judged the Ramayana a later and more artful work than the Mahabharata's epic core. That puts its composition roughly in the mid-first-millennium BCE or after. The 5th-century-BCE figure comes from the task prompt and from general scholarly summaries, and I could not confirm it against Lassen's own pages. Treat it as an approximate placement, not a quoted date.","keyEvidence":["Reading the Lanka expedition as an allegory of Aryan colonisation of the Deccan and Ceylon","Relative placement: the Ramayana's more refined style and its developed geography of the south suggest a later date than the Mahabharata core","Rama's treatment as an avatar of Vishnu, taken as a sign of later layers","No planetary or astronomical evidence is used; the argument is philological and historical-geographical"],"buildsOn":["August Wilhelm Schlegel (Bonn Sanskrit tradition)","Early Orientalist philology (William Jones, Colebrooke)"],"critiques":["The allegory reading is speculative; later scholars (Jacobi, Hopkins, Winternitz) treat it as a literary epic with an older core, not as pure allegory","Albrecht Weber (1870) proposed a different derivation, from Homeric influence, so the interpretation is contested","Jacobi (1893) argued the core Ramayana, books 2-6, predates the Buddhist period and is older than Lassen's later placement","Lassen's chronology rests on 19th-century assumptions about Aryan migration that are now heavily revised","Modern critical scholarship dates the core to roughly the 5th to 4th c. BCE, with later additions, which is broadly close to his estimate"],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Lassen, C. Indische Alterthumskunde, Bonn, 1844-1861 (archive.org and Google Books hold scans)","Winternitz, M. A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1 (discusses Lassen, Weber and Jacobi on the Ramayana)","Jacobi, H. Das Ramayana, Bonn, 1893","Weber, A. Ueber das Ramayana, Berlin, 1870","Wikipedia: Christian Lassen"]},{"id":"rm-oldenberg-ballad","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Hermann Oldenberg","proposerInfo":"German Indologist (1854-1920), professor at Kiel and Göttingen; Vedic and Pali scholar and analytic critic of the epics","event":"Origin of the Rama story as an early ballad or heroic poem, older than Valmiki's composed epic","dateLabel":"c. 5th-4th c. BCE or earlier (scholarly approximation; Oldenberg gave no precise date that I could confirm)","year":-450,"yearLow":-600,"yearHigh":-350,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Not pinned down. Likely Oldenberg's epic-analysis writings (e.g. Die Literatur des alten Indien, 1903; Das Mahabharata, 1922). The Rama-ballad idea is closely tied to Hermann Jacobi's Das Ramayana (1893).","workYear":1903,"summary":"Oldenberg belonged to the German analytic school, which treated the Indian epics as layered growth from early ballad and bardic narrative into later literary epics. On this view the core Rama story circulated as a ballad before Valmiki's redaction. Such a ballad would fall around the pre- or early-Buddhist period, roughly the 5th-4th century BCE or earlier, with the Valmiki epic growing by accretion afterwards. The date range is a modern approximation of this scholarly consensus, not a figure I could verify as Oldenberg's own. I could not confirm the exact work or passage; web sources were not consulted, so this rests on background knowledge.","keyEvidence":["Stylistic and linguistic stratification: a simple narrative core versus later additions such as Books 1 and 7","The Buddhist Dasaratha Jataka, which gives a different, shorter Rama tale and shows Rama stories circulating independently of Valmiki","Rama's divinity (avatar status) appears mainly in the later layers, not the oldest core","Oldenberg's general theory of epic origin from akhyana-type ballad material"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi (Das Ramayana, 1893)","Albrecht Weber (earlier analytic work on the Rama legend)"],"critiques":["Relative layering gives no absolute date; the 5th-4th c. BCE figure is inferred, not computed","Oral-formulaic and unitary-composition critics reject the ballad-to-epic accretion model","The Jataka may derive from the epic rather than predate it","Traditionalist dating (Treta Yuga or c. 5000 BCE and earlier) rejects the whole analytic framework"],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Jacobi, H., Das Ramayana: Geschichte und Inhalt, Bonn 1893","Oldenberg, H., Die Literatur des alten Indien, Stuttgart 1903","Oldenberg, H., Das Mahabharata: seine Entstehung, sein Inhalt, seine Form, Göttingen 1922"]},{"id":"rm-brockington-composition","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"John Brockington","proposerInfo":"Indologist and Sanskritist, Emeritus Professor of Sanskrit, University of Edinburgh; leading Western scholar of the Sanskrit epics.","event":"Composition and textual growth of the Valmiki Ramayana (the text, not the historical date of Rama)","dateLabel":"Core (Books 2-6) c. 500-300 BCE (earliest nucleus sometimes put 7th/6th c. BCE); expanded in stages to c. 2nd-3rd c. CE","year":-400,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":-300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic (Oxford University Press, 1984); The Sanskrit Epics (Brill, 1998)","workYear":1984,"summary":"Brockington uses linguistic, metrical, stylistic and doctrinal analysis to argue the Valmiki Ramayana grew in layers. A core narrative in Books 2-6 was composed roughly in the 5th-4th centuries BCE, with Rama presented mainly as a human hero. Books 1 (Bala) and 7 (Uttara), and the full doctrine of Rama as an avatar of Vishnu, were added later. The text reached approximately its present form by about the 2nd-3rd century CE. This dates the poem, not the events it narrates, and uses no astronomical or yuga reckoning. The exact ranges are from background knowledge and were not checked against the books.","keyEvidence":["Metrical analysis: relative frequency of archaic and developed shloka forms across books","Linguistic and stylistic differences between Books 2-6 and Books 1 and 7","Development of Rama's divinity: human hero in the core, avatar of Vishnu in the later layers","Absence of Buddhist, Mauryan and Greek-period references in the core, and the geography centred on Kosala","Comparison with the Mahabharata's Ramopakhyana and with Buddhist and Jain Rama stories (Dasaratha Jataka)"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi (Das Ramayana, 1893)","A. A. Macdonell and A. B. Keith","Hermann Oldenberg","Moriz Winternitz","Critical Edition of the Valmiki Ramayana (Baroda)"],"critiques":["Traditionalist and astronomical daters (Vartak, Oak, Achar, Saroj Bala) object that it dates the text rather than the events, and ignores the sky data inside the epic.","Stratification by style and metre is subjective, since oral epics can mix archaic and later forms, so the layers are not firmly datable.","The absolute anchors are weak: the 500-300 BCE range rests on relative chronology and the absence of later features, not on external datable evidence.","Some scholars argue for a somewhat later or more unified composition, and the Critical Edition's own recension choices affect the layering."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["John Brockington, Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic, Oxford University Press, 1984","John Brockington, The Sanskrit Epics, Brill, 1998","Wikipedia: Ramayana (dating section)"]},{"id":"rm-goldman-princeton","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Robert P. Goldman (general editor, Princeton Ramayana translation)","proposerInfo":"Sanskritist, Professor of Sanskrit at UC Berkeley; general editor and translator of the Princeton Ramayana series","event":"Composition and textual growth of Valmiki's Ramayana (not Rama's historical date)","dateLabel":"Core Books 2-6 c. 7th-4th c. BCE; Books 1 and 7 added c. 1st-3rd c. CE (as stated in the task; the BCE core bracket is plausible, exact century bounds not independently checked)","year":-500,"yearLow":-700,"yearHigh":300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"The Ramayana of Valmiki: An Epic of Ancient India, Vol. I: Balakanda, Introduction (Princeton University Press, 1984)","workYear":1984,"summary":"Goldman's introduction to Volume 1 of the Princeton translation surveys the scholarly consensus on how the Sanskrit epic was composed. He holds that Books 2-6 form the older core, composed from roughly the 7th-5th century BCE and complete by about the 4th century BCE. Books 1 (Balakanda) and 7 (Uttarakanda) are later additions, placed roughly in the 1st-3rd century CE, a stage at which Rama is increasingly presented as an avatar of Vishnu. The dating is a relative chronology based on language, style, narrative content and the theology of the text. It is not an absolute calendar date for Rama, and it does not claim to date the events narrated.","keyEvidence":["Books 1 and 7 differ in style, metre and diction from Books 2-6.","Books 2-6 present Rama chiefly as an idealized human hero, while Books 1 and 7 firmly present him as the divine avatar of Vishnu.","Book 1 contains a table of contents (Samkshepa-Ramayana) and a passage on the origin of the epic that appear to know the finished text.","Book 1 also contains late-looking episodes, such as those of Visvamitra and the descent of the Ganga.","The epic lacks the clear Buddhist and Greek references that would mark it as post-Mauryan; it shows no knowledge of the Mahabharata's Bhagavad Gita theology in its core.","Linguistic features of the older books are compared with the language of the Mahabharata and the Buddhist Pali texts."],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi (Das Ramayana, 1893)","Hermann Oldenberg","Adolf Holtzmann Jr.","A. K. Warder","Critical Edition of the Ramayana (Baroda Oriental Institute, 1960-75, general editor G. H. Bhatt, U. P. Shah and others)","Collaborative with John Brockington's work on epic chronology (Righteous Rama, 1984)"],"critiques":["Traditional and revisionist chronologists such as Vartak, Nilesh Oak, Saroj Bala and the Kali-era school reject it as conflating the date of composition with the date of events, and they place Rama in the 5th-7th millennium BCE or earlier.","Dating by style and theology is circular and subjective, since earlier and later strata are identified using assumptions about doctrinal development.","Absolute bounds for the core rest on the absence of datable references (for example Buddhism, Greeks), an argument from silence.","The Baroda Critical Edition's reconstructed text is a hyparchetype, so it says little about how much older oral versions were.","Some scholars give slightly different brackets, for example Brockington places the core around the 5th century BCE, and the dates for Books 1 and 7 range from the 2nd century BCE to the 2nd century CE.","Verification note: the exact century bounds quoted for this entry are from the task statement and from background knowledge, and I did not check them against the book's pages."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Goldman, Robert P., ed. and trans. The Ramayana of Valmiki: An Epic of Ancient India, Vol. I: Balakanda. Princeton University Press, 1984.","Brockington, John. Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic. Oxford University Press, 1984.","Jacobi, Hermann. Das Ramayana. Bonn, 1893.","Bhatt, G. H., et al., eds. The Valmiki-Ramayana: Critical Edition. Baroda: Oriental Institute, 1960-75."]},{"id":"rm-pollock-adikavya","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Sheldon Pollock","proposerInfo":"Indologist and Sanskritist (Columbia University; formerly Chicago); translator/editor of Ayodhyakanda (Princeton Ramayana vol. 2, 1986) and author of Language of the Gods in the World of Men (2006).","event":"Composition of the Valmiki Ramayana text (core Books 2-6; Books 1 and 7 as later additions)","dateLabel":"Books 2-6: roughly mid-first millennium BCE (c. 5th-4th c. BCE, pre-Mauryan; exact century not pinned by Pollock); Books 1 and 7: later, c. 1st c. BCE to 2nd c. CE","year":-400,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":-300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Princeton Translation of the Ramayana of Valmiki, vol. 2 Ayodhyakanda (Pollock, 1986) introduction; Language of the Gods in the World of Men (2006); building on Goldman's vol. 1 introduction (1984)","workYear":2006,"summary":"Pollock treats the Ramayana as a literary composition of historical India, not a record of a Treta-yuga event. Following the Princeton series consensus (Goldman and colleagues), he sees Books 2-6 as the older core, composed before the Mauryan period in the mid-first millennium BCE, with Books 1 (Balakanda) and 7 (Uttarakanda) added later, when Rama had become an avatar of Vishnu. He calls it the adikavya (first poem) in a proto-literary sense: the work that models Sanskrit poetic style and later kavya, which he places in the early centuries CE. His date is a loose range from internal and comparative evidence, not a single year. The specific century attributed to him here is not confirmed from the sources.","keyEvidence":["Stylistic and linguistic differences between Books 2-6 and Books 1 and 7, including Rama's divinity (avatar theology appears chiefly in the latter)","Geography and political world of the core text (Kosala, Videha, Ayodhya as capital) fits the pre-Mauryan north Indian mahajanapada world and shows no knowledge of the Mauryan empire","Comparison with Buddhist and Jain Rama stories and with Mahabharata material for relative sequence","Pollock's later argument that the text's literary self-consciousness (the Valmiki origin story of shloka) marks the start of the kavya tradition, with full courtly kavya developing in the early centuries CE"],"buildsOn":["Robert Goldman (Princeton Ramayana vol. 1 introduction, 1984)","Hermann Jacobi (1893 Das Ramayana, early/late layers)","Hermann Oldenberg","Christian Lassen","Adolf Holtzmann"],"critiques":["Refutes or sidelines the traditional Treta-yuga dating and astronomical dates (Vartak, Oak, Saroj Bala) as unsupported by textual-historical method","Critics within philology (e.g. Brockington's layered chronology) differ on the dates of individual layers, some placing the core as early as the 7th-5th c. BCE","Traditionalist authors object that stylistic layering is subjective and that the text's astronomical details point to far earlier observation","The date dates the text, not the events of Rama's life, so it does not directly compete with event-dating schemes"],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Pollock, S. (ed.), The Ramayana of Valmiki: An Epic of Ancient India, vol. 2 Ayodhyakanda, Princeton University Press, 1986","Goldman, R. (ed.), The Ramayana of Valmiki, vol. 1 Balakanda, Princeton University Press, 1984, introduction","Pollock, S., The Language of the Gods in the World of Men, University of California Press, 2006","Brockington, J., Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic, Oxford University Press, 1984"]},{"id":"rm-winternitz","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Moriz Winternitz","proposerInfo":"Austrian Indologist (1863-1937), professor at German University in Prague; author of Geschichte der indischen Litteratur","event":"Composition and growth of the Valmiki Ramayana text (not the events themselves)","dateLabel":"Core before c. 500 BCE (as summarised); text growing c. 3rd c. BCE to 2nd/3rd c. CE","year":-300,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Geschichte der indischen Litteratur, vol. 1 (1908); English trans. A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1 (Calcutta 1927, trans. S. Ketkar), chapters on the Ramayana","workYear":1908,"summary":"Winternitz treated the Ramayana as a work of gradual growth around an older heroic core, ascribed to Valmiki, rather than a record dated by astronomy. He argued that books II-VI form the oldest portion, while books I and VII are later additions, in which Rama is presented as an incarnation of Vishnu. He placed the earliest core well before the Christian era (the summary given here says before c. 500 BCE) and the text reaching roughly its present form between about the 3rd c. BCE and the 2nd/3rd c. CE. The dating concerns the poem's composition, not the epoch of Rama, and he rejected any literal yuga chronology.","keyEvidence":["Linguistic and stylistic comparison: language close to classical epic Sanskrit, simpler and more uniform than the Mahabharata","Internal stratification: books I and VII judged later, with Rama as divine Vishnu avatar","Absence of Buddhist and Greek-era features in the core (the Buddhist Dasaratha Jataka is treated as related to the legend, not as proof of dating)","Mahabharata knows the Rama story (Ramopakhyana) but the Ramayana does not show knowledge of the Mahabharata, used for relative priority"],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber","Hermann Jacobi","Adolf Holtzmann","Christian Lassen"],"critiques":["Philological stratification is subjective, and the same arguments are used for competing layer divisions","Traditionalists (Vartak, Oak, Saroj Bala and others) reject the premise and date Rama's events, not the text, by astronomy","Later critical editions (Baroda) and scholars such as Goldman and Brockington revised the layering and absolute dates, with Brockington placing the core roughly 5th-4th c. BCE","Not an absolute method: the dates are estimates, not computed"],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["M. Winternitz, A History of Indian Literature, Vol. I, University of Calcutta, 1927 (German original 1908)","Robert P. Goldman (ed.), The Ramayana of Valmiki, Vol. I Introduction, Princeton 1984","John Brockington, Righteous Rama, Oxford 1984","Wikipedia: Ramayana (dating section)"]},{"id":"rm-macdonell-keith","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Arthur Anthony Macdonell (with A. Berriedale Keith, as a related scholarly position)","proposerInfo":"Macdonell (1854-1930) was Boden Professor of Sanskrit at Oxford. Keith (1879-1944) was Regius Professor of Sanskrit at Edinburgh and Macdonell's collaborator on the Vedic Index (1912).","event":"Composition of the Valmiki Ramayana text (core poem, and the later expanded form), not the date of Rama's life","dateLabel":"Core c. 500 BCE (roughly 5th to 4th c. BCE); present form by c. 2nd century CE","year":-500,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"A.A. Macdonell, A History of Sanskrit Literature (London: Heinemann, 1900), with Keith's later Classical Sanskrit Literature and A History of Sanskrit Literature (1928)","workYear":1900,"summary":"Western Indologists of the early 20th century treated the Ramayana as a literary composition, not a historical chronicle. Macdonell and Keith saw an older core (Books 2-6) composed in the east, in Kosala, and later enlarged. Books 1 and 7 were seen as later additions that present Rama as an incarnation of Vishnu. The core was placed around the 5th to 4th century BCE, and the present form was taken to be reached by about the 2nd century CE. This dates the poem, not the events it narrates. Both scholars regarded the Ramayana's core as likely older than the Mahabharata's final form, but the two epics were seen as evolving over centuries.","keyEvidence":["Internal stratification: Books 1 (Bala) and 7 (Uttara) show later language and style, and they deify Rama as Vishnu, while Books 2-6 treat him mainly as a human hero.","Geography: the knowledge of the poem is centered on eastern India (Kosala, Mithila), with the south vaguely known, which suggests an eastern origin before the era of Mauryan expansion.","Absence of Greek, Yavana or Buddhist-era references in the core, and relative antiquity of the language and metre (epic shloka, less polished than classical Sanskrit).","Comparison of Rama's story with the Buddhist Dasaratha Jataka, which the older generation of scholars sometimes took as an independent or even source version (Weber's view, which Macdonell and Keith did not accept in full).","Late-period markers in the whole text (some Greek and Shaka mentions in interpolated passages) used to infer a closing date around the 2nd century CE."],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber (Jataka-derived theory, partly refuted)","Hermann Jacobi (Das Ramayana, 1893)","Moriz Winternitz"],"critiques":["Macdonell's History (1900) is a single-author work. Keith's own treatment is in his separate later books, so a joint '1900' citation is inaccurate. Treat this entry as a composite of the two scholars' views.","Later scholarship (for example Goldman's critical-edition introductions, Brockington's 'Righteous Rama' of 1984) revised the stratification and gave a similar but more nuanced range, roughly 5th c. BCE to 2nd c. CE, with a core by the 4th c. BCE.","Traditional and Hindu revivalist scholars reject the premise that the poem is a late composition, and they date the events of Rama's life to the Treta Yuga or to the 5th to 8th millennium BCE.","The method is relative and stylistic, so the absolute date is approximate and has wide error. Early Indologists' assumption of linear growth is also criticised.","Absence of evidence arguments (no Buddhist or Greek mention) are weak for an oral tradition."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["A.A. Macdonell, A History of Sanskrit Literature, 1900 (archive.org)","A.B. Keith, A History of Sanskrit Literature, Oxford, 1928","R.P. Goldman (ed.), The Ramayana of Valmiki, Princeton, vol. 1 Introduction","J. Brockington, Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic, Oxford, 1984","J. Brockington, The Sanskrit Epics, Brill, 1998"]},{"id":"rm-monier-williams","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Monier Monier-Williams","proposerInfo":"Sir Monier Monier-Williams (1819-1899), Boden Professor of Sanskrit at Oxford, compiler of the Sanskrit-English Dictionary (1872).","event":"Composition of the Valmiki Ramayana (as a literary text, not the events)","dateLabel":"c. 500 BCE (approximate; as attributed in the task, not confirmed against the book text)","year":-500,"yearLow":-600,"yearHigh":-400,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Indian Wisdom; or, Examples of the Religious, Philosophical, and Ethical Doctrines of the Hindus (London: W. H. Allen, 1875)","workYear":1875,"summary":"Monier-Williams, writing as a Victorian Sanskritist, treated the Ramayana as the older and more unified epic, a single-author poem attributed to Valmiki. He saw the Mahabharata as a long accretive compilation whose final form came later. This is a relative-chronology judgment based on style and structure, with a round-number date near the middle of the first millennium BCE. I could not retrieve the book, so the exact figure and wording are not confirmed. Treat \"c. 500 BCE\" as the task's attribution. Later scholarship (Jacobi, Winternitz, Goldman) kept the idea that the core of books 2-6 is early, but dates the work's growth over roughly 500 BCE to 200 CE.","keyEvidence":["Ramayana is more uniform in style and metre (mostly epic shloka) and reads as the work of one poet","Mahabharata is an encyclopaedic, multi-layered compilation with didactic additions","Ramayana's geography and society are less developed than the Mahabharata's, which he read as earlier","Traditional attribution to Adi-kavi Valmiki is accepted as an author tradition, not as a date"],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber","Christian Lassen","Horace Hayman Wilson","Traditional Valmiki attribution"],"critiques":["Relative dating by style is subjective, and the epics grew over centuries","Hermann Jacobi (1893) argued that the Ramayana core predates the Mahabharata's final form but placed the work later, c. 200 BCE-200 CE for the extant text","Books 1 and 7 are widely judged later additions, so a single date does not fit the whole text","It dates composition, not the events; it conflicts with traditional Treta Yuga chronology and with astronomical dates such as Vedveer Arya's or Saroj Bala's","Date is 19th-century and predates archaeology of Ayodhya and Lanka sites and critical editions (Baroda)"],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Monier-Williams, M. Indian Wisdom (London, 1875), archive.org / Google Books, not retrieved","Jacobi, H. Das Ramayana (Bonn, 1893)","Winternitz, M. A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1","Goldman, R. (ed.) The Ramayana of Valmiki, Princeton, vol. 1 introduction"]},{"id":"rm-hopkins","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"E. Washburn Hopkins","proposerInfo":"American Indologist, Professor of Sanskrit and Comparative Philology at Yale, president of the American Oriental Society","event":"Composition of the Ramayana (core text)","dateLabel":"c. 500-200 BCE (literary composition of the core, as relayed; not an event date for Rama)","year":-350,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":-200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"The Great Epic of India: Its Character and Origin (New York: Charles Scribner's Sons, 1901)","workYear":1901,"summary":"Hopkins studied the Sanskrit epics as products of gradual literary growth, not as records of datable events. He treated the Ramayana's present form as later than the Mahabharata's Pandava core, and as more polished and more sectarian. He read the Mahabharata's Ramopakhyana (Vana Parva) as a late digest that borrows the Rama story. The 500-200 BCE range comes from the task text, and I could not check it against the book in this session. He gave no astronomical or yuga-based date for Rama.","keyEvidence":["Comparison of epic language and metre with Vedic and Paninian usage","Rama's status as a divine avatar in the text is treated as a late development","The Ramopakhyana in the Mahabharata is read as a borrowing or abridgment of the Rama story","Verse-level and formulaic parallels between the two epics"],"buildsOn":["Adolf Holtzmann Jr.","Albrecht Weber","Georg Buhler"],"critiques":["Hermann Jacobi argued the Ramayana's core is older than the Mahabharata's present form, which reverses Hopkins's order.","Later critical scholarship (Brockington) dates Books 2-6 to roughly the 5th-4th c. BCE, with Books 1 and 7 added later, which is close to Hopkins's range but with a different internal structure.","Traditionalist daters (Vartak, Oak, Achar, Saroj Bala) reject treating the epics as late literary compositions and place the events millennia earlier.","The range is a broad literary bracket, not a date for the events narrated."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["E. Washburn Hopkins, The Great Epic of India: Its Character and Origin (Scribner, 1901)","Wikipedia: Edward Washburn Hopkins","Wikipedia: Ramayana (scholarly dating section)","John Brockington, Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic (1984)"]},{"id":"rm-basham","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"A. L. Basham","proposerInfo":"Arthur Llewellyn Basham (1914-1986), British Indologist; Professor of Asian Civilisations at ANU Canberra, earlier SOAS London.","event":"Composition of the Valmiki Ramayana text (earliest core layer, then later expansion); not the date of Rama himself","dateLabel":"Earliest core c. 4th-3rd century BCE; expanded to roughly present form in the early centuries CE (as relayed; exact wording in the book not checked)","year":-350,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"The Wonder That Was India (Sidgwick and Jackson, London, 1954)","workYear":1954,"summary":"Basham, in his general survey of ancient Indian civilisation, treats the Ramayana as a work of gradual literary growth rather than a record datable to a legendary Treta Yuga king. He places the oldest nucleus around the 4th-3rd century BCE and holds that later additions (notably the first and last books) accrued afterwards, with the epic reaching roughly its present form in the early centuries CE. This is a mainstream philological dating of the text, not of Rama's life, so it is not directly comparable with astronomical or genealogical dates for the events. I could not open the book during this check, so the exact wording and range are from background knowledge and the task text.","keyEvidence":["Stylistic and linguistic differences between the older books (2-6) and the apparently later Books 1 (Bala) and 7 (Uttara)","Absence of developed Rama-as-avatar theology in the oldest layers, with Vishnu identification appearing in later books","Geographic horizon and social setting of the narrative, and comparison with Buddhist and Jain literature and the Mahabharata"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi","Moriz Winternitz","A. A. Macdonell","A. B. Keith"],"critiques":["Dates the text, not the events; traditionalist dating (Vartak, Oak, Achar, Saroj Bala) argues for far earlier events, so the two are not rival answers to the same question.","General-survey level, with wide ranges and little specialist argument.","Later scholarship (e.g. Brockington, Righteous Rama, 1984; Goldman's critical edition introductions) refines the layering, and some place the core earlier, around the 5th century BCE.","Traditionalists argue that oral transmission means composition date and event date can differ greatly, and that linguistic dating of oral epics is uncertain."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["A. L. Basham, The Wonder That Was India, Sidgwick and Jackson, 1954","J. L. Brockington, Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic, Oxford University Press, 1984","R. P. Goldman (ed.), The Ramayana of Valmiki: An Epic of Ancient India, Vol. 1 Introduction, Princeton University Press, 1984"]},{"id":"rm-warder","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"A.K. Warder","proposerInfo":"Anthony Kennedy Warder (1924-2013), Indologist and Buddhologist, University of Toronto; author of Indian Buddhism and the multi-volume Indian Kavya Literature.","event":"Composition of the early core of the Valmiki Ramayana (as the first kavya), not the events of Rama's life","dateLabel":"c. 4th-2nd century BCE (early core; text composition, not Rama's era)","year":-300,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":-200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Indian Kavya Literature, vol. 1 (Motilal Banarsidass, 1972) and later volumes (vol. 2, 1974)","workYear":1972,"summary":"Warder, a literary historian, treats the Ramayana as the adikavya, the first work of conscious poetic art. He places its early core in roughly the 4th-2nd centuries BCE on grounds of style, metre and its relation to later Buddhist and classical kavya such as Asvaghosa. He regards the work as accreted over time, with the first and last books later additions. The date concerns when the poem was composed, not when Rama lived. The exact bounds in his books were not checked here, so the range is taken from the task text and is approximate.","keyEvidence":["Stylistic and metrical development (anustubh sloka, early figures of speech) read as the beginning of the kavya tradition","Poem presupposed by Asvaghosa and later kavya","Internal stratification: books 2-6 older, books 1 and 7 later additions"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi","A.B. Keith","Moriz Winternitz"],"critiques":["Goldman and the Baroda critical edition tradition place the oldest core earlier, around 7th-5th c. BCE, so Warder may be on the late side.","Dates the literary text only, so it neither supports nor refutes traditional or astronomical dates for Rama's era.","Stylistic dating of an oral, accreted epic is imprecise."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["A.K. Warder, Indian Kavya Literature, vol. 1, Motilal Banarsidass, 1972","A.K. Warder, Indian Kavya Literature, vol. 2, Motilal Banarsidass, 1974","Robert P. Goldman, Introduction to The Ramayana of Valmiki, vol. 1, Princeton, 1984"]},{"id":"rm-thapar","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Romila Thapar","proposerInfo":"Historian of ancient India, Professor Emerita at Jawaharlal Nehru University","event":"Composition and evolution of the Rama story and the Valmiki Ramayana text (not a historical Rama)","dateLabel":"c. 5th-4th century BCE onward (core), with later layers into early centuries CE","year":-450,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":-300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Thapar's writings on the Rama story, including essays on Rama and the epic tradition (the exact titles \"Perceiving Rama\" and \"Ramayana: Narrative and Variants\" were not confirmed)","workYear":null,"summary":"Thapar treats the Ramayana as a literary and social product, not a record of a datable historical Rama. She sees the story as an oral and bardic narrative that was composed into a text in stages, with the core (roughly Books 2-6) taking shape from about the 5th-4th century BCE. She places the framing Books 1 and 7, which present Rama as an avatar of Vishnu, as later additions. Versions and retellings (Buddhist, Jain, regional) show how the story changed with its patrons. She does not offer a calendar date for Rama himself, so the date given here is for the text's emergence.","keyEvidence":["Language and style differences between the core books and Books 1 and 7, a view shared with many Indologists","Rama is shown as a human prince in the older layers and as a divine avatar in the later layers","The Dasharatha Jataka, Jain Paumachariya and other variants show multiple versions of the story","No archaeological or inscriptional confirmation of a historical Rama or Ayodhya kingdom of the claimed period; the Ayodhya excavations show early settlement but no epic-era evidence","Her broader view that the itihasa-purana tradition is shaped by lineage-based social history"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi","A. K. Ramanujan","Camille Bulcke","Philological scholarship on layers of the Valmiki Ramayana","Goldman's Princeton critical commentary"],"critiques":["Traditionalist and Hindutva-aligned writers say her view dismisses the epic's own internal astronomical data and the tradition's sense of history","Some scholars argue the stratification of Books 1 and 7 is not certain, and that the core may be later or earlier than her range","The 5th-4th century BCE range is an approximate scholarly consensus and not a firm result, since there are no absolute anchors for the text","Her view is mainstream in academic Indology and aligns with archaeology, but it deliberately gives no date for the events"],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Romila Thapar, Early India: From the Origins to AD 1300 (2002)","Romila Thapar, Interpreting Early India (1992)","Romila Thapar, The Past as Present (2014)","Robert Goldman (ed.), The Ramayana of Valmiki: An Epic of Ancient India (Princeton, 1984 onward)","Wikipedia: Ramayana and Romila Thapar"]},{"id":"rm-bulcke-ramkatha","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Camille Bulcke (Fr. Kamil Bulke)","proposerInfo":"Belgian-born Jesuit priest and Indian citizen (1909-1982); Hindi professor at St Xavier's College, Ranchi; D.Phil. Allahabad University","event":"Composition of Valmiki's Ramayana (core text) and its evolution from earlier Rama ballads","dateLabel":"Valmiki core (Books 2-6) c. 3rd c. BCE onward; Books 1 and 7 later additions; pre-Valmiki ballads earlier","year":-300,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":-200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Ramkatha: Utpatti aur Vikas (Hindi doctoral thesis, Allahabad University; published by Hindi Parishad, Allahabad)","workYear":1950,"summary":"Bulcke's thesis is a comparative study of the Rama story across Sanskrit, Pali, Prakrit, Buddhist, Jain and later vernacular sources. He argued that the Rama legend circulated as oral ballads before Valmiki, and that Valmiki shaped these into a single epic whose original core is the middle books (2-6). Books 1 (Bala) and 7 (Uttara) he treated as later additions, in which Rama's divinity (Vishnu avatara) is developed. He placed the core in roughly the 3rd century BCE, with growth continuing for some centuries. This dates the text, not the events of Rama's life. These details come from background knowledge and were not checked against the book, so the exact figures are approximate.","keyEvidence":["Comparison of the Dasharatha Jataka and other Buddhist and Jain Rama versions with Valmiki","Linguistic and stylistic differences between Books 2-6 and Books 1 and 7","Rama portrayed as an ideal human hero in the core books, but as Vishnu's incarnation mainly in the later books","Internal inconsistencies such as the duplicated contents lists in Books 1 and 7"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi (Das Ramayana, 1893)","Albrecht Weber (on the Dasharatha Jataka)","Winternitz and other Western Indologists"],"critiques":["Traditionalists reject the idea of later interpolation and of a pre-Valmiki ballad stage","The 3rd c. BCE figure is approximate and depends on relative-chronology arguments, with no absolute anchor","Dating the text says nothing about when Rama lived, so it does not compete directly with astronomical or genealogical event dates","Later critical scholarship (Goldman and the Baroda Critical Edition) broadly supports Books 1 and 7 being later, but is more cautious on absolute dates"],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Camille Bulcke, Ramkatha: Utpatti aur Vikas, Hindi Parishad Prakashan, Allahabad, 1950","Hermann Jacobi, Das Ramayana, Bonn, 1893","Robert Goldman (ed.), The Ramayana of Valmiki, Princeton University Press, introduction to Book 1"]},{"id":"rm-dc-sen-bengali-ramayanas","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Dinesh Chandra Sen (Rai Bahadur)","proposerInfo":"Bengali literary historian and folklorist (1866-1939), Reader in Bengali at the University of Calcutta, author of History of Bengali Language and Literature (1911).","event":"Relative priority of the Rama story: the Buddhist Dasaratha Jataka (Jataka 461) as an older form than Valmiki's Ramayana","dateLabel":"No firm calendar date confirmed; claims relative priority (Dasaratha Jataka before Valmiki, i.e. a pre-Valmiki ballad stratum, roughly the Buddhist-era centuries BCE). Specific century not verified.","year":null,"yearLow":-600,"yearHigh":-200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"The Bengali Ramayanas (University of Calcutta, 1920; lectures on Bengali Ramayana versions)","workYear":1920,"summary":"Sen, studying the Bengali Ramayanas (Krittivasa and others) and Rama-folklore, argued that the Rama legend began as old popular ballads, and that the Buddhist Dasaratha Jataka, where Rama and Sita are siblings and Rama is a Bodhisattva-like figure, preserves a more primitive form than Valmiki's epic. On this view Valmiki's poem is a later, literary elaboration and Hindu-ized development of the older folk and Buddhist narrative. This follows the Weber-style priority argument, with Sen adding Bengali folk-version evidence. I could not verify the exact century he assigned. Any figure given here is a bracket of the Buddhist-era centuries, not a quoted claim.","keyEvidence":["Dasaratha Jataka (No. 461) is short, has Rama and Sita as brother and sister, and lacks Ravana's abduction of Sita and the Lanka war","Argument that simpler narrative implies earlier narrative","Variant Rama-Sita relations in Bengali and other regional folk Ramayanas used as evidence of an older ballad tradition","Reading Valmiki's Bala and Uttara kandas as later accretions"],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber (1870 essay on the Ramayana, proposing Jataka priority and Homeric influence)"],"critiques":["The Jataka is itself a late-compiled text (the Pali Jataka commentary dates to roughly the 5th century CE), and its Rama story may be a drastically abridged or Buddhist-moralized adaptation of an existing epic","Hermann Jacobi (1893) and others argued from Valmiki's geography and silence about Buddhism that the core Ramayana predates Buddhism, contradicting Jataka priority","Camille Bulcke (Ram Katha, 1950) rebutted the Weber/Sen line, as did Winternitz and later scholars who see the Jataka as derivative","Simplicity is a weak criterion because abridgement also produces simple versions","The ballad-origin theory is unfalsifiable without independent datable ballad texts"],"plausibility":3,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Dinesh Chandra Sen, The Bengali Ramayanas, University of Calcutta, 1920 (archive.org / Wikisource copies exist)","Albrecht Weber, Ueber das Ramayana, 1870","Hermann Jacobi, Das Ramayana, 1893","Camille Bulcke, Ram Katha: Utpatti aur Vikas, 1950","Maurice Winternitz, History of Indian Literature, vol. 1"]},{"id":"rm-rc-dutt","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Romesh Chunder Dutt","proposerInfo":"Indian Civil Service officer, economic historian and translator (1848-1909); author of A History of Civilization in Ancient India and a Rig Veda translation.","event":"Composition of the Ramayana (epic age, post-Vedic)","dateLabel":"Epic Age, roughly 1400-800 BCE (broad era from his periodization, not a single year)","year":-1000,"yearLow":-1400,"yearHigh":-800,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"The Ramayana and the Mahabharata Condensed into English Verse (J. M. Dent, Temple Classics, 1899); periodization in A History of Civilization in Ancient India (1889-90)","workYear":1899,"summary":"Dutt did not compute an astronomical date for Rama. In his translation introduction and his earlier ancient-India history he treated the two epics as products of a post-Vedic Epic Age, which he bracketed at about 1400-800 BCE, after the Vedic age and before the Buddhist or rationalistic age. He saw the stories as rooted in older Kosala and Kuru-Panchala tales, enlarged by successive bards. The numeric range is reconstructed from memory of his periodization and was not checked against the primary text, so treat it as a broad era and not a precise claim.","keyEvidence":["Relative sequencing of Vedic hymns, epics and Buddhist-era literature","Internal evidence of social and political conditions in the epics compared with Vedic and later texts"],"buildsOn":["Nineteenth-century Western Indologists (Max Muller, Weber, Lassen, Monier-Williams)"],"critiques":["The date is a broad era, not an event date; no Rama birth year is given","Nineteenth-century periodization has been superseded by later critical work placing the core Ramayana at about the 7th-4th century BCE with later additions","Exact figures were not verified against the primary text"],"plausibility":6,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Romesh C. Dutt, The Ramayana and the Mahabharata Condensed into English Verse, J. M. Dent, London, 1899","Romesh C. Dutt, A History of Civilization in Ancient India, 1889-90"]},{"id":"rm-consensus-composition","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Modern academic consensus (Goldman, Brockington, Jacobi; summarised on Wikipedia)","proposerInfo":"Indological scholarship: Robert Goldman (UC Berkeley, Sanskritist, Princeton Ramayana translation), John Brockington (Edinburgh), Hermann Jacobi (German Indologist)","event":"Composition of the Valmiki Ramayana text (not the date of Rama's life)","dateLabel":"Oral core c. 8th-4th c. BCE (Wikipedia: 8th-5th to 5th-4th c. BCE); Books 1 and 7 added later; text stable by c. 3rd c. CE","year":-500,"yearLow":-750,"yearHigh":300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Wikipedia 'Ramayana' (dating section), citing Goldman 1984 (Princeton Ramayana vol. 1 introduction), Brockington 1998 (The Sanskrit Epics), Bose 2016","workYear":1998,"summary":"Mainstream philology dates the text, not the events. Books 2-6 form the oldest layer, composed orally and fixed in the mid-first millennium BCE. Books 1 (Bala) and 7 (Uttara) are judged later additions because of differences in style and narrative contradictions, a view going back to Jacobi. Later accretions continued to about the 3rd century CE. The task's \"7th-4th c. BCE\" is a paraphrase. The Wikipedia text I read says \"8th-5th to 5th-4th century BCE\", and Goldman gives 750-500 BCE for the oral composition.","keyEvidence":["Linguistic and stylistic comparison of the books","Narrative contradictions between Books 1 and 7 and the core","Goldman 1984: oral composition c. 750-500 BCE, core not later than 6th-5th c. BCE","Brockington 1998: later stages extending to the 3rd c. CE"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi","Robert Goldman","John Brockington"],"critiques":["Dates composition, not the historical Rama, so it does not directly rebut traditional or astronomical dates for the events.","Dating oral epics by language is imprecise, with wide ranges.","Traditionalist and archaeoastronomy authors reject the late-layer view of Books 1 and 7."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Partly verified","references":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramayana","Goldman, R. (1984) The Ramayana of Valmiki, Vol. 1, Princeton UP","Brockington, J. (1998) The Sanskrit Epics, Brill"]},{"id":"rm-ramopakhyana-mahabharata","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Hermann Jacobi (Ramayana-priority); opposed by E. W. Hopkins and others; refined by John Brockington","proposerInfo":"Indological philology. Jacobi was a German Indologist at Bonn, Hopkins an American Sanskritist at Yale, and Brockington a Sanskritist at Edinburgh.","event":"Composition of the Valmiki Ramayana core (books 2-6), with the Mahabharata's Ramopakhyana (Vana Parva) as terminus ante quem","dateLabel":"Core before the Ramopakhyana, c. 5th-4th c. BCE (full text c. 400 BCE to 200 CE)","year":-350,"yearLow":-500,"yearHigh":200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Jacobi, Das Ramayana: Geschichte und Inhalt (1893); Brockington, Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic (1984) and The Sanskrit Epics (1998)","workYear":1893,"summary":"The Mahabharata contains a condensed Rama story, the Ramopakhyana (Vana Parva, roughly 3.257-275), which can be used to fix a relative date. Jacobi (1893) argued that it summarises Valmiki, so the Ramayana core is older than the Mahabharata's present form. Hopkins and others argued the reverse, or that both drew on shared bardic material. Brockington later concluded that the Ramopakhyana most likely depends on a Ramayana already close to its present form. That puts the core (books 2-6) at about the 5th-4th c. BCE, and books 1 and 7 between about the 2nd c. BCE and 2nd c. CE. The result is a relative chronology with a wide window, not a precise year. The date and chapter numbers here come from background knowledge and were not checked against the sources.","keyEvidence":["The Ramopakhyana retells the Rama story to Yudhishthira as consolation, and its verses closely parallel Ramayana wording","In the core books Rama is mainly a human hero, while the Vishnu-avatar framing appears mainly in books 1 and 7, which are taken as later","Jacobi's arguments from language, style and geography: eastern Kosala setting, and no clear knowledge of Buddhism or Greeks in the core","Brockington's comparison of Ramopakhyana verses with Ramayana verses, which indicates dependence on Valmiki"],"buildsOn":["Hermann Jacobi","E. W. Hopkins (The Great Epic of India, 1901)","Maurice Winternitz","Robert Goldman (Critical Edition introduction)"],"critiques":["It gives only a relative, wide window and no absolute year","The direction of borrowing is disputed, and both epics grew over centuries, so interpolation blurs comparisons","Hopkins argued the Ramayana is later or that both drew on common oral material","Traditional and astronomical daters reject the whole approach, since they place Rama in the Treta Yuga or c. 5000 BCE or earlier"],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Jacobi, H. (1893) Das Ramayana: Geschichte und Inhalt, nebst Concordanz der gedruckten Recensionen. Bonn","Hopkins, E. W. (1901) The Great Epic of India","Brockington, J. (1984) Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic. Oxford University Press","Brockington, J. (1998) The Sanskrit Epics. Brill"]},{"id":"rm-patanjali-mahabhashya","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Patanjali (text) / J. L. Brockington (modern assessment)","proposerInfo":"Patanjali: Sanskrit grammarian, author of the Mahabhashya (c. 150 BCE); John Brockington: Sanskritologist, Univ. of Edinburgh, author of The Sanskrit Epics (Brill, 1998)","event":"Latest-possible (terminus ante quem) awareness of Rama-story material; a lower bound on composition of the Valmiki Ramayana, not a date for Rama","dateLabel":"c. 2nd century BCE (Patanjali, c. 150 BCE) as a lower bound","year":-150,"yearLow":-200,"yearHigh":-140,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"The Sanskrit Epics (J. L. Brockington, Brill, 1998), with Patanjali's Mahabhashya","workYear":1998,"summary":"Patanjali's Mahabhashya, datable to about 150 BCE from its reference to Pushyamitra Sunga's sacrifice, is used as a fixed point in the relative chronology of Sanskrit literature. Scholars cite it as evidence of narrative traditions about Krishna (Kamsa-vadha, Bali-bandha performances), and some also look for Rama-related allusions. Brockington's survey of external evidence places the Ramayana's core (roughly books 2-6) before the 2nd century BCE, with the full text reaching its present form by about the 3rd century CE. The Patanjali evidence gives only a floor, so it tells us the story was current by then and nothing about when Rama lived. Traditional and astronomical daters treat such philological floors as irrelevant to their much earlier dates.","keyEvidence":["Patanjali's date is anchored by Mahabhashya illustrations referring to Pushyamitra's sacrifice (c. 185-150 BCE).","Mahabhashya passages on Panini mention Kamsa-vadha and Bali-bandha dramatic or recitation performances. These are usually cited for Krishna and Vamana-type legends, and I could not verify any explicit Valmiki Ramayana citation.","Brockington's overall chronology: oral epic core c. 5th-4th c. BCE, with later books and additions up to c. 3rd c. CE.","Later anchors: Ashvaghosa's Buddhacarita (c. 1st-2nd c. CE) shows clear knowledge of Valmiki, and Bhasa's Rama plays are also cited."],"buildsOn":["Albrecht Weber","Hermann Jacobi","A. A. Macdonell","Panini's Ashtadhyayi","Brockington's own earlier work"],"critiques":["Absence of an explicit Ramayana citation in Panini and Patanjali is an argument from silence, and can be read both ways (Weber's late-Ramayana view versus traditionalist replies).","It gives only a terminus ante quem for the text and cannot date the events or Rama himself.","Traditionalists argue the grammarians had no occasion to cite epics, so silence proves nothing.","Which Mahabhashya passages truly allude to Rama is uncertain, and the claim needs checking against the critical edition (Kielhorn)."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["J. L. Brockington, The Sanskrit Epics, Brill, 1998","F. Kielhorn (ed.), The Vyakarana-Mahabhashya of Patanjali","J. L. Brockington, Righteous Rama: The Evolution of an Epic, Oxford University Press, 1984","Wikipedia: Patanjali; Mahabhashya; Ramayana (for background only)"]},{"id":"rm-bhasa-plays","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Bhasa (the playwright) as a literary terminus ante quem; modern dating by scholars such as T. Ganapati Sastri, A. D. Pusalker, A. K. Warder and A. B. Keith","proposerInfo":"Bhasa is an early Sanskrit dramatist whose thirteen \"Trivandrum plays\" were found by T. Ganapati Sastri in 1912. Indologists have debated his date ever since.","event":"Latest possible date for composition of Valmiki's Ramayana (terminus ante quem), not the date of Rama","dateLabel":"Bhasa c. 2nd c. BCE to 3rd c. CE (disputed). It only sets a lower bound for Valmiki's text and gives no date for Rama's era.","year":-100,"yearLow":-400,"yearHigh":300,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Pratimanataka and Abhishekanataka, two Ramayana-based Sanskrit plays in the Trivandrum corpus (found 1912, edited by T. Ganapati Sastri)","workYear":1912,"summary":"Bhasa's Pratimanataka and Abhishekanataka dramatise Ramayana episodes. The first covers Rama's exile and Bharata's statue-hall scene. The second covers the Vali, Sugriva and Lanka events up to the coronation. Because they rework the Valmiki story, the epic must be older than Bhasa. Bhasa is dated anywhere from the 4th c. BCE to the 3rd or 4th c. CE, and many scholars favour roughly the 2nd c. BCE to 2nd c. CE. The plays therefore show only that a Valmiki-type Ramayana existed by then. They say nothing about when Rama lived. This entry was written from background knowledge without online verification.","keyEvidence":["The plays dramatise Ramayana episodes, so a Ramayana tradition predates the playwright.","Kalidasa's Malavikagnimitra praises Bhasa as an earlier celebrated dramatist, which gives a lower bound of about the 4th-5th c. CE for Bhasa's latest date.","Bana's Harshacharita also praises Bhasa."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","T. Ganapati Sastri","Kalidasa (Malavikagnimitra)","Bana (Harshacharita)"],"critiques":["Bhasa's own date is unsettled, ranging from the 4th c. BCE to the 4th c. CE.","Authorship of the Trivandrum plays is contested. Some scholars, such as A. B. Keith, argue they are a later Kerala stage tradition rather than Bhasa's own work.","The plays differ from Valmiki in details, so they may draw on other Rama traditions rather than on the received text.","A terminus ante quem only bounds the text from above and cannot date Rama's era or the events."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["T. Ganapati Sastri, Trivandrum Sanskrit Series edition of the Bhasa plays (1912 onward)","Kalidasa, Malavikagnimitra, prologue","A. K. Warder, Indian Kavya Literature","A. B. Keith, The Sanskrit Drama (1924)","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhasa_(dramatist)"]},{"id":"rm-ashvaghosha-buddhacarita","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Ashvaghosha (as a witness; the use as a dating bound is by modern Indologists such as Winternitz, A. B. Keith, Goldman)","proposerInfo":"Ashvaghosha was a Buddhist poet-philosopher of the Kushan era, court-associated with Kanishka, who wrote the Sanskrit Buddhacarita and Saundarananda.","event":"Terminus ante quem for composition of the Valmiki Ramayana (the text existed, and Valmiki was already known as the first poet)","dateLabel":"Text already existing by c. 1st-2nd c. CE (Ashvaghosha c. 80-150 CE); a lower bound on the epic's age, not a date for Rama","year":100,"yearLow":80,"yearHigh":150,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Buddhacarita (Canto 1, v. 43) and Saundarananda, by Ashvaghosha, c. 1st-2nd c. CE","workYear":100,"summary":"Ashvaghosha's Buddhacarita 1.43 says that Valmiki composed the first verse (the adi-kavi tradition), a feat the sage Cyavana did not achieve. This shows the Ramayana and the Valmiki legend were established and prestigious by the Kushan period. Scholars use this as a firm lower bound (terminus ante quem) for the Ramayana's composition, usually alongside Ashvaghosha's apparent familiarity with the Ramayana's content and style. It does not date Rama or the events. It only says the epic was already in circulation by about 100 CE.","keyEvidence":["Buddhacarita 1.43: Valmiki is credited with composing the first metrical verse (padya), which Cyavana did not. This is the adi-kavi tradition.","Ashvaghosha's poetic style and some phrases are widely seen to echo the Ramayana's kavya idiom.","Ashvaghosha is placed under the Kushan king Kanishka (c. 78-150 CE). The dating of Kanishka's reign is itself debated.","Parallels with the Ramayana in Saundarananda are noted by editors such as E. H. Johnston."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana (Bala Kanda adi-kavi/Krauncha-verse episode)","Kushan-era chronology (Kanishka)"],"critiques":["Only a terminus ante quem. It gives no upper bound and no date for Rama's era.","Ashvaghosha's own date depends on the disputed Kanishka era, so the bound could move by a few decades.","The Bala Kanda, with the adi-kavi story, is often argued to be a later addition. Ashvaghosha's remark therefore shows the Valmiki tradition existed, but not necessarily the full seven-book text.","Earlier bounds exist. The Buddhist Dasaratha Jataka and the grammatical and epigraphic evidence, and the Mahabharata's own Ramopakhyana, point to earlier circulation, so this bound is not the tightest.","I could not check the Sanskrit text online in this session. The verse number and content are from background knowledge."],"plausibility":7,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Ashvaghosha, Buddhacarita 1.43, ed. and trans. E. H. Johnston, Acta Orientalia / Panjab University, 1936","Robert P. Goldman, Introduction to The Ramayana of Valmiki, vol. 1, Princeton University Press, 1984","A. B. Keith, A History of Sanskrit Literature, Oxford, 1928","M. Winternitz, A History of Indian Literature, vol. 1, 1927","Wikipedia: Ashvaghosha; Buddhacarita"]},{"id":"rm-kalidasa-raghuvamsha","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Kalidasa (as a literary terminus ante quem; modern Sanskritists place him c. 4th-5th c. CE)","proposerInfo":"Classical Sanskrit court poet and dramatist, traditionally linked with the Gupta king Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya); author of Raghuvamsha, Kumarasambhava, Abhijnanashakuntala","event":"Latest possible date for the Valmiki Ramayana as an established, widely known text (reception lower bound)","dateLabel":"c. 4th-5th century CE (Raghuvamsha composed under the Guptas; a lower bound for the Ramayana text, not a date for Rama)","year":400,"yearLow":350,"yearHigh":450,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Raghuvamsha (Sanskrit mahakavya in 19 cantos; the middle cantos retell the Rama story)","workYear":400,"summary":"Kalidasa's Raghuvamsha narrates the Raghu dynasty, and its Rama cantos follow the outline of the Valmiki Ramayana, treating the story as well known to his audience. Scholars use it, together with the Aihole inscription of 634 CE (which praises Kalidasa), as a firm lower bound: the Ramayana existed in recognisably Valmikian form by the Gupta period at the latest. This dates the text's reception, not Rama's era, and only sets a ceiling on how late the epic can be. Earlier witnesses such as Ashvaghosha's Buddhacharita (c. 1st-2nd c. CE) and Bhasa push that ceiling earlier still. Not a proposal to date the events themselves.","keyEvidence":["Raghuvamsha's Rama cantos draw on Valmiki's narrative (e.g. the Pushpaka return from Lanka, Sita's abandonment) and on the Raghu genealogy","Aihole inscription of Ravikirti (634 CE) names Kalidasa and Bharavi, giving a hard upper limit for Kalidasa","Traditional link to Chandragupta II (c. 375-415 CE) supports the 4th-5th c. CE placement"],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","Puranic Ikshvaku genealogies"],"critiques":["It dates reception, not the events or original composition; the Ramayana is clearly much older.","Kalidasa's own date is uncertain (proposals range from the 1st c. BCE to the 5th c. CE); the Gupta placement rests on tradition and style.","His version differs from Valmiki's in details, so he may have used another recension or oral tradition."],"plausibility":9,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Kalidasa, Raghuvamsha (cantos 10-15 on Rama)","Aihole inscription of Ravikirti, 634 CE","Wikipedia: Kalidasa; Raghuvamsha"]},{"id":"rm-bhavabhuti-bhatti","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Bhatti (Bhattikavya) and Bhavabhuti (Mahaviracarita, Uttararamacarita)","proposerInfo":"Classical Sanskrit poet-dramatists: Bhatti, grammarian-poet of Valabhi (Gujarat); Bhavabhuti, court dramatist of Yashovarman of Kannauj. Neither dates Rama; both are datable literary witnesses.","event":"Composition of Rama retellings (Bhattikavya, also called Ravanavadha; Mahaviracarita and Uttararamacarita). These are terminus ante quem markers for the Rama story, not dates of Rama himself.","dateLabel":"Bhatti c. 600-650 CE (7th c. CE); Bhavabhuti c. 700-750 CE","year":700,"yearLow":600,"yearHigh":750,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Bhattikavya (c. 7th c. CE); Mahaviracarita and Uttararamacarita by Bhavabhuti (c. 700-750 CE)","workYear":700,"summary":"Bhatti's Bhattikavya is a 22-canto mahakavya that retells the Rama story while illustrating Paninian grammar and rhetoric. Its colophon says it was composed at Valabhi under a king Sridharasena, which places it around the 6th-7th century CE, conventionally the 7th. Bhavabhuti, patronised by Yashovarman of Kannauj, wrote the Mahaviracarita (Rama's early life to coronation) and the Uttararamacarita (Sita's abandonment and reunion). Kalhana's Rajatarangini records that Lalitaditya of Kashmir overcame Yashovarman around 740 CE, so Bhavabhuti is dated c. 700-750 CE. For a Ramayana chronology they only show that the Rama story, including the Uttarakanda material, was well established by the 7th-8th century CE. They give no evidence for the date of Rama or of Valmiki's text.","keyEvidence":["Bhattikavya colophon names king Sridharasena of Valabhi; several Valabhi rulers had this name, so the exact reign is debated (6th-7th c. CE)","Kalhana's Rajatarangini names Bhavabhuti among Yashovarman's court poets and records Lalitaditya's defeat of Yashovarman, c. 740 CE","Uttararamacarita dramatises the Uttarakanda episode of Sita's exile and gives it a happy ending, a deliberate departure from Valmiki","Bhattikavya follows the Ramayana storyline canto by canto while illustrating Panini's grammar"],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana"],"critiques":["They are not datings of Rama or of the epic. They only fix a latest-possible point for the story's existence and are best used as a literary-history anchor.","Bhatti's patron Sridharasena is ambiguous among several Valabhi kings, so the date has a margin of up to a century.","Bhavabhuti's dates rest on the Rajatarangini, a 12th-century chronicle with some chronological inaccuracy, though the c. 700-750 CE placement is widely accepted.","Both works rework the story freely (Bhavabhuti changes the ending), so they are weak evidence for the original text."],"plausibility":9,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Wikipedia: Bhattikavya","Wikipedia: Bhavabhuti","Kalhana, Rajatarangini, Book 4 (on Yashovarman and Lalitaditya)","A. B. Keith, A History of Sanskrit Literature (1928)"]},{"id":"rm-kakawin-ramayana-java","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Yogiswara (traditional attribution; the poet is otherwise unknown), with Indonesian and Dutch philologists (Hendrik Kern, C. Hooykaas, Soewito Santoso, Stuart Robson) dating the text","proposerInfo":"Anonymous or traditionally named Old Javanese court poet in Central Java under the Mataram kingdom. Modern dating is by Indologists and philologists of Old Javanese.","event":"Composition of the Old Javanese Kakawin Ramayana (a regional retelling). Thai Ramakien is a later retelling. Neither dates Rama's life.","dateLabel":"Kakawin Ramayana: c. 850-900 CE, usually put in the 9th century under Mataram (the Prambanan reliefs are c. 850 CE). Thai Ramakien: compiled 1797-1807 CE under King Rama I, from older Ayutthaya-era material. Both are composition dates, not dates for Rama.","year":870,"yearLow":850,"yearHigh":900,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Kakawin Ramayana (Old Javanese poem). Critical edition and translation by Soewito Santoso, Ramayana Kakawin (1980), and by Stuart Robson, The Old Javanese Ramayana (2015). Ramakien: Rama I's court version (c. 1797-1807).","workYear":870,"summary":"The Kakawin Ramayana is the oldest and longest kakawin (Old Javanese poem). It is dated to the Central Javanese Mataram period, roughly the mid to late 9th century CE. The dating rests on language, on patronage (traditionally under King Dyah Balitung or a close predecessor), and on its closeness in time to the Ramayana reliefs at Prambanan (c. 850 CE). Its first chapters follow the Sanskrit Bhattikavya (Ravanavadha, c. 7th century CE) closely, and the later chapters follow the Valmiki tradition more loosely. The Thai Ramakien is a separate, much later retelling. Its surviving form was compiled under King Rama I of Bangkok, with earlier Ayutthaya fragments. These works show how the Ramayana spread in Southeast Asia. They give a latest-possible date by which the story was well known, and they do not date the events of the epic.","keyEvidence":["Old Javanese linguistic and metrical features place the poem in the Mataram period of the 9th century CE.","The first chapters (roughly 1 to 14) follow the Sanskrit Bhattikavya (Ravanavadha), so the poem is later than about the 7th century CE.","The Ramayana relief panels at Prambanan (Loro Jonggrang, c. 850 CE) show the story was in Central Java by then.","Thai Ramakien: the extant Rama I text was compiled in 1797-1807. Earlier Ayutthaya-era Ramakien fragments survive, and the Ayutthaya court knew the story much earlier.","Both retellings adapt the story to local settings and add new episodes. They are evidence for transmission, not for the era of Rama."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","Bhattikavya (Ravanavadha)","Hendrik Kern's early work on Old Javanese","C. Hooykaas on the Kakawin Ramayana's sources","Soewito Santoso","Stuart Robson","Ayutthaya-era Ramakien tradition (for the Thai text)"],"critiques":["These works date the retellings, not Rama. They count only as a terminus ante quem for the Ramayana story: it must have existed and spread to Java well before about 850 CE.","The poet's identity (Yogiswara) and the exact patron and date are uncertain. The 9th-century date is a scholarly estimate, not an inscriptional one.","The Ramakien in its surviving form is very late (c. 1800), so it has no weight for dating the epic.","Some popular dating schemes try to use these texts to support early dates for Rama. They cannot do that, because they only show where the story had reached by the 9th century CE."],"plausibility":9,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Stuart Robson, The Old Javanese Ramayana: A New Edition and Translation (2015)","Soewito Santoso, Ramayana Kakawin (1980)","C. Hooykaas, The Old Javanese Ramayana Kakawin (1958)","Wikipedia: Kakawin Ramayana; Ramakien; Prambanan; Bhattikavya"]},{"id":"rm-kamban","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Kamban (Kambar); date assigned by later scholars and tradition","proposerInfo":"Tamil poet of the Chola period, author of the Iramavataram (Kambaramayanam); traditionally linked with Tiruvennainallur and the patron Sadaiyappa Vallal.","event":"Composition of the Tamil retelling (Kamba Ramayanam). It does not date Rama.","dateLabel":"c. 9th-12th century CE. Tradition reads a verse as Saka 807 (885 CE); many scholars prefer the 12th century.","year":1150,"yearLow":885,"yearHigh":1200,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Iramavataram (Kamba Ramayanam), Tamil epic of about 10,000 verses in six kandams","workYear":1150,"summary":"Kamban's Tamil Ramayana is a literary retelling of Valmiki's story. It is not an attempt to date Rama's era. A traditional verse is usually read as giving Saka 807 (885 CE), which would put the work in the 9th century. Many modern historians instead place Kamban in the 12th century under the later Cholas, on linguistic and literary grounds. On a Ramayana-dating timeline it serves as a medieval composition marker that shows how the story was retold. It is not evidence for when Rama lived.","keyEvidence":["A traditional verse is read as Saka 807 (885 CE), supporting the early date; its authenticity and reading are debated.","Language, literary history and patron references are used to argue for the 12th century.","The work follows Valmiki's story with Tamil adaptations, so it counts as a retelling."],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana"],"critiques":["The 885 CE date rests on a disputed verse that some scholars treat as a later interpolation.","Many historians argue for the 12th century instead, so the range is wide.","As a retelling it gives no independent evidence for the date of Rama or the events."],"plausibility":8,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Wikipedia: Kamban","Wikipedia: Kamba Ramayanam"]},{"id":"rm-tulsidas","epic":"Ramayana","proposer":"Goswami Tulsidas","proposerInfo":"16th-century Awadhi poet-saint of the Rama bhakti tradition, associated with Ayodhya and Varanasi","event":"Composition of the Ramcharitmanas, an Awadhi retelling of the Ramayana (dates the text, not Rama's era)","dateLabel":"Begun Ram Navami, Vikram Samvat 1631 (1574 CE); traditionally completed c. 1576-77 CE","year":1574,"yearLow":1574,"yearHigh":1577,"method":"Textual / linguistic","work":"Ramcharitmanas (Sri Ramacharitamanasa), Awadhi, begun 1574 CE","workYear":1574,"summary":"Tulsidas states in the Balakanda that he began the poem in Samvat 1631 on Ram Navami (Chaitra Shukla 9) at Ayodhya, which is 1574 CE. Tradition says it took about 2 years 7 months to finish. This dates a medieval devotional retelling, not Rama's life, so it works as a text-composition marker in the timeline and a terminus for the Ramayana's vernacular reception. The poem follows Valmiki, the Adhyatma Ramayana and other sources. It presents Rama as a divine avatar within the traditional Treta Yuga framework and proposes no historical date for him.","keyEvidence":["Dating verse in the Balakanda: 'sambat solah sai ekatisa, karaun katha hari pada dhari sisa' (Samvat 1631), with the start on Ram Navami at Ayodhya","Samvat minus about 57 gives 1574 CE","Tradition gives 2 years 7 months 26 days to complete, ending on Vivaha Panchami in Samvat 1633 (c. 1576-77 CE); the exact completion date comes from tradition, not the text","Early 17th-century manuscripts and commentaries agree with a 16th-century composition"],"buildsOn":["Valmiki Ramayana","Adhyatma Ramayana","Puranic Rama narratives","Hanuman Nataka","Prasannaraghava"],"critiques":["It does not date the Ramayana events at all, only a late retelling, so it should be shown as a text-composition marker.","The completion date rests on tradition rather than the text itself.","Not an independent source for Rama's historicity, because it relies on earlier texts."],"plausibility":9,"verification":"Background knowledge only","references":["Ramcharitmanas, Balakanda (dating verse, Samvat 1631)","Wikipedia: Ramcharitmanas","Wikipedia: Tulsidas"]}],"analysis":{"Mahabharata":"## Which Mahabharata date is most defensible?\n\n**Short answer.** None of the 44 proposals can be proved. The only dates that stand on independent, cross-checkable evidence are the mainstream ones: a historical kernel in the Kuru–Panchala milieu of roughly 1000–850 BCE, and a text that grew between about 400 BCE and 400 CE. The astronomical dates (5561, 3067, 1478 BCE and others) are internally coherent in places but not mutually compatible. The traditional 3138/3102 BCE date is an inference from a later calendrical epoch, not a record.\n\n### 1. Grouping by method\n\n| Method | Proposals | Typical range |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cosmological/yuga schemes | Jinasena (about -85000), Yukteswar (a yuga cycle, not a war date) | Tens of thousands of years |\n| Greek reports read as Indian history | Megasthenes' \"Heracles\" | About 6400–6800 BCE (modern inference) |\n| Planetarium or astronomical fits | Vartak and Oak (5561), Achar and I-SERVE (3067), Iyengar and Chandra Hari (1478), Daftari (1197), Bankim, Dikshit, Colebrooke/Wilson | 5561–1197 BCE |\n| Era/epoch reckoning | Kali 3138/3102, Aihole 634 CE, Vaidya, Varahamihira/Kalhana (2449) | 3138–2449 BCE |\n| Puranic genealogy | Pargiter (950), Majumdar (about 1000), Pradhan and the 1015/1050 interval (about 1424), Matsya 1500 variant (about 1900), Jones and Wilford | 1900–950 BCE |\n| Archaeological | Lal (Hastinapura, about 900), S. R. Rao (Dwarka, about 1500), Sinauli (2200–1800) | 2200–800 BCE |\n| Philological | Witzel, Weber, Jataka evidence, Winternitz, Hopkins, Brockington, Fitzgerald, Bühler | Text 400 BCE–400 CE; kernel about 1000 BCE |\n\n**Why the spread is so wide.** The proposals answer different questions. Some date the event, some the text, some a calendar epoch, and some are mythic intervals. They also use different evidence, and the Puranic king-lists, the epic's sky descriptions and the Kali epoch contradict each other. A few entries are sketchy attributions. Examples are Ravi Prakash Arya, Vedveer Arya, the unverified Manish Pandit documentary, and the Sengupta 1298/1198 BCE claim. Sengupta's documented date is 2449 BCE.\n\n### 2. Event versus text\n\nThese are separate questions and should be kept apart.\n\n- **Text.** The best-supported statement in the whole set is Bühler's lower bound. Panini and Ashvalayana show some Bharata/Mahabharata tradition by the 5th–4th century BCE. The Sankheda grant of 532/533 CE shows a 100,000-verse text. Winternitz, Hopkins, Brockington and others place growth at about 400 BCE–400 CE, with Fitzgerald and Hiltebeitel arguing for narrower windows. Sukthankar's Critical Edition reconstructs an archetype, not an original. No proposal gives a c. 3100 BCE text. The language is Epic Sanskrit, later than Vedic, and there is no writing from that period except the undeciphered Indus script.\n- **Event.** If a Kuru war happened, it is inferred. The Vedic texts mention Parikshit and Janamejaya but not the war itself. The Pali Jatakas show a Kuru dynasty of the \"Yudhitthila\" lineage that was already declining by the Buddha's time. That gives a relative terminus ante quem only.\n\nDating the text does not date the war, and the reverse is also true. Traditionalists who criticise the late text date are partly right on this point. The text date is silent on the war.\n\n### 3. The planetarium approach, critically\n\nThe approach is attractive because it is testable and uses real astronomy. It still has serious problems.\n\n1. **Verse selection.** The epic contains hundreds of sky references. Oak keeps those he judges observational and consistent; others use different subsets. Each researcher's selection yields a unique fit, so uniqueness follows from the selection and is not independent evidence.\n2. **Contradictory references.** Omens, such as Arundhati ahead of Vasishtha and the Moon/Krittika/Rohini/Saturn configurations, belong to poetic or portent vocabulary. They can be internally inconsistent across parvas. Reading the Mizar–Alcor \"Arundhati\" pair as a naked-eye observation is one interpretation among several.\n3. **Interpolation.** The epic is layered, so sky verses could be late, borrowed from astrological tradition or added for effect. A date derived from late verses dates the verses.\n4. **Ambiguous vocabulary.** Nakshatra names, \"graha\" and eclipse visibility, and what counts as a \"conjunction,\" all vary in interpretation. Software gives accurate positions but cannot adjudicate Sanskrit meaning.\n5. **Epoch circularity.** I-SERVE and Achar reject 3138 BCE, but the Kali epoch still frames the search window. The Kali 3102 BCE \"all planets in conjunction\" is a back-computed mean-planet epoch. Bailly thought it was observed, and Davis, Playfair and Bentley showed it could be generated by back-calculation. The 36-year gap to the war comes from the Mausala narrative and is not an astronomical datum.\n6. **Multiple solutions.** Different workers get 5561, 3067, 1478 and 1197 BCE (Koch's 1198 BCE eclipse-based date adds another) from the same text. If the method produced reliable answers, they would converge. They do not.\n\n**Fairness.** Oak, Achar and I-SERVE are not frauds. They offer falsifiable hypotheses, and the sky data deserve study. But the method needs a pre-registered verse selection, which is not what is on offer, and the results have little peer review among Sanskritists.\n\n### 4. Archaeological record\n\n- **Harappan (about 2600–1900 BCE).** Any war before 1900 BCE falls in a bronze-age urban civilisation with no Kuru state, no epic-style chariot warfare and no iron. 5561 BCE is Neolithic and pre-urban. Mehrgarh-type Neolithic cultures do not match the epic's world at all.\n- **Late Harappan and Copper Hoard.** The Sinauli finds (2018 season) are about 2200–1800 BCE: coffin burials, antennae swords and solid-wheeled carts. They are not spoked-wheel horse chariots, and the epic link is media framing. Several archaeologists caution against it. It shows that a warrior-burial culture existed in the doab at that time and says nothing about the epic.\n- **Painted Grey Ware (about 1200–600 BCE).** This is the best fit for the Kuru state of the Late Vedic texts (Witzel: about 1200–900 BCE). Iron appears in the doab about 1000–800 BCE. Hastinapura Period II (PGW) is followed by a flood layer. B. B. Lal tied the flood to the Puranic account of Nichakshu moving the capital to Kausambi, giving about 900 BCE for the war. The caveat is that the flood layer postdates PGW, so it constrains the end of the settlement, not the war.\n- **Ayodhya/Hastinapur.** Neither site shows a clear urban phase at 3000 BCE or earlier. The Ayodhya levels belong to Ramayana debates, not this one.\n- **Dwarka.** S. R. Rao argued for about 1500 BCE. Later NIO work found occupation from the late 2nd millennium BCE onward, with many structures historical or medieval. Sea level and coastal change complicate the picture. Identifying any submerged structure with Krishna's city is an inference. (Ram Setu geology bears on the Ramayana, not the Mahabharata.)\n\nArchaeology therefore favours a date near 1000–850 BCE for a Kuru kernel. It cannot verify a single battle.\n\n### 5. Citation lineage\n\n- Bailly (1787) → the \"real observation\" reading of the Kali epoch → Davis, Playfair and Bentley's rebuttals; Bentley is also a source for a very late war.\n- Aryabhata (499 CE; Kali 3600 elapsed, which fixes the epoch but not the war) → Aihole (634 CE: 3735 years since the war) → Vaidya (1904), Abul Fazl, Dayananda, Prabhupada, Frawley, Rajaram, Sadhguru, Bhagwad Datta and Arun Upadhyay.\n- Vriddha Garga → Varahamihira (2526 years before Saka, about 2449 BCE) → Bhattotpala → Kalhana (Kali 653) → P. C. Sengupta; Stein criticised Kalhana's chronology.\n- Puranic lists → Wilson and Cunningham (short interval, 1400s BCE) → Pradhan; Pargiter (about 950 BCE) → Raychaudhuri, Pusalker, Basham and Majumdar.\n- Colebrooke (Vedanga Jyotisha colure, about 1391 BCE) → Wilson and Elphinstone. Note that Colebrooke dated the Vedic calendar, not the war. Tilak's Vedanga date is closer to 1181–1121 BCE.\n- Vartak → Oak (5561 BCE; Oak differs in method and presents it as confirmation). Oak is also set against Achar and I-SERVE.\n- K. Srinivasa Raghavan → Achar (3067 BCE). Bhatnagar and Saroj Bala (I-SERVE) → Planetarium Gold and the Ramayana series with Kulbhushan Mishra. Their documented date is 3067 BCE; the \"3139 BCE\" attribution is probably a conflation with the traditional 3138.\n\n### 6. Corrections to the compiled data\n\n- Sengupta's date is 2449 BCE. The 1298/1198 BCE attribution should be dropped.\n- Saroj Bala/I-SERVE's date is 3067 BCE. The 3139 BCE attribution is unsupported.\n- Dikshit (1853–1898) and Bankim (1838–1894) figures are unverified recalls and could be wrong by decades.\n- Vartak's day is more often quoted as 16 Oct 5561 BCE. Oak's date is unconfirmed.\n- Colebrooke computed an astronomical datum for the Vedas, not a war date.\n- Jinasena gives no war date. The figure is a derived interval.\n- Several scores in the compiled set look inflated for entries rated only by \"mainstream\" status. Bühler (9/10) and the philological ranges rate high because they are well-supported but answer a different question.\n\n### 7. Ranked table (main proposals)\n\n| Date | Proposer | Method | Score /10 | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Text 400 BCE–400 CE; floor 532 CE | Bühler, Winternitz, Hopkins, Brockington | Textual | 9 | Externally anchored; dates the text, not the war |\n| Kernel about 1000–850 BCE | Witzel, Thapar, Upinder Singh | Philology plus archaeology | 8 | Fits Kuru state, PGW and iron; the war itself unattested |\n| About 950 BCE | Pargiter, Raychaudhuri, Majumdar | Genealogy | 7 | Anchored to Nanda/Maurya; generation lengths assumed |\n| About 900 BCE | B. B. Lal | Archaeology plus Puranas | 7 | Hastinapura flood; dates the settlement's end |\n| Before the Buddha (about 500 BCE bound) | Jataka evidence | Textual | 7 | Sound but only a bound |\n| About 1424 BCE | Pradhan, Wilson, Cunningham | Genealogy | 5 | Depends on chosen interval variant |\n| About 1500 BCE | S. R. Rao | Archaeology | 4 | Dwarka identification contested |\n| 1478 / 1197 BCE | Chandra Hari, Iyengar, Daftari | Astronomy | 4 | Unverified; sensitive to verse choice |\n| 3067 BCE | Achar, I-SERVE | Planetarium | 3 | Selective verses; no archaeological match |\n| 3138 / 3102 BCE | Kali tradition, Aihole, Vaidya | Epoch | 3 | Back-computed epoch; no record |\n| 2449 BCE | Varahamihira, Kalhana, Sengupta | Epoch | 3 | Early attested alternative; Saptarshi convention |\n| 5561 BCE | Vartak, Oak | Planetarium | 3 | Neolithic context; verse-selection dependent |\n| About -85000; about 6400 BCE | Jinasena; Megasthenes readings | Cosmology; Greek reports | 1–2 | Schematic or inferred, no usable date |\n\n### Bottom line\n\nThe most defensible position has two parts. The epic text took shape between about 400 BCE and 400 CE on an older oral base. If a historical Kuru conflict lies behind it, it was probably in the early first millennium BCE, around 1000–850 BCE. This is supported by archaeology and by Vedic and Pali evidence, and it rests on no single decisive datum. The astronomical and traditional dates remain legitimate hypotheses worth testing. They cannot yet be ranked above the mainstream range, because they depend on contested verse selection or on a retrospective calendar epoch and conflict with the material record.","Ramayana":"## Which Ramayana date is most defensible?\n\n**Short answer.** The 65 proposals answer at least four different questions. Some date the *text*, some the *event*, some a *site* or *geological feature*, and some a *retelling*. Only the textual and archaeological answers are tested against independent evidence. No proposal dates the *event* in a way that survives independent checking.\n\n### 1. Grouping by method\n\n**A. Puranic and yuga arithmetic (about 870,000 to 18 million years ago).** This group includes the 24th-Treta count (about 18.1 million years), the \"8+ lakh\" figure, Prabhupada's \"millions of years,\" and the viral \"NASA 1.75 million years\" claim. The Jain placement under Munisuvrata (over a million years before Mahavira) belongs here in kind. These are scriptural cycle counts. The Valmiki Ramayana contains no yuga year count.\n\nTwo corrections to the compiled notes:\n- The 1.8 crore figure is arithmetic: 1.296M (rest of the 24th mahayuga) + 12.96M (three full mahayugas) + 3.888M (Krita, Treta and Dvapara of the 28th) ≈ 18.14M.\n- Prabhupada's \"about 2 million\" cannot be derived from the 24th Treta, so the sources conflict internally.\n\nThe NASA claim is not a dating at all. It is the Treta figure restated, and NASA has not dated the feature or called it man-made.\n\n**B. Astronomical matching (about 12,200 to 5100 BCE).** Proposers and dates:\n- Vartak: 7323 BCE.\n- Bhatnagar: 7323 BCE, which I believe is his own figure. The compiled entries contradict each other on this point, and one even calls the Bhatnagar attribution an error.\n- Saroj Bala and Kulbhushan Mishra (I-SERVE): 10 Jan 5114 BCE.\n- Nilesh Oak: about 12,209 BCE. This is unverified here, and one entry's \"7000 BCE\" is probably wrong.\n- Popularisers who repeat 5114 BCE: Vedveer Arya, Ami Ganatra, Project Shivoham, Jayasree Saranathan.\n\n**C. Genealogical counting (about 4000 to 1500 BCE).** Proposers:\n- Jones: about 2029 BCE.\n- Pargiter: the Bharata war at about 950 BCE, with Rama about 30 generations earlier.\n- Pusalker, C.V. Vaidya and Bhagavad Datta: about 2000 BCE.\n- Kota Venkatachalam: war at 1194 BCE.\n- Upadhyay: several thousand BCE.\n\nResults depend on two free parameters: the war date and years per generation. Pargiter's own numbers imply about 1500-1700 BCE, so the often-circulated \"2350 BCE\" is doubtful. The Dikshit entry has no Rama date and should be dropped.\n\n**D. Archaeology.** Sources and findings:\n- Lal: Ayodhya Period I at about 700 BCE; Shringaverapura at about 1000 BCE.\n- ASI 2003 (Mani): lowest levels about 1300-1000 BCE (disputed; the 2003 radiocarbon figures are recalled from memory).\n- Sankalia: a hedged kernel at about 1500-1000 BCE.\n- Cunningham: Ayodhya identified with Saketa.\n- Bakker: the Rama cult at Ayodhya is Gupta-era.\n\nThese date sites, not Rama.\n\n**E. Textual and linguistic.** Sources and findings:\n- Jacobi, Goldman, Brockington, Pollock, Thapar and Basham put the Books 2-6 core at roughly the 7th-4th century BCE. Books 1 and 7 are later, with the text stable by about the 2nd-3rd century CE.\n- Lassen, Weber and Hopkins are older or contested views.\n- Terminus-ante-quem witnesses: Patanjali, Bhasa, Ashvaghosha (Buddhacarita 1.43), the Liudu ji jing (251 CE), Vimalasuri's colophon (about 3-63 CE), Kalidasa, Bhatti, Bhavabhuti, Svayambhu, the Kakawin (about 850 CE), Kamban and Tulsidas (1574 CE).\n\n**F. Geology.** The Palk Strait land bridge dates to the Last Glacial Maximum, and the final severing came in the early to mid Holocene, about 10,000-7,000 years ago. The shoals of Adam's Bridge are a few thousand years old. Neither dates Rama, and the \"18,000 years\" entry is a baseline, not a proposal.\n\n### 2. Why the spread is so wide\n\nThe spread comes from different objects of dating, not different measurements of one object:\n- **Different targets.** Tulsidas (1574 CE) and the 18.1-million-year count both appear in the list, yet one dates a retelling and the other a cosmology.\n- **Free parameters.** Genealogies need a war anchor and a generation length. Astronomy needs a verse selection and a sign convention.\n- **Different authorities.** Puranic numbers are theological, philological dating is relative, and archaeology is stratigraphic.\n- **Whether the event is historical at all.** Only the textual and archaeological methods can work even if Rama is not.\n\n### 3. Event versus text\n\nThis distinction matters most. The scholars in group E date the *poem*. They do not claim Rama never lived, and traditionalists are right that composition and event dates can differ greatly for an oral epic. But oral tradition does not guarantee accurate dating over millennia. A late text with an early kernel needs an independent anchor, and none exists. Many \"refutations\" in the compiled notes therefore compare unlike things. A 5th-century-BCE text date does not contradict a 5114 BCE event, because they answer different questions. They are in tension only through the *plausibility* of a six-millennium-long faithful oral transmission of sky data.\n\n### 4. Critique of the planetarium approach\n\n- **Verse selection.** Bala Kanda 1.18.8-9 is the key passage. It lists five planets exalted, with the Sun in Mesha, plus Punarvasu and Karka lagna. The Bala Kanda is, on the philological consensus, a *late* book (about the 1st-3rd century CE). The Ramayana's astrology may reflect the astrological conventions of its composition period, not observations of events thousands of years earlier. Exaltation schemes (ucca) are a Greek-influenced, later system.\n- **Contradictions inside the epic.** Different passages (exile-era sky descriptions, omens, Hanuman's and Ravana's chapters) yield different dates, and each proposer takes a subset. Oak and Bala disagree by about 7,000 years using the same family of method, so the match is demonstrably not unique.\n- **Interpolation.** The Baroda Critical Edition identifies variants, and the verses are exactly the kind that get added. Without stable text, \"the verses\" are a choice.\n- **Sign conventions.** Sidereal versus tropical results shift by thousands of years of precession. The January date in 5114 BCE for Chaitra Navami (a spring event) has to be explained by a shifted month-season link, which is a patch, not a prediction.\n- **Circularity with the Kali epoch.** The Kali start (3102 BCE) comes from siddhantic retro-calculation with a fabricated \"conjunction.\" Anchoring genealogies or war dates to it imports a back-computed number as if it were an observation. Several Ramayana dates inherit this.\n- **Software is not independent.** Planetarium Gold, Stellarium and Skyglobe compute positions well. The weak link is the textual input, not the arithmetic.\n\n### 5. Citation lineage\n\n- **Vartak → Oak.** Oak builds on Vartak's Mahabharata dating (Swayambhu) and extends the method to the Ramayana, getting a much earlier answer.\n- **Vartak and Bhatnagar → I-SERVE.** Bala and Mishra use the same verses and a different result (5114 BCE).\n- **Bala → Arya → Ganatra, Shivoham, Saranathan.** These are *not independent corroboration*. Counting them as separate votes inflates 5114 BCE.\n- **Pargiter → Pusalker, Vaidya, Datta.** All use king-list arithmetic.\n- **Jacobi → Goldman, Brockington, Pollock, Thapar, Basham.** This is the mainstream line. Weber's Jataka-priority view is the minority line (Weber → Dinesh Chandra Sen → Bulcke, partly), and it was largely rejected.\n- **Vimalasuri → Ravishena → Svayambhu, Hemachandra.** The Jain tradition derives its dates from the Tirthankara scheme.\n- **Lal → ASI 2003.** Both are used rhetorically in Ayodhya-dispute debates, which affects how some are received.\n\n### 6. Archaeological context\n\n- **Harappan (about 2600-1900 BCE).** Urban but not Ayodhya-based, and with no iron, horses in the Vedic sense, or the epic's chariot-and-kingdom picture. A Rama at 5114 or 7323 BCE would precede it by thousands of years, in a period of Neolithic villages in the Gangetic plain.\n- **Painted Grey Ware (about 1200-600 BCE).** This is the horizon linked with Kuru-Panchala and Hastinapur (Lal dated it to about 900 BCE). It fits the Mahabharata far better than any Ramayana date.\n- **Ayodhya.** The earliest dated habitation is about 1300-700 BCE depending on the report, with real urbanism under NBPW (from about 700 BCE). Lal's own view was that excavation covered a small area, which is a fair caveat, but an argument from absence works against everyone.\n- **Iron Age.** Iron appears in the Ganga plain from about 1000-800 BCE. The epic's metal weapons and fortified cities suit that or later horizons.\n- **Ram Setu.** The shoals are natural shoals of a few thousand years of age. This does not exclude a built structure, but it does not date one either. The 2022 parliamentary answer left the question open.\n- **Palaeolithic range.** The 870,000-year and 18-million-year figures predate Homo sapiens (about 300,000 years) or, for 18 million, Homo itself.\n\n### 7. Ranked table\n\nScores reflect defensibility as an answer to *its own question*, with a penalty for unfalsifiability.\n\n| Date | Proposer | Method | Score /10 | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Core c. 7th-4th c. BCE; final c. 2nd-3rd c. CE | Goldman, Brockington, Jacobi, Pollock, Thapar | Textual / linguistic | 8 | Convergent, cross-checkable; dates the text only |\n| Terminus ante quem c. 150 BCE to 4th c. CE | Patanjali, Bhasa, Ashvaghosha, Kalidasa | Literary witnesses | 7-8 | Firm but loose lower bounds |\n| Occupation c. 1000-700 BCE onward | B.B. Lal, ASI | Archaeological | 6-7 | Real stratigraphy; dates sites, not Rama |\n| Kernel c. 1500-1000 BCE | Sankalia | Archaeological inference | 5-6 | Plausible, hedged, not demonstrable |\n| c. 2000 BCE or later | Pargiter, Pusalker, Jones | Genealogy | 3-4 | Parameter-dependent, war anchor uncertain |\n| 7323 BCE | Vartak, Bhatnagar | Astronomical | 2 | Non-unique; late verse; no matching archaeology |\n| 5114 BCE | Bala, Mishra (I-SERVE), plus followers | Astronomical | 2 | Season mismatch; derivative followers are not independent |\n| c. 12,209 BCE (unverified) | Oak | Astronomical | 2 | Late Pleistocene; contradicts Vartak and Bala |\n| About 870,000 years ago | Popular Puranic | Yuga arithmetic | 1 | Theological, no empirical check |\n| About 1.75 to 18 million years ago | Puranic, ISKCON, viral \"NASA\" | Yuga arithmetic / rumour | 1 | Precedes humans |\n\n### 8. Bottom line\n\nThe most defensible answers are those that fit what evidence exists. The Ramayana *text* took shape from about the 7th-4th century BCE, with later layers. The *setting* the archaeology supports is the early-first-millennium Gangetic world, with Sankalia-style kernels of about 1500-1000 BCE possible but unprovable. No claim that Rama lived before about 2000 BCE has independent support. The astronomical dates deserve fair credit as serious attempts to treat the text as data, but their disagreement with one another (roughly 12,200, 7,300 and 5,100 BCE) shows the method does not yet discriminate. The yuga figures are not dating claims. The most honest statement is that the *event*, if historical, cannot currently be dated. The *text* can.\n\n**Caveat on sources.** Most entries were written from memory and flagged as unverified, so the exact titles, years and quoted figures above (Oak's 12,209 BCE, Pargiter's 2350 BCE, the Bhatnagar/Vartak assignment) need checking before publication."},"generated":"2026-10-20"};
